<#
.SYNOPSIS
    Near-enterprise-grade Active Directory forest assessment with two-way trust health.

.DESCRIPTION
    Invoke-ADForestAssessment is an enhanced successor to Invoke-CoreInfraAssessment.ps1.
    It keeps that script's robust engine (per-DC reachability gating, external-tool
    timeout/retry wrapper, StrictMode, honest "Not Assessed" status, CSV-per-topic + HTML)
    and adds full-forest coverage:

        - Forest / domain functional levels and FSMO roles, and an inventory of the raw levels
          (msDS-Behavior-Version), the five FSMO holders (fSMORoleOwner), UPN and allowed DNS
          suffixes, each DC's DNS suffix (disjoint namespace, not graded) and the runner DC
        - Domain controller inventory (OS / GC / site / IP), and each DC's DNS client settings:
          the DNS server list of its enabled adapters (Win32_NetworkAdapterConfiguration, remote
          CIM, needs local admin on the DC), with a single DNS server flagged where more than one
          DC is known (Microsoft: a single point of failure); the rest is inventory
        - Replication health (partner metadata, failures, queue) and topology
          (sites, subnets, site links, connection objects)
        - FOREST & DOMAIN TRUSTS with verified TWO-WAY trust health
          (secure-channel verification per direction via nltest/netdom, plus SID filtering,
          selective authentication, TGT delegation and encryption posture)
        - Parsed DC diagnostics (dcdiag key tests + core service state) as PASS/FAIL per DC,
          with Topology per domain, CheckSDRefDom and CrossRefValidation per partition of the
          forest, and Intersite per site (opt-in); a partition's or the enterprise's result is
          never counted as a DC's. With the opt-in, the DNS test's summary table graded per test
          group (Auth, Basc, Forw, Del, Dyn, RReg, Ext), per DC and per domain
        - DNS health, per DNS server and per zone over the DNS servers of each domain: zone
          dynamic update (Nonsecure and secure on an AD-integrated zone fails; Microsoft
          recommends Secure only), zone aging and server scavenging (tool defaults; Microsoft
          publishes no requirement), a zone configuration inventory, forwarders and zone
          transfer. Get-DnsServer* reads only                              [DnsServer optional]
        - SYSVOL / DFSR replication migration state                          [DFSR optional]
        - GPO inventory (linked / unlinked)                                  [GroupPolicy optional]
        - GPO directory inventory, without the GroupPolicy module: status flags, WMI filter and
          links of each GPO, links per site, domain and OU (inventory only)
        - GPO version consistency, per scoped domain: each GPO's version in the directory
          (versionNumber) against the version in its GPT.INI in SYSVOL, user and computer
          halves, both read from the domain's PDC emulator; GPOs whose SYSVOL folder or GPT.INI
          is missing, and SYSVOL GPO folders with no container. A directory read and a
          read-only file read over SMB; the GroupPolicy module is not needed for it and nothing
          is written
        - Password & lockout policy (default + fine-grained)
        - Privileged groups, per scoped domain: Schema Admins and Enterprise Admins membership
          (forest root; a member is a Warning, as Microsoft says both should be empty day to
          day), and the transitive member count of every privileged group, each located by SID
          and never by name, with nested groups, primary-group members and foreign security
          principals (by SID) followed; a member that could not be resolved makes the count Not
          Assessed, never lower
        - Security posture
          (krbtgt password age, AD Recycle Bin, tombstone lifetime, machine account quota,
          stale / never-expiring / RC4-DES accounts; the permissions of AdminSDHolder are in
          the Dangerous ACLs section)
        - SID history, per scoped domain (rows of the security posture section): how many
          security principals carry sIDHistory, whether a historic SID belongs to the
          principal's own domain or is a privileged well-known SID, and which domains the
          historic SIDs came from. Directory reads only; sIDHistory is never written. An empty
          answer is "none visible to this account", because a read returns no attribute the
          account may not see
        - Stale user / computer objects, and stale disabled ones (inventory only)
        - Object counts per domain (users, computers, groups, contacts, OUs, gMSA, sMSA; enabled
          and disabled) and a group inventory (scope, type, empty groups, direct member counts),
          from capped reads with listed properties
        - User & computer export (CSV; HTML shows a summary) with a DECLARED attribute list per
          object kind (the DirectoryRead table), never every attribute: since v1.33.0 no read of
          this script asks the directory for every attribute, and none names a password, password
          hash, LAPS password, trust secret or other secret attribute. The CSV columns are the
          declared list plus Enabled and ListComplete; a capped read marks every row "No"
        - AD CS / PKI: enrolment CAs and ESC1-susceptible certificate templates
        - Dangerous ACLs, per scoped domain: principals outside an allowlist, matched by SID and
          never by name, that hold a replication (DCSync) right, WriteDacl, WriteOwner, write of
          all properties or full control on the domain head, or a write or control right or
          ownership on AdminSDHolder or on any GPO. Directory reads of nTSecurityDescriptor only;
          the descriptor is read in memory and nothing is written. The allowlist is Microsoft's
          published default holders plus labelled tool defaults, and is provisional
        - Kerberos exposure: Kerberoastable (SPN) users, AS-REP-roastable accounts, and per
          scoped domain unconstrained delegation and protocol transition outside the domain
          controllers (excluded by the DN of their computer account, never by name), Kerberos-only
          constrained delegation (inventory) and resource-based constrained delegation with the
          SIDs it allows
        - Privileged hygiene, per scoped domain: adminCount=1 objects outside every protected
          group ("orphan" is this tool's word for what Microsoft calls formerly protected),
          enabled privileged user accounts with an SPN, privileged accounts that can be delegated,
          and the Protected Users member count
        - DC hardening, per DC: LDAP server signing and its Windows Server 2025 enforcement,
          LDAP channel binding, SMB server and client signing, the LAN Manager authentication
          level (LmCompatibilityLevel), the Print Spooler service and the SMBv1 server. Six
          registry values, two CIM classes and the SMB server configuration in one session
          per DC (remote CIM, needs local admin on the DC). Each value is graded against the
          value Microsoft's security baselines expect on a domain controller; a value that is
          not set is graded on the default Microsoft documents for the DC's OS build where it
          documents one, and is Not Assessed where it does not. These are registry reads: they
          show the policy configured on each DC, not what a client and the DC negotiate on the
          wire, and no LDAP bind or SMB session is attempted. The SMBv1 row and the second
          instrument of SMB server signing are read with Get-SmbServerConfiguration, the read
          cmdlet Microsoft documents, run in the same session (PORT-PLAN C3f); the SMBv1 row is
          a Pass only where the DC returned EnableSMB1Protocol False
        - DC system: operating system past end of extended support, judged per DC from the
          directory against a table of Microsoft lifecycle dates; and per-DC inventory of OS
          build, uptime and latest update install date (remote CIM, needs local admin on the
          DC; reported as Info or Not Assessed, never graded)
        - DC disk space: free space on the volumes that hold the NTDS database, the NTDS
          logs and SYSVOL, per DC. The three paths are read from each DC's registry and the
          volumes from Win32_Volume (remote CIM, needs local admin on the DC). Judged against
          thresholds that are tool defaults, not Microsoft values, and are labelled as such
        - Windows LAPS: whether the forest schema carries the Windows LAPS attributes (and
          the legacy Microsoft LAPS ones); per domain controller, whether a DSRM password
          backup is visible; and per scoped domain, how many enabled Windows computers have
          no LAPS expiry time visible. Directory reads only, with domain read access. This
          section never reads a password: the attributes that hold a LAPS password, an
          encrypted password or a password history are never requested by it, and presence
          is judged from the two expiry time attributes alone. (The Identity export above is
          the one place in the tool that can still be handed them by the directory; it does
          not write them out.) A value the account has no right to read
          looks like a value that is not set, so a row says "no LAPS expiry time visible to
          this account" and never calls a computer unmanaged with certainty; where nothing
          at all is visible in a domain the rows are Not Assessed. A device that backs its
          password up to Microsoft Entra ID is not visible to this read. The policy in each
          DC's registry is not read (PORT-PLAN C4a)
        - DC certificates, per DC: the certificates a CA published on the DC's computer object
          (userCertificate, a directory read with domain read access), and - only with
          -IncludeLdapsHandshake - the certificate the DC presents on LDAPS port 636, read with
          a TLS handshake that sends no LDAP request, no bind and no credential and needs no
          right on the DC. Each is judged on expiry against windows that are tool defaults, not
          Microsoft values; trust, name and EKUs are reported, not graded. No published value is
          Info, never "no certificate": Microsoft's Domain Controller Authentication and
          Kerberos Authentication templates do not publish to the directory. The DC's own
          certificate stores are not read (PORT-PLAN C5a)
        - Directory backup status (repadmin /showbackup), time synchronization (w32tm), and each
          DC's W32Time Type, NtpServer and AnnounceFlags from the registry (inventory only)
        - Event inventory per DC: Critical, Error and Warning events of five logs, bounded by a
          time window and a per-log cap, grouped by event ID (inventory only)
        - DNS depth: critical SRV records (secure dynamic updates moved to DNS health in v1.30.0)
        - Redundancy: DC count, GPO central store, duplicate SPNs
        - RECOVERY & CONSISTENCY (post-incident, e.g. after a restore-from-backup):
            * DNS vs AD divergence - DC locator SRV records compared against the DCs the
              directory actually contains (stale entries for removed DCs, live DCs not
              advertised), and per DC: whether each writable DC is named by its DC locator SRV
              records on every DNS server, by two instruments (the server's zone and a query to
              it), whether its A records are the addresses its adapters hold, and the locator
              records that name no DC
            * DSA GUID CNAME records in _msdcs - the usual cause of RPC 1722 replication
              failures after metadata cleanup or restore
            * Global Catalog flag vs _gc._tcp DNS advertisement
            * Per-DC reachability class, decided once per run (name resolution, LDAP 389,
              WinRM 5985, RPC 135), and the reachability matrix on the ports replication
              requires (Kerberos/RPC/LDAP/SMB + LDAPS/GC/ADWS)
            * DC machine-account password age (replicated attribute, collected centrally)
              and per-DC secure-channel verification over WinRM where reachable
            * Directory Service event log scan per DC for the events that block or mask
              recovery: lingering objects (1988), tombstone-lifetime exceeded (2042),
              USN rollback (2095), unsupported restore (2103), GUID DNS lookup failures
              (2087/2088), KCC topology failures (1311/1865/1925)
        - Exchange schema markers
        - Optional raw repadmin / dcdiag capture

    Every Fail / Warning / Broken / Degraded row in the consolidated findings carries a
    best-practice remediation recommendation (Recommendation column in
    csv\Findings-Consolidated.csv, the detailed log, and the HTML lead section).

    The report is written to the logged-on user's Documents folder by default:
        %USERPROFILE%\Documents\AdAssessment\yyyy-MM-dd_HH-mm-ss\

    Coverage-aware & fail-closed: any value that could not be collected is reported as
    "Not Assessed" (never a false Pass / 0). Trust directions that could not be tested from
    the local side are reported "Not Assessed", never "Verified".

    The script writes nothing to the directory itself. Every command that Microsoft documents
    as changing something, and that this project knows of, is off unless its switch is given:

      -IncludeSecureChannelVerify  nltest /sc_verify (netdom trust /Verify as fallback);
                                   /sc_verify removes and rebuilds a secure channel that is
                                   not working.
      -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests    dcdiag /test:DNS /DnsAll, whose dynamic update test
                                   registers and then deletes a DNS test record;
                                   /test:SystemLog, which Microsoft describes as "reading and
                                   writing entries"; and /test:Intersite /a, which triggers a
                                   replication (run once per site that holds a DC).
      -IncludeDcdiag               raw 'dcdiag /c /v'; /c runs all tests except DCPromo and
                                   RegisterInDNS, so it includes CutoffServers (triggers
                                   replication) and the DNS test above, independently of
                                   -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests.
      -IncludeLingeringObjectScan  repadmin /removelingeringobjects in advisory mode, which
                                   removes nothing but writes events on the target DCs.
      -IncludeLdapsHandshake       a TLS handshake to port 636 of each DC. It writes nothing,
                                   but Microsoft does not state that a handshake leaves no
                                   trace, and Schannel logs errors by default, so a DC that
                                   cannot complete it may log a Schannel error event.

    That is a list of what is KNOWN. The dcdiag tests a default run invokes were each checked
    against Microsoft's description of them in the dcdiag reference (PORT-PLAN C11, 2026-11-16):
    none is described as writing or replicating, Topology is documented as analysing "without
    actually replicating changes", and RidManager is described as checks only (it is accessible,
    holds the proper information, can be contacted, has valid RID pool values) - Learn does not
    say in so many words that it never requests a pool. FsmoCheck is not in that reference at all
    (PORT-PLAN C11c). The other commands a default run invokes (repadmin, w32tm, nltest /sc_query
    and /dsgetdc, dfsrmig, nslookup, and remote reads over WinRM, CIM, the remote registry, the
    DNS cmdlets and the event log) are used as queries, but have not each been audited for a
    documented side effect. PORT-PLAN H20a.

    Two sections make CIM METHOD calls, and they are the only ones in the script. Both read
    the registry of each DC through the WMI registry provider, StdRegProv, which exposes no
    instances and can be read only by calling a method. DcDisk reads three values with
    GetStringValue and, where that gives nothing usable, GetExpandedStringValue - the two
    methods Microsoft documents as getting a string value. DcHardening reads six values with
    GetDWORDValue, which Microsoft documents as returning the data of a REG_DWORD value. Each
    of the three is named literally in the source, and no other method is called. StdRegProv's
    writing methods (Create*, Delete*, Set*) are never called. Microsoft does not state in so
    many words that the Get* methods have no side effect; the decision rests on their
    documented purpose and signature.

.PARAMETER OutputPath
    Root folder for the assessment bundle. A timestamped sub-folder is always created inside it.
    Default: <MyDocuments>\AdAssessment

.PARAMETER AllDomains
    Assess every domain in the forest. Without it, only the current/target domain is assessed
    (forest-wide sections such as trusts and topology are still collected).

.PARAMETER Sections
    Restrict collection to the named sections. Default: All. 'Identity' performs a full
    all-attributes export of every user and computer (heavy on large domains) to CSV; use
    -Sections to exclude it for a quick health-only run. Cross-forest note: -AllDomains covers
    domains WITHIN the target forest only. To assess another forest, run again with -Server /
    -Credential pointed at a DC in that forest.

    Recovery sections (post-incident): DnsAdConsistency, DsaCname, GcConsistency, PortMatrix,
    DcSecureChannel, DsEvents. For a focused post-restore triage run:
    -Sections DnsAdConsistency,DsaCname,GcConsistency,PortMatrix,Replication,Trusts,DcSecureChannel,DsEvents,TimeSync,Sysvol,Backup

    DcSystem judges each DC's operating system against Microsoft's lifecycle dates and lists
    its build, uptime and latest update install date. The lifecycle verdict reads the DC
    inventory, so name DomainControllers with it: -Sections DomainControllers,DcSystem

    DcDisk judges the free space on each DC's NTDS database, NTDS log and SYSVOL volumes. It
    reads each DC over remote CIM and needs local Administrators rights there. It covers the
    DCs of every scoped domain, each row carrying its domain: from the DC inventory when
    DomainControllers is also selected, and otherwise from its own enumeration of each scoped
    domain. A scoped domain whose DCs could not be enumerated gets a Not Assessed row of its
    own; it is never left out.

    DcHardening reads six registry values, two CIM classes and, with Get-SmbServerConfiguration
    (module SmbShare, needed on the computer running the assessment), the SMB server
    configuration of each DC, over remote CIM, and needs local Administrators rights there. Like DcDisk it covers the DCs of every scoped
    domain, from the DC inventory or from its own enumeration, and gives a scoped domain with
    no DC row a Not Assessed row of its own. Its rows show configured policy, not what is
    negotiated on the wire.

    Laps reads the directory only and needs domain read access: the forest schema once, and
    for every scoped domain its functional level and its computer objects. It gives the
    forest one schema row, every scoped domain one "LAPS coverage" row, and every domain
    controller one "DSRM password backup" row; a scoped domain that could not be read gets
    Not Assessed rows naming the cause. It never requests a password attribute. The
    population the coverage row grades is stated in the row, and uses -StaleDays.

    DcCertificates covers the DCs of every scoped domain, as DcDisk does. For each DC it reads
    the userCertificate attribute of the DC's computer object (domain read access) and gives
    one row per published certificate, graded on expiry, or one Info row when none is visible.
    With -IncludeLdapsHandshake it also gives each DC one "LDAPS certificate" row for the
    certificate the DC presents on port 636; without it that row is Not Assessed and says the
    handshake was not run.

    RidPool reads the directory only and needs domain read access. For every scoped domain it
    reads rIDAvailablePool and msDS-RIDPoolAllocationEnabled on CN=RID Manager$,CN=System and
    gives two rows: whether RID pool allocation is blocked (FALSE is a Fail, TRUE a Pass, and an
    attribute that is not returned Not Assessed, because an unset value and one this account
    cannot read look the same) and how much of the
    global RID space has been issued, against the size read from the attribute itself (Warning
    from 10 % used, Fail from 90 % used: Microsoft's event 16658 milestone and RID ceiling). For
    every domain controller of every scoped domain, as DcDisk does, it reads the RID Set object
    that rIDSetReferences names and reports the current pool, the next RID and the prefetched
    pool as Info. A missing RID Set, a refused read and an unreachable server are each their own
    Not Assessed result. It runs no dcdiag test and never reads or writes SidCompatibilityVersion.

    SecurityPosture also gives every scoped domain four SID history rows, from a directory read
    with domain read access: one search for (sIDHistory=*) against the domain, asking for
    sIDHistory, objectSid, objectClass, sAMAccountName, distinguishedName and userAccountControl
    only; the trustedDomain objects under CN=System; and the SID of every domain of the forest.
    "SID history" counts the principals by class (Warning when there are any - a tool default,
    Info by override; Pass, worded "none visible to this account", when the read succeeded and
    returned none). "SID history same-domain" is a Fail when a historic SID belongs to the
    principal's own domain; "SID history privileged" is a Fail when one is a privileged
    well-known RID or SID, naming which; "SID history sources" lists the domains the historic SIDs
    came from, as a forest domain, a trust partner or "unresolved". Principals returned with no
    sIDHistory value read make the same-domain, privileged and sources rows Not Assessed, never
    Pass. Unreachable, access denied and timed out are each their own Not Assessed result. Names
    and SIDs are in their own columns, never in Detail. Whether a trust filters SID history is in
    the Trusts section.

    Gpo also compares, for every scoped domain, each GPO's version in the directory with the
    version in its GPT.INI, both read from the domain's PDC emulator: the groupPolicyContainer
    objects under CN=Policies,CN=System (versionNumber, gPCFileSysPath, displayName and flags
    only, domain read access), then, after a probe of SMB port 445, a listing of
    \\<PDC>\SYSVOL\<domain>\Policies and a read-only open of each GPT.INI, as the account running
    the script (-Credential is not used for file reads). A GPO whose two versions differ is a
    Warning (a tool default; Fail by override) naming the half - user or computer - and both
    numbers, and saying it can be replication in flight. A GPO that could not be compared is
    Not Assessed with its cause: unreachable, access denied, folder or GPT.INI not found, not
    parsed, versionNumber not returned, or a gPCFileSysPath that is not the GPO's own folder (the
    host it names is never contacted). A SYSVOL folder named by a GPO GUID with no container
    visible to this account is a Warning (Info by override). Every scoped domain gets a summary
    row with the number of containers read, compared, mismatched and not compared; it passes only
    when every GPO was compared and none differs. The inventory rows are unchanged.

    Acl reads the directory only and needs READ_CONTROL on each object it reads. Microsoft's default
    descriptors give Authenticated Users that right on the domain head and on GPOs; the default DACL
    of AdminSDHolder is not published. Which parts of the descriptor the ActiveDirectory module asks
    for is not published either: if it asks for the SACL, which needs SeSecurityPrivilege, an
    ordinary account may get "Not returned" on every object (PORT-PLAN C9a). For every scoped domain
    it reads nTSecurityDescriptor - and nothing else - of the domain head, of
    CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System and of every groupPolicyContainer under CN=Policies,CN=System, and the
    domain's DN and SID with Get-ADDomain, each read sent to that domain. The owner and the DACL are
    read in memory; the SACL is not read. An object ACE scoped to the object's own class (or a class
    above it) is graded as unscoped; one scoped to any other GUID grants only that node. Every principal is a SID and is matched by SID
    only against an allowlist (Microsoft's published default holders, plus tool defaults labelled as
    such), never by name: a group named like a default group is still flagged. Rows: "DCSync
    rights" (a replication right, alone or through all extended rights or full control), "Domain
    root control rights" (WriteDacl, WriteOwner, write of all properties, full control, or a
    non-default owner), "AdminSDHolder rights", one "GPO rights" row per GPO, and a GPO summary. A
    flagged principal is a Warning (a tool default; Fail by override) and every row says the
    allowlist is provisional. Deny ACEs are honoured only when they name the same SID, come first and
    cover every graded bit; inherit-only ACEs are skipped. A descriptor that was not returned, came
    back without a DACL, or could not be read is Not Assessed with its cause, and unreachable, access
    denied and timed out are told apart; a NULL DACL (present flag set, no ACL), which grants everyone
    full access, is flagged as Everyone. A flagged principal is reported even when another ACE is of
    a type the tool does not interpret. Group membership is not expanded.

    PrivilegedAccounts, PrivilegedHygiene and Kerberos share one set of directory reads, made once
    per run for the sections selected, and change nothing. Every domain of the forest is read with
    Get-ADDomain for its DN and SID, so a member is read from the domain that holds it. Per scoped
    domain, each group of the Privileged table is located by SID (objectSid, never a name) and
    expanded transitively: every member DN read with Get-ADObject from its own domain, nested groups
    followed (security and distribution alike), and the members whose primaryGroupID is the group's
    RID added. A foreign security principal is counted and reported by SID; a member in a forest
    domain this run did not scope, unreachable, refused or not found, or a member list cut at a
    range-retrieval limit (exactly 1500 or 5000 values), makes the count Not Assessed - never lower.
    PrivilegedAccounts: "Schema Admins members" and "Enterprise Admins members" for the forest root
    (Warning at one member, a tool default from the catalogue; Learn names the root's Administrator
    as a possible Enterprise Admins exception, which a config switch can apply), one "Privileged
    group" count per group (inventory, no threshold) and a population row per domain.
    PrivilegedHygiene: "adminCount orphans" (adminCount=1 on an object no protected group of
    Appendix C reaches - plus Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers, which Learn's security-groups
    page also marks as protected - and not Administrator or krbtgt; an object protected only through
    an operator group is not judged when dSHeuristics' dwAdminSDExMask is not 0), "Privileged accounts
    with SPN" (enabled user accounts only), "Privileged accounts delegable" (neither "Account is
    sensitive and cannot be delegated" nor a Protected Users membership at domain functional level
    Windows Server 2012 R2 or later, where Learn says it blocks delegation; a C10 addition, not a
    catalogue rule) and "Protected Users members". Kerberos: the Kerberoast and AS-REP rows, then
    unconstrained delegation and protocol transition outside the domain controllers - which are
    excluded by the DN of their computer account, read from serverReference of each server object
    holding an nTDSDSA child in the configuration partition, never by name or primaryGroupID -
    Kerberos-only constrained delegation (inventory) and resource-based constrained delegation,
    whose descriptor is read in memory to list the SIDs it allows. A row that could not be read is
    Not Assessed, with unreachable, access denied and timed out told apart; every grade is a tool
    default. Nothing passes on an empty answer: the group reads must find the domain's registered
    DCs in its DC groups, the adminCount search and the privileged-account population must hold
    the domain's Administrator (RID 500), and the delegation search must return every DC registered
    for the domain; otherwise the rows built on them are Not Assessed.

    DcDiagnostics runs dcdiag from the host running the assessment, one test per invocation
    ("dcdiag /test:<test> /s:<server>"; administrative rights on the DC and an elevated session,
    as Microsoft requires), and reads its en-US output. Each verdict is attributed to the block it
    is printed in - a DC, a partition or the enterprise - and never counted against an unrelated
    DC. Per DC, a grid row of 21 tests carrying the DC's domain (19 run by default; Connectivity
    first, and when it fails nothing more is started on that DC). Per scoped domain, one "dcdiag
    Topology" row and one "dcdiag coverage" row. Per forest, CheckSDRefDom and CrossRefValidation
    once each, with one row per partition and a roll-up. Per site that holds a DC, Intersite with
    -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests. LocatorCheck is never run: it is not in Microsoft's table of dcdiag
    tests. With -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests the DNS test's summary table is read from the same output
    (no further invocation): one row per DC and test group (Auth, Basc, Forw, Del, Dyn, RReg, Ext),
    PASS Pass, FAIL Fail, WARN Warning, n/a Not Assessed except under Ext, where /DnsAll does not
    ask for it (Info), and one roll-up per domain and test group over every DC of the domain. The
    header is found by its tokens and the cells mapped by order (one Learn sample, KB 2102154); a
    missing table, row, column or cell is Not Assessed. Without the switch, one "dcdiag DNS not
    run" row per domain says why. dcdiag's /x (XML) output is not used: Microsoft publishes no
    schema for it. Unreachable, access denied, output not recognised (possibly localised) and an event log
    that could not be queried are each their own Not Assessed cause, never Pass. Like DcDisk it
    covers the DCs of every scoped domain; the site and read-only flag of each DC come from the DC
    inventory, so name DomainControllers with it for the per-site rows.

    Dns asks every DC of every scoped domain as a DNS server, through the DnsServer module of the
    host running the assessment (Get-DnsServerZone, Get-DnsServerScavenging, Get-DnsServerZoneAging,
    Get-DnsServerForwarder; reads only). Per DC, one "DNS server" row and one "Zone configuration"
    row per zone (inventory). Per domain and primary zone, over every DNS server of the domain:
    "Zone dynamic update" (Nonsecure and secure on an AD-integrated zone is a Fail - Microsoft
    recommends Secure only; a zone that is not AD-integrated is Info) and "Zone scavenging" (zone
    aging off, or no hosting server scavenging, is a Warning - tool defaults, Microsoft publishes no
    requirement). A DC that could not be read keeps every zone of its domain from passing.
    Unreachable, access denied, DNS provider missing, timed out and the DnsServer module missing are
    each their own Not Assessed cause.

    DnsAdConsistency also gives every DC of each scoped domain a "DC locator records" row (named by
    _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs and _kerberos._tcp.dc._msdcs on every DNS server; a Fail only when both
    instruments - the server's own zone and Resolve-DnsName against it - agree it is missing, a
    Warning when only one could say so; an RODC is Info) and a "DC A record" row (its A records
    against the IPv4 addresses of its enabled adapters), and lists each locator record that names no
    DC ("Stale DC record", Warning) with a population row per domain.

    DomainControllers also gives each DC a "DNS client" row in its own report section, DC DNS
    Client: the DNS server list of its enabled adapters (Win32_NetworkAdapterConfiguration over
    remote CIM, local Administrators rights on the DC), a Warning for a single DNS server where more
    than one DC is known, otherwise inventory.

    DnsRecordExport is opt-in: it is not part of All and runs only when named. It exports every
    record of every zone of every DC's DNS server to csv\DnsRecords_<server>_<zone>.csv, capped per
    zone at RecordExportCap (50000, a tool default), with one row per zone saying how many records
    were exported of how many, and whether the cap was reached.

    PortMatrix also classifies every DC of every scoped domain ONCE per run, before the first per-DC
    section, and gives it a "DC reachability" row: name resolution from the host running the
    assessment, then a TCP connection to LDAP (389) on each resolved address, then WinRM (5985) and
    RPC (135) at the address that answered. Unreachable - by name resolution, refused, no answer
    within the time-out, or no route - is a Fail (Warning by override); LDAP answering with both
    default management ports silent is a Warning (Info by override); LDAP with WinRM, or with RPC
    only (remote CIM over DCOM), is a Pass. No ICMP, one attempt per port, a 3000 ms time-out (a tool
    default). Since v1.34.0 (PORT-PLAN C14c, C1b) the class is reused by every per-DC section that
    connects to the DC - the port matrix, Replication, Backup, DcSystem, DcDisk, DcHardening,
    DcCertificates, Dns, DcDiagnostics, DcSecureChannel, Sysvol, DsEvents, RestoreIntegrity, Site
    Health, Time Hierarchy, the SYSVOL read of Gpo on each PDC, and the partner DC of a trust: a DC
    classified Unreachable is Not Assessed there with its class (the dcdiag grid row stays Fail) and
    is probed by its name on no port again; every other probe goes through one per-run cache, so a
    DC is probed once per port. The trust check still probes the trusted domain's own name (135, 389),
    which is not a DC host name. Only PortMatrix classifies: without it no DC is classified (Topology
    alone does not), and the sections share the cache only. Access denied is not a reachability
    class: the read that meets it reports it.

    Since v1.34.0 (PORT-PLAN C2e) the per-DC sections that took the unscoped DC list - Replication,
    the SYSVOL shares, DFS Replication events and backlog, the patch level of DcSystem, Backup, Time
    Hierarchy, the port matrix, DsEvents, RestoreIntegrity, the DNS server set of the DNS-vs-AD
    sections and the raw repadmin/dcdiag captures - take the DCs of every scoped domain, each row
    carrying its domain, even when DomainControllers is not among -Sections; a scoped domain whose DCs
    could not be enumerated, or returned none, gets its own Not Assessed "(population)" row. Not yet
    the SYSVOL subscription verdict, which still takes its DCs from the DC inventory only (PORT-PLAN
    C2i).

    Topology also gives the forest one "Subnets without a site" row (siteObject of every subnet
    object; a Warning when any has none, a tool default) and every DC of every scoped domain one
    "Clients without a site" row: the NO_CLIENT_SITE lines of its netlogon.log, read-only over
    \\<dc>\ADMIN$\debug\netlogon.log (administrative rights on the DC), the last 4 MB only (a tool
    default). Clients found are a Warning; none found is Info, never Pass, because Microsoft does not
    say how long the lines are kept. netlogon.bak is not read.

    TimeHierarchy also gives every DC a "Clock offset vs forest root PDC" row: the difference between
    its w32tm /stripchart reading and the forest root PDC's, both taken from the host running the
    assessment, graded against 300 s (the Kerberos default maximum tolerance, Fail) and 60 s (a tool
    default, Warning), with an error bound from the round trips (tool reasoning). The forest root PDC
    itself is Info; a DC or a reference that could not be measured is Not Assessed.

    StaleObjects now counts, per scoped domain, enabled users and computers with no logon recorded
    for -StaleDays days, those that never logged on included (whenCreated older than the cutoff),
    with a listed-property, capped read (never every property), and states in every row the domain's
    msDS-LogonTimeSyncInterval and that the real last logon can be up to that many days more recent
    (Microsoft: updated when the value is "9 to 14 days older" under the default 14). It also gives
    "Enabled users with an old password" (pwdLastSet older than 365 days, a tool default; pwdLastSet 0
    with UF_DONT_EXPIRE_PASSWD clear, "must change at next logon", is counted, not graded; pwdLastSet 0
    with it set is a password of unknown age, and the row cannot pass while one exists). Each domain's
    enabled users and computers are counted first, as the declared population: a read that found
    nothing passes only when that count is at least one. A failed or capped read, a sync interval of
    0, a -StaleDays not longer than the sync interval, and an empty or uncounted population behind an
    empty result are Not Assessed.

    Sysvol also gives every DC of every scoped domain a "SYSVOL engine" row and every scoped domain a
    "SYSVOL replication engine" row, from the directory of that domain: the DC's DFSR SYSVOL
    subscription and its NTFRS subscriber. A writable DC still on FRS, or between FRS and DFSR, is a
    Warning (Fail by override); every writable DC on DFSR is a Pass; an engine not known is Not
    Assessed. msDFSR-Flags is quoted as corroboration only.

    Inventory depth (PORT-PLAN C15). Every row below is Info when it was read and Not Assessed with
    its cause (unreachable, access denied, timed out, cap reached, not set, not returned) when it was
    not - never Pass, Warning or Fail, and a count that could not be read is never 0. Each read names
    its properties (never every property) and has a cap.

    Forest also gives a "Topology Inventory" section: the forest and domain functional levels
    (msDS-Behavior-Version, named as MS-ADTS names them; 8, 9 or a value not returned is Not
    Assessed), the five FSMO holders from fSMORoleOwner (a value naming a deleted NTDS Settings
    object is Not Assessed), the UPN suffixes of CN=Partitions, each domain's msDS-AllowedDNSSuffixes,
    each DC's DNS suffix against its domain (Disjoint Yes or No; Microsoft calls a disjoint namespace
    usable with caution) and the runner DC (the rootDSE dnsHostName that answered the run).

    Trusts also gives a "Trust Inventory" section: every trustedDomain object of every scoped
    domain, read from that domain, with trustAttributes decoded by its MS-ADTS names (a bit with no
    name kept as "unknown 0x.."), direction and type with their names, transitivity, the SID filtering
    flags, CROSS_ORGANIZATION by its raw name, and TGT delegation from the MS-KILE rule.

    Identity also gives "Object Counts" (users, computers and groups, each split enabled and
    disabled or security and distribution, contacts, OUs, gMSA and sMSA, from capped reads that are
    not the full export) and "Group Inventory" (groups by scope and type, empty groups, direct
    member counts - a group of 1500 values or more, the MaxValRange default, is not counted exactly;
    one row per group in csv\Groups_<domain>.csv, ListComplete "No" on every row of a read that
    reached its cap). A failed user or computer export is now Not
    Assessed in "Identity Inventory", not 0.

    StaleObjects also gives "Stale disabled users" and "Stale disabled computers" per domain:
    disabled accounts with no logon recorded for -StaleDays days, counted and named, not graded.

    Gpo also gives a "GPO Directory Inventory" section from the directory, without the GroupPolicy
    module: each GPO's status flags, WMI filter (gPCWQLFilter matched to its msWMI-Som object) and
    links, the links of every site, domain and OU (gPLink options disabled and enforced; gPOptions
    raw), and per domain the linked and unlinked GPOs. A GPO whose links could not be read is never
    called unlinked. A GPO can be linked from any domain of the forest: the links of every scoped
    domain are matched against every other's GPOs, and "unlinked" is stated only when every forest
    domain was read (-AllDomains); otherwise a GPO with none found is "no link found", Not Assessed.

    DsEvents also gives an "Event Inventory" section: on every DC, the System, Directory Service,
    DFS Replication, DNS Server and File Replication Service logs read by level (Critical, Error,
    Warning) over -EventLookbackDays with at most 2000 events per log (a tool default), grouped by
    event ID (at most 50 groups per log, a tool default). A log that does not exist is Info "log
    absent"; a count read at the bound or over a log that does not reach back is a floor, on the log
    row and on each event group row. "No events" is taken only after a one-event read of the log
    (-LogName -MaxEvents 1) succeeds, because the filtered read answers a refusal as "no events".

    TimeHierarchy also gives a "Time Configuration" section: each DC's W32Time Type and NtpServer
    (W32Time\Parameters) and AnnounceFlags (W32Time\Config), read from the registry with the two
    StdRegProv Get methods the tool already uses (local Administrators on the DC). Not graded. The
    Group Policy area that overrides these values is not read, and each row says so.

.PARAMETER ConfigOverridePath
    Path to a JSON file that replaces built-in config tables, one top-level key per section.
    Seventeen sections can be named. DcDisk, whose keys are WarnBelowPercent, FailBelowPercent,
    WarnBelowFreeGB and FailBelowFreeGB (WsmanPort and RpcPort are refused since v1.34.0, see below):

        { "DcDisk": { "WarnBelowPercent": 25, "FailBelowPercent": 15 } }

    And DcHardening, whose one key is Settings. Settings is keyed by
    setting id (LdapServerIntegrity, LdapServerEnforceIntegrity, LdapEnforceChannelBinding,
    SmbServerSigning, SmbClientSigning, LmCompatibilityLevel), and under each id a file can
    set ExpectedMinimum, to one of the values the tool can grade against, and OtherStatus,
    to Warning or Fail:

        { "DcHardening": { "Settings": { "LmCompatibilityLevel": { "ExpectedMinimum": 3 } } } }

    DcHardening and DcDisk refuse WsmanPort and RpcPort, and so does -DcSystemConfigPath (PORT-PLAN
    C3g, v1.34.0): WsmanPort and RpcPort are refused because the CIM session always uses the default
    ports - WinRM 5985, or DCOM through the RPC endpoint mapper on 135 - so a port set in the file
    would change the probe and not the connection. New-CimSession -Port needs a WinRM listener on that
    port, which only a change on the DC creates, and DCOM has no port option.

    And Laps, whose keys are WarnAbovePercent and FailAbovePercent (the share of in-population
    computers with no LAPS expiry time visible above which a domain is a Warning or a Fail),
    SampleLimit (how many computer names one row lists, 0 to 1000) and ExpiredGraceDays (how
    long after its expiry time a DC's value still counts as current). All four are tool
    defaults, not Microsoft values:

        { "Laps": { "WarnAbovePercent": 5, "FailAbovePercent": 50 } }

    Laps refuses every other key. In particular no attribute name can be set from a file, so
    a file cannot make the tool request a password attribute.

    And DcCertificates, whose keys are WarnWithinDays and FailWithinDays (a certificate with at
    most that many whole days left is a Warning or a Fail; 0 switches a window off, and an
    expired certificate is always a Fail), HandshakeTimeoutMs (the bound on the whole LDAPS
    handshake) and ConnectTimeoutMs (the bound on its TCP connection), each 100 to 60000 ms.
    All four are tool defaults, not Microsoft values. The LDAPS port and the EKU names are
    refused:

        { "DcCertificates": { "WarnWithinDays": 45, "FailWithinDays": 7 } }

    And RidPool, whose keys are WarnAtPercentUsed and FailAtPercentUsed: the share of the global
    RID space issued at which a domain is a Warning and a Fail, each a whole number from 1 to 99,
    Fail above Warning. The built-in 10 and 90 are Microsoft's published points (event 16658 and
    the RID ceiling). The ceilings and every attribute name are refused:

        { "RidPool": { "WarnAtPercentUsed": 5 } }

    And SidHistory, used by the SecurityPosture section, whose keys are PresentStatus (the grade
    of SID history that is present and neither same-domain nor privileged: Warning or Info) and
    NameListCap (how many names one row lists, a whole number from 0 to 1000). Both are tool
    defaults, not Microsoft values. The privileged list, the filters and the property lists are
    refused:

        { "SidHistory": { "PresentStatus": "Info", "NameListCap": 50 } }

    And Gpo, used by the Gpo section's version check, whose keys are MismatchStatus (the grade of
    a GPO whose directory and GPT.INI versions differ: Warning or Fail) and OrphanFolderStatus
    (the grade of a SYSVOL GPO folder with no container: Warning or Info). Both are tool
    defaults, not Microsoft values: Microsoft publishes no grade for either. The filter, the
    property list, the share, the folder and file names, the port and the size bound are
    refused:

        { "Gpo": { "MismatchStatus": "Fail" } }

    And Acl, used by the Acl section, whose keys are FlaggedStatus (the grade of a row with a
    principal outside the allowlist: Warning or Fail; Warning is a tool default) and
    AdditionalAllowed, a list of SIDs to add to the allowlist, each with Sid (a SID in its text form -
    a name is refused), Objects (DomainRoot, AdminSDHolder, Gpo), Rights (WriteDacl, WriteOwner,
    WriteAllProperties, AllExtendedRights, DS-Replication-Get-Changes, DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All,
    DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set) and Reason. Everyone, Anonymous Logon, Authenticated
    Users, Users, Guests and RIDs 513, 514 and 515 of any domain can never be added. The built-in
    allowlist, the masks, the GUIDs, the filter and the property list are refused:

        { "Acl": { "AdditionalAllowed": [ { "Sid": "S-1-5-21-1-2-3-1105", "Objects": [ "DomainRoot" ],
                   "Rights": [ "DS-Replication-Get-Changes", "DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All" ],
                   "Reason": "Entra Connect AD DS connector account" } ] } }

    And Privileged, used by the PrivilegedAccounts, PrivilegedHygiene and Kerberos sections, whose
    keys are the eight grades SchemaAdminsStatus, EnterpriseAdminsStatus, OrphanStatus,
    PrivilegedSpnStatus, DelegableStatus, UnconstrainedStatus, ProtocolTransitionStatus and
    RbcdStatus (each Warning or Fail; Warning is a tool default), EnterpriseAdminsExemptRootAdministrator
    (true leaves the forest root's built-in Administrator out of the Enterprise Admins count),
    NameListCap (0 to 1000) and MaxExpandedObjects (objects read per run, member reads and
    primary-group search results together, 1 to 100000). The groups,
    the userAccountControl bits, the filters and the property lists are refused:

        { "Privileged": { "SchemaAdminsStatus": "Fail", "EnterpriseAdminsExemptRootAdministrator": true } }

    And DcDiag, used by the DcDiagnostics section, whose keys are TestTimeoutSeconds (the time-out
    of one dcdiag invocation, a whole number from 5 to 600; it takes precedence over
    -ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds for dcdiag) and Retries (attempts per invocation, 1 to 10). Both
    are tool defaults, not Microsoft values: Microsoft publishes no dcdiag time-out. The test
    lists, the opt-in list, the output markers and the evidence patterns are refused, so a file
    can never add a dcdiag test:

        { "DcDiag": { "TestTimeoutSeconds": 180 } }

    And Dns, whose keys are the grades NonsecureStatus (Fail or Warning), ScavengingOffStatus,
    AgingOffStatus, ARecordStatus, StaleRecordStatus and SingleServerStatus (each Warning or Info),
    OneInstrumentStatus (Warning or Not Assessed: a missing record seen by one instrument only) and
    RecordExportCap (records per zone for DnsRecordExport, 1 to 1000000). All are tool defaults, not
    Microsoft values. The record names, zone types, patterns, error codes and ports are refused:

        { "Dns": { "NonsecureStatus": "Warning", "RecordExportCap": 10000 } }

    And Reach, used by the PortMatrix section's reachability classes, whose keys are ProbeTimeoutMs
    (100 to 60000), UnreachableStatus (Fail or Warning) and NotManageableStatus (Warning or Info). All
    are tool defaults, not Microsoft values. The ports and the socket error names are refused:

        { "Reach": { "ProbeTimeoutMs": 5000 } }

    And SiteCoverage, used by the Topology section, whose keys are UnassignedStatus and
    NoClientSiteStatus (each Warning or Info), LogTailBytes (65536 to 20000000, how much of the end of
    netlogon.log is read) and NameListCap (0 to 1000). All are tool defaults. The log path, the marker
    and the port are refused:

        { "SiteCoverage": { "UnassignedStatus": "Info" } }

    And ClockSkew, used by the TimeHierarchy section, whose keys are WarnSeconds (1 to 299, a tool
    default) and Samples (1 to 20). KerberosMaxSeconds is Microsoft's and is refused:

        { "ClockSkew": { "WarnSeconds": 30 } }

    And Stale, used by the StaleObjects section, whose keys are StaleStatus and PasswordAgeStatus (each
    Warning or Info), PasswordAgeDays (30 to 3650), MaxObjects (1 to 1000000) and NameListCap (0 to
    1000). All are tool defaults. The stale age itself is -StaleDays; the sync interval default, the
    filters and the property lists are refused:

        { "Stale": { "PasswordAgeDays": 180 } }

    And SysvolEngine, used by the Sysvol section, whose one key is FrsStatus (Warning or Fail; Warning
    is a tool default). The DN templates and the state names are refused:

        { "SysvolEngine": { "FrsStatus": "Fail" } }

    And Inventory, used by the inventory rows of the Identity, Forest, Trusts, Gpo, DsEvents and
    TimeHierarchy sections, and since v1.33.0 as the cap of the user and computer export, the trust
    health read and the site link and connection reads (Topology), whose keys are MaxObjects
    (objects per directory read, 1 to 1000000),
    NameListCap (names listed per row, 0 to 1000), EventMaxEvents (events read per log and DC, 1 to
    100000) and EventGroupCap (event ID groups listed per log and DC, 1 to 1000). All are tool
    defaults, not Microsoft values. The logs, the levels, the filters, the flag tables and labels,
    the registry keys and the patterns are refused; the event window is -EventLookbackDays:

        { "Inventory": { "EventMaxEvents": 5000 } }

    Keys a section omits keep their built-in value, and findings cite the file only for the
    values it supplied. Each section's value is checked by that section's own rules. The run
    stops, before anything is collected, on a missing or malformed file, a file that is not one
    JSON object (an array is refused even when it holds one object), a key written twice, an
    unknown section, an unknown key, a null, or a value that cannot be used.
    The file is checked even when the section it names is not among -Sections.
    -ExchangeSeConfigPath and -DcSystemConfigPath are separate and unchanged.

.PARAMETER DcSystemConfigPath
    Path to a JSON file that replaces the built-in DC system table, whose OsLifecycle list
    holds, per Windows Server release, a Label, a Build and/or a name Pattern, an ExtendedEnd
    date and an EsuEnd date (yyyy-MM-dd, or null where Microsoft publishes none). Use it when
    Microsoft ships a release or publishes an Extended Security Updates date this build does
    not know. Keys the file omits keep their built-in value; a file that supplies OsLifecycle
    replaces the whole list. A missing or malformed file, or a row that could not be used,
    stops the run. WsmanPort and RpcPort are refused (PORT-PLAN C3g): the CIM session of the
    patch-level read always uses the default ports.

.PARAMETER IncludeDcdiag
    Also capture raw 'dcdiag /c /v' output per DC under raw\. NOT read-only. Microsoft
    documents /c as "Comprehensive. Runs all tests except DCPromo and RegisterInDNS, including
    non-default tests". By that description it includes CutoffServers, which "triggers
    replication on the DCs", and the DNS test, whose dynamic update test registers and deletes
    a test record - whether or not -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests is given. The two switches are
    independent: this one controls the raw capture, that one the parsed grid.

.PARAMETER IncludeDcdiagActiveTests
    Also run the three dcdiag tests whose Microsoft description is not read-only:
    /test:DNS /DnsAll, whose dynamic update test performs "registration of a test record
    (_dcdiag_test_record)" that "is deleted automatically after the test"; /test:SystemLog, which
    "Validates the System Event Log's health by reading and writing entries" (Microsoft does not
    say what is written); and /test:Intersite /a, which works by "reporting back any replication
    failures after triggering a replication", run once per site that holds a DC rather than once
    per DC. NOT read-only. Without this switch the DNS and SystemLog cells of the DC Diagnostics
    grid read Not Assessed, each DC's row says why, the row's status is taken from the other 19
    tests, one "dcdiag Intersite: not run" row says Intersite was not run, and one "dcdiag DNS not
    run" row per domain says the same of the DNS summary. With it, the DNS test's summary table is
    graded per test group - Auth, Basc, Forw, Del, Dyn, RReg, Ext - for each DC and each domain.
    Microsoft documents that membership in Enterprise Admins, or equivalent, is the minimum
    requirement to complete the DNS tests.

.PARAMETER IncludeLdapsHandshake
    In the DcCertificates section, also read the certificate each DC presents on LDAPS, port
    636, with one TLS handshake per DC that sends no LDAP request, no bind and no credential,
    and needs no right on the DC. The certificate is neither trusted nor rejected by this tool:
    its expiry is graded, and what the platform of the host running the assessment said about
    its chain and name is quoted. Opt-in because Microsoft documents Schannel error logging as
    on by default and does not state that a handshake leaves no trace on the server: a DC that
    cannot complete the handshake may log a Schannel error event in its System log. Building the
    chain on the host running the assessment may also fetch issuer certificates from the
    addresses a certificate names. Each handshake is bounded as a whole by HandshakeTimeoutMs
    and its connection by ConnectTimeoutMs (DcCertificates config, 5000 ms each, tool
    defaults). Without this switch each DC's LDAPS row reads Not Assessed.

.PARAMETER IncludeRepadmin
    Also capture raw 'repadmin /replsummary' and per-DC '/showrepl /errorsonly' under raw\.

.PARAMETER IncludeLingeringObjectScan
    Proactively enumerate lingering objects per DC with
    'repadmin /removelingeringobjects ... /advisory_mode' (each DC compared against its
    domain's PDC emulator). Advisory mode changes NOTHING in the directory, but it does
    write events (1938/1942/1946) to the target DCs' Directory Service logs - hence
    opt-in. Strongly recommended in a post-restore recovery, where tombstone lifetime
    may already have been exceeded.

.PARAMETER IncludeSysvolBacklog
    Measure the SYSVOL replication backlog in both directions between every DC and its domain's
    PDC emulator, using the DFSR module. Read-only. Opt-in because it costs two RPC round trips
    per DC. Get-DfsrBacklog displays at most 100 records and its true total appears only in the
    verbose stream, so where that cannot be read the count is reported as a floor, never as a
    total.

.PARAMETER SkipTrustVerification
    Collect trust configuration but do not run the external secure-channel verification
    (nltest / netdom). Trust health is then reported as "Not Assessed".

.PARAMETER IncludeSecureChannelVerify
    Run the live secure channel test, nltest /sc_verify, in place of the default nltest
    /sc_query. NOT read-only: Microsoft's nltest reference says that if the secure channel does
    not work, /sc_verify removes the existing channel and builds a new one. It runs on the host
    running the assessment (outbound trust direction), on a domain controller of each trust
    partner (inbound direction), and on every domain controller of the scoped domains
    (DcSecureChannel section). Without this switch those checks report the state recorded the
    last time each channel was used, which can miss a channel that has broken since.

.PARAMETER Server
    Optional DC / ADWS endpoint to target for AD cmdlets.

.PARAMETER Credential
    Optional credential for AD cmdlets and trust verification.

.PARAMETER StaleDays
    Age (days since the last logon timestamp, or since creation for an account that never logged on)
    beyond which an enabled account is stale. Default: 90, a tool default, not a Microsoft value. It
    must be longer than the domain's msDS-LogonTimeSyncInterval (14 days by default), the most
    lastLogonTimestamp can trail the real last logon, or the stale rows are Not Assessed. The password age is the PasswordAgeDays key of the Stale section of
    -ConfigOverridePath.

.PARAMETER KrbtgtMaxAgeDays
    krbtgt password age (days) beyond which a Warning is raised. Default: 180.

.PARAMETER RpcPortTimeoutMs
    TCP connect timeout for reachability probes (RPC/135, ADWS/9389). Default: 1200.

.PARAMETER ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds
    Timeout for each external tool invocation (dcdiag/repadmin/nltest/netdom). Default: 90, a
    tool default, not a Microsoft value. For dcdiag, the TestTimeoutSeconds key of the DcDiag
    section of -ConfigOverridePath takes precedence when the file sets it.

.PARAMETER Retries
    Max attempts for external tools before marking FAIL. Default: 2.

.PARAMETER RetryDelaySeconds
    Delay between external-tool retries. Default: 2.

.EXAMPLE
    .\Invoke-ADForestAssessment.ps1 -AllDomains -IncludeDcdiag -IncludeRepadmin -Verbose

    Full forest assessment (all domains) with raw diagnostics, verbose progress, report in
    the current user's Documents.

.EXAMPLE
    .\Invoke-ADForestAssessment.ps1 -Sections Trusts,Replication -SkipTrustVerification

    Collect only trust configuration and replication, without running external trust checks.

.OUTPUTS
    [pscustomobject] run summary (paths, counts, per-section status). Data files are written
    as CSV and a single HTML report.

.NOTES
    Author  : TakeItToCloud (Carlos Annes)
    Requires: RSAT ActiveDirectory module. DnsServer / GroupPolicy / DFSR modules optional.
    Runtime : Windows PowerShell 5.1 or PowerShell 7+. See .DESCRIPTION for what a run changes.
#>

[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [ValidateNotNullOrEmpty()]
    [string]$OutputPath,

    [switch]$AllDomains,

    [ValidateSet('All', 'Forest', 'Domains', 'Fsmo', 'DomainControllers', 'Replication',
        'Topology', 'Trusts', 'DcDiagnostics', 'Dns', 'Sysvol', 'Gpo', 'PasswordPolicy',
        'PrivilegedAccounts', 'SecurityPosture', 'StaleObjects', 'Identity',
        'Pki', 'Acl', 'Kerberos', 'PrivilegedHygiene', 'DcHardening', 'Backup', 'TimeSync',
        'DnsDepth', 'Redundancy', 'ExchangeSchema', 'ExchangeSeReadiness',
        'DnsAdConsistency', 'DsaCname', 'GcConsistency', 'PortMatrix', 'DcSecureChannel',
        'DsEvents', 'RestoreIntegrity', 'MsdcsDelegation', 'TimeHierarchy', 'SiteGc',
        'DcSystem', 'DcDisk', 'Laps', 'DcCertificates', 'RidPool', 'DnsRecordExport')]
    [string[]]$Sections = @('All'),

    [switch]$IncludeDcdiag,
    [switch]$IncludeRepadmin,
    [switch]$IncludeLingeringObjectScan,

    # Measures the SYSVOL backlog in both directions between each DC and its domain's PDC
    # emulator. Opt-in because it costs two RPC round trips per DC and needs the optional DFSR
    # module; strongly recommended on a post-restore run, where a standing SYSVOL backlog is the
    # difference between "Group Policy is converging" and "it silently is not".
    [switch]$IncludeSysvolBacklog,
    [switch]$SkipTrustVerification,

    # Run the LIVE secure channel test, nltest /sc_verify, in place of the read-only
    # nltest /sc_query. Opt-in because it is not read-only: Microsoft documents that /sc_verify
    # removes and rebuilds a secure channel that is not working. It runs on the host, on a DC of
    # each trust partner, and on every DC of the scoped domains.
    [switch]$IncludeSecureChannelVerify,

    # Also run the dcdiag tests that Microsoft documents as changing state: /test:DNS /DnsAll,
    # whose dynamic update test registers and then deletes a DNS test record; /test:SystemLog,
    # which Microsoft describes as reading and writing System log entries; and /test:Intersite /a,
    # which triggers a replication and runs once per site. Opt-in because they are not read-only.
    # Without it the DNS and SystemLog grid cells read Not Assessed, the row says why, one
    # "dcdiag Intersite: not run" row says the same, and so does one "dcdiag DNS not run" row per
    # domain. With it the DNS summary table is graded per test group, per DC and per domain.
    [switch]$IncludeDcdiagActiveTests,

    # Read the certificate each DC presents on LDAPS (port 636) with a TLS handshake that sends
    # nothing but the handshake. Opt-in because Microsoft does not state that a handshake leaves
    # no trace on the server: a DC that cannot complete it may log a Schannel error event.
    # Without it the DcCertificates section reads the directory only.
    [switch]$IncludeLdapsHandshake,

    [string]$Server,
    [pscredential]$Credential,

    # Replaces the built-in Exchange SE prerequisite table (supported forest functional levels
    # and DC operating systems) from a JSON file, so a revision to Microsoft's supportability
    # matrix is a config edit rather than a code change. Keys omitted from the file keep their
    # built-in value; an unreadable or malformed file is a terminating error rather than a
    # silent fall back to defaults, because a run that quietly judged against the wrong table
    # would be worse than one that stopped.
    [ValidateNotNullOrEmpty()]
    [string]$ExchangeSeConfigPath,

    # Replaces the built-in DC system table (the operating system lifecycle dates the DcSystem
    # section judges against) from a JSON file, so a new Windows Server release or a newly
    # published Extended Security Updates date is a config edit. Same contract as
    # -ExchangeSeConfigPath: keys omitted keep their built-in value, and a missing or malformed
    # file is a terminating error, never a silent fall back.
    [ValidateNotNullOrEmpty()]
    [string]$DcSystemConfigPath,

    # One JSON file that replaces built-in config tables section by section. Its top-level keys
    # are section config names - "DcDisk", "DcHardening", "Laps", "DcCertificates", "RidPool", "SidHistory", "Gpo", "Acl", "Privileged", "DcDiag", "Dns", "Reach", "SiteCoverage", "ClockSkew", "Stale", "SysvolEngine" and "Inventory" - and each value is checked by that section's
    # own validator. Keys a section omits keep their built-in value, and a finding cites the
    # file only for the values the file supplied. A missing or malformed file, an unknown
    # section, an unknown key, a null or a value that cannot be used is a terminating error,
    # raised before anything is collected. -ExchangeSeConfigPath and -DcSystemConfigPath are
    # separate and unchanged; their tables cannot be set from this file.
    [ValidateNotNullOrEmpty()]
    [string]$ConfigOverridePath,

    [ValidateRange(1, 3650)][int]$StaleDays = 90,
    [ValidateRange(1, 3650)][int]$KrbtgtMaxAgeDays = 180,

    # How far back the Directory Service and DFS Replication event scans look. Before v1.14.0
    # this was a pair of constants in $script:Config with NO runtime override, so a restore that
    # happened more than 14 days ago could not be reached without editing the script - on the
    # exact tool built for assessing a forest after a restore. Widening it is safe because the
    # coverage guard already compares the oldest retained record against the window and reports
    # Truncated when the log does not reach back that far, so a 180-day request over a 30-day log
    # says so rather than reporting a clean scan of a period it never saw.
    [ValidateRange(1, 365)][int]$EventLookbackDays = 14,

    [ValidateRange(100, 60000)][int]$RpcPortTimeoutMs = 1200,
    [ValidateRange(5, 600)][int]$ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds = 90,
    [ValidateRange(1, 10)][int]$Retries = 2,
    [ValidateRange(0, 60)][int]$RetryDelaySeconds = 2
)

Set-StrictMode -Version Latest
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Versioned configuration (no magic numbers scattered in logic)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
$script:Config = @{
    Version                 = '1.35.0'
    StaleDays               = $StaleDays
    KrbtgtMaxAgeDays        = $KrbtgtMaxAgeDays
    RpcPortTimeoutMs        = $RpcPortTimeoutMs
    ExternalToolTimeoutSec  = $ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds
    Retries                 = $Retries
    RetryDelaySeconds       = $RetryDelaySeconds
    ReplicationStaleMinutes = 180
    # --- Replication convergence lag ------------------------------------------------------
    # OURS, not vendor limits, and the findings say so. Reasoning: intra-site replication is
    # notification-driven (seconds) and inter-site follows a site-link schedule whose default is
    # 180 minutes, so a link that has not succeeded for a day is outside any normal schedule, and
    # a week means it has stopped rather than lagged. The hard cliff is tombstone lifetime - past
    # that, re-connecting a stale DC introduces lingering objects instead of converging - so the
    # detail names it rather than these thresholds pretending to be it.
    ReplLagWarnHours        = 24
    ReplLagFailHours        = 168
    # The DCSync GUIDs and the name patterns that used to decide "default" here were retired by
    # PORT-PLAN C9 (v1.26.0): the allowlist is now matched by SID, in the Acl table below.
    # userAccountControl bit flags.
    Uac                     = @{
        PASSWD_NOTREQD         = 0x0020
        DONT_EXPIRE_PASSWORD   = 0x10000
        TRUSTED_FOR_DELEGATION = 0x80000
        DONT_REQ_PREAUTH       = 0x400000
        USE_DES_KEY_ONLY       = 0x200000
        ENCRYPTED_TEXT_PWD     = 0x0080
    }
    # Certificate template flags for ESC1 detection.
    EnrolleeSuppliesSubject = 0x00000001  # msPKI-Certificate-Name-Flag
    PendAllRequests         = 0x00000002  # msPKI-Enrollment-Flag (manager approval)
    AuthEkuOids             = @('1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2', '1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.2', '1.3.6.1.5.2.3.4', '2.5.29.37.0')
    # --- Recovery & consistency checks (post-incident) ---
    # DC machine-account passwords rotate every 30 days by default (client-initiated).
    # pwdLastSet is a replicated attribute, so age is collectable centrally; an old value
    # on a DC means rotation is not happening - typical of a DC restored from old backup.
    DcPasswordWarnDays      = 45
    DcPasswordFailDays      = 90
    # Ports replication and authentication actually require between DCs.
    ReplicationPorts        = @(
        @{ Port = 88;   Name = 'Kerberos';            Critical = $true }
        @{ Port = 135;  Name = 'RPC Endpoint Mapper'; Critical = $true }
        @{ Port = 389;  Name = 'LDAP';                Critical = $true }
        @{ Port = 445;  Name = 'SMB';                 Critical = $true }
        @{ Port = 636;  Name = 'LDAPS';               Critical = $false }
        @{ Port = 3268; Name = 'Global Catalog LDAP'; Critical = $false }
        @{ Port = 9389; Name = 'ADWS';                Critical = $false }
    )
    DsEventLookbackDays     = $EventLookbackDays
    # --- DFS Replication log events that decide whether SYSVOL is actually replicating ------
    # Meanings are taken from the vendor's own troubleshooting articles, not inferred:
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-missing-sysvol-and-netlogon-shares
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/group-policy/force-authoritative-non-authoritative-synchronization  (KB 2218556)
    # Read: 2026-09-21. Events whose meaning the vendor does not publish are deliberately absent
    # rather than guessed.
    DfsrEventsOfInterest    = @(
        @{ Id = 2213; Severity = 'Fail'; Meaning = 'Dirty shutdown detected - DFSR replication is PAUSED on this volume and will not resume until the ResumeReplication WMI method is run.' }
        @{ Id = 4012; Severity = 'Fail'; Meaning = 'Content freshness protection stopped replication - the folder has not replicated for longer than MaxOfflineTimeInDays. SYSVOL will not converge without reinitialisation.' }
        @{ Id = 4114; Severity = 'Fail'; Meaning = 'SYSVOL is no longer being replicated on this DC (membership disabled, msDFSR-Enabled=FALSE).' }
        @{ Id = 4144; Severity = 'Fail'; Meaning = 'DFSR membership disabled for this replicated folder - logged during a D2/D4-equivalent reinitialisation.' }
        @{ Id = 4614; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'DFSR is WAITING to perform initial replication of SYSVOL. Until event 4604 follows, this DC has not initialised SYSVOL.' }
        @{ Id = 4604; Severity = 'Info'; Meaning = 'SYSVOL replicated folder initialised successfully - the healthy end state.' }
        @{ Id = 2212; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'Dirty shutdown - DFSR is rebuilding its database; expect 2214 to follow on completion.' }
        @{ Id = 2214; Severity = 'Info'; Meaning = 'Dirty shutdown recovery completed.' }
        @{ Id = 5002; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'DFSR connection error with a replication partner.' }
        @{ Id = 5014; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'DFSR RPC communication problem with a replication partner.' }
    )
    DfsrEventLookbackDays   = $EventLookbackDays
    # --- SYSVOL backlog thresholds ---------------------------------------------------------
    # Microsoft is explicit that a DFSR backlog "is not necessarily an indication of problems"
    # and "indicates latency", so these are not vendor limits and are not presented as such:
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dfsr/get-dfsrbacklog  (read 2026-09-21)
    # The reasoning for applying a tighter bar to SYSVOL than to a general replicated folder is
    # that SYSVOL only changes when Group Policy changes, so it should sit at or near zero; a
    # standing backlog means a policy edit is not reaching that DC. Thresholds live here so an
    # operator can move them without touching code.
    SysvolBacklogWarnAt     = 1
    # FailAt is set to the cmdlet's own 100-record display cap: at or above it the true size is
    # no longer observable from the object count, and a folder that changes only with Group
    # Policy does not accumulate that much ordinary latency.
    SysvolBacklogFailAt     = 100
    # Get-DfsrBacklog returns at most 100 records; the true total appears only in the verbose
    # stream. Kept as data so the cap is not a magic number buried in the parser.
    DfsrBacklogDisplayCap   = 100
    SysvolReplicationGroup  = 'Domain System Volume'
    SysvolReplicatedFolder  = 'SYSVOL Share'
    # DN template for the SYSVOL subscription object, per KB 2218556. msDFSR-Enabled=FALSE means
    # SYSVOL replication is switched off on that DC; msDFSR-options=1 marks it authoritative (the
    # DFSR equivalent of FRS D4). Both are set by hand during a recovery and are easy to leave
    # behind, and neither is visible anywhere else in this report.
    SysvolSubscriptionDn    = 'CN=SYSVOL Subscription,CN=Domain System Volume,CN=DFSR-LocalSettings,{0}'
    SysvolShares            = @('SYSVOL', 'NETLOGON')
    # --- Exchange Server SE: the AD prerequisites, as a versioned table -------------------
    # Volatile vendor facts, so they live here with their source and read date and can be
    # replaced from a file via -ExchangeSeConfigPath when Microsoft revises the matrix. A new
    # supported OS or functional level must be a config edit, never a code edit.
    #
    # Source: https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/plan-and-deploy/supportability-matrix#supported-active-directory-environments
    # Read:   2026-11-12 (first read 2026-09-21; the Exchange Server SE row was unchanged)
    ExchangeSe              = @{
        Release                     = 'Exchange Server SE'
        SourceUrl                   = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/plan-and-deploy/supportability-matrix#supported-active-directory-environments'
        ReadDate                    = '2026-11-12'
        # ADForestMode names, as the supportability matrix lists them for this release:
        # "Windows Server 2016, Windows Server 2012 R2" (matrix re-read 2026-11-12).
        #
        # Windows Server 2016 is NOT the highest forest functional level. A Windows Server 2025
        # forest and domain functional level exists, and Windows Server 2019 and 2022 "use
        # Windows Server 2016 as the most recent functional level":
        #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/active-directory-functional-levels
        #   Read: 2026-11-12
        # The matrix does not list the 2025 level for this release, so it is absent here because
        # the vendor does not list it - not because it does not exist. A forest at that level is
        # ABOVE the supported levels, which is a different finding from one below them.
        SupportedForestModes        = @('Windows2016Forest', 'Windows2012R2Forest')
        # msDS-Behavior-Version on CN=Partitions,CN=Configuration -> the functional level it
        # denotes. The verdict is taken from this NUMBER, never from the mode name: the number
        # is ordered, so "below" and "above" the supported levels can be told apart, and it does
        # not depend on which ActiveDirectory module version printed the name.
        #   Attribute and its location, values 0-4:
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/raise-active-directory-domain-forest-functional-levels
        #   5 = 2012, 6 = 2012 R2, 7 = 2016 (the -ForestMode integers):
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/addsdeployment/install-addsforest
        #   10 = 2025 ("maps to the value of DomainLevel 10 and ForestLevel 10"):
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025
        #   Mode names (the -ForestMode accepted values, incl. Windows2025Forest):
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/set-adforestmode
        #   Read: 2026-11-12 (all four)
        # 8 and 9 are not published as functional levels and are deliberately absent. A value
        # not in this table is reported Not Assessed with the raw value, never interpreted.
        ForestFunctionalLevels      = @(
            @{ Level = 10; Mode = 'Windows2025Forest';        Label = 'Windows Server 2025' }
            @{ Level = 7;  Mode = 'Windows2016Forest';        Label = 'Windows Server 2016' }
            @{ Level = 6;  Mode = 'Windows2012R2Forest';      Label = 'Windows Server 2012 R2' }
            @{ Level = 5;  Mode = 'Windows2012Forest';        Label = 'Windows Server 2012' }
            @{ Level = 4;  Mode = 'Windows2008R2Forest';      Label = 'Windows Server 2008 R2' }
            @{ Level = 3;  Mode = 'Windows2008Forest';        Label = 'Windows Server 2008' }
            @{ Level = 2;  Mode = 'Windows2003Forest';        Label = 'Windows Server 2003' }
            @{ Level = 1;  Mode = 'Windows2003InterimForest'; Label = 'Windows Server 2003 interim' }
            @{ Level = 0;  Mode = 'Windows2000Forest';        Label = 'Windows 2000' }
        )
        # Matched against Get-ADComputer's OperatingSystem string. Note 2012 R2 is supported
        # and plain 2012 is NOT, so the R2 pattern must not be loosened to bare '2012'.
        SupportedDomainControllerOs = @(
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2025'; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2025' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2022'; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2022' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2019'; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2019' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2016'; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2016' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2012 R2'; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2012\s*r2' }
        )
        # "Read-only GCs and read-only DCs aren't supported" - same source.
        ReadOnlySupported           = $false
    }
    # --- dcdiag tests that change state (PORT-PLAN H20) ---------------------------------------
    # Tests in the dcdiag grid that Microsoft documents as doing something beyond reading. They
    # are NOT run unless -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests is given; by default their cells read
    # Not Assessed and the row says why. Kept as data, with the vendor's own words and where
    # they were read, so adding a test to this list is a config edit with its evidence beside it.
    DcdiagActiveTests       = @(
        @{ Test     = 'DNS'
           Effect   = 'its dynamic update test registers a test record in the DNS zone and then deletes it'
           Quote    = 'perform registration of a test record (_dcdiag_test_record) ... The test record is deleted automatically after the test.'
           Source   = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/troubleshoot/verify-dns-functionality-to-support-directory-replication'
           ReadDate = '2026-11-13' }
        @{ Test     = 'Intersite'
           Effect   = 'it triggers a replication'
           Quote    = 'Reporting back any replication failures after triggering a replication.'
           Source   = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag'
           ReadDate = '2026-11-13' }
        # PORT-PLAN C11. Learn's description says "writing". It does not say what is written, and the
        # wording may be a documentation error; until Microsoft says the test only reads, it is
        # treated as changing state, by the H20 rule. Operator to confirm (PORT-PLAN C11a).
        @{ Test     = 'SystemLog'
           Effect   = 'Microsoft describes it as reading and writing System log entries'
           Quote    = 'Validates the System Event Log''s health by reading and writing entries from the past 60 minutes for any errors and warnings.'
           Source   = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag'
           ReadDate = '2026-11-16' }
    )
    # --- dcdiag: which tests run, at which scope, and how their output is read (PORT-PLAN C11) --
    # Every test named below is in the "DCDiag known tests" table of the dcdiag command reference,
    # except FsmoCheck, which is flagged (InLearnTable = $false) and kept only because the grid had
    # it before this row (PORT-PLAN C11c):
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag
    #   Read: 2026-11-16.
    # Learn publishes no table of the scope each test runs at. The scope here follows the blocks of
    # Learn's own sample output: per-DC tests under "Testing server: <site>\<DC>", CheckSDRefDom and
    # CrossRefValidation under "Running partition tests on : <NC>", Intersite under "Running
    # enterprise tests on : <forest>". Topology's block is not in the sample; it is run once per
    # domain and every verdict it prints is counted. The parser attributes each verdict to the block
    # it is printed in, whatever this table says, so a partition or enterprise result never becomes
    # the failure of a DC it is not about: a partition verdict counts for the DC asked only for a
    # test with PartitionParts, an enterprise verdict only for a test with EnterpriseParts.
    #
    # The test lists cannot be set from a file: the "DcDiag" key of -ConfigOverridePath accepts only
    # TestTimeoutSeconds and Retries, so a file can never add a test that changes state. Tests that
    # Microsoft documents as changing state are in DcdiagActiveTests above and run only with
    # -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests. /fix, /c, /e, /u, /p, /skip and the MachineAccount repair options
    # are never passed.
    DcDiag                  = @{
        Version            = '1.29.0'
        SourceUrl          = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag'
        ReadDate           = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource    = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value'
        # Microsoft publishes no dcdiag run time or time-out ("Domain controllers and DNS servers that
        # are offline will increase run times due to long time-out periods for RPC and other
        # protocols"). $null takes -ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds (90 s unless set) and -Retries (2):
        # tool defaults, not Microsoft values. A file can set either, 5-600 s and 1-10 attempts.
        TestTimeoutSeconds = $null
        Retries            = $null
        TimeoutSource      = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value (-ExternalToolTimeoutSeconds)'
        # Per DC, in the reference order (AdAssessment26 SPEC, per-DC order). Connectivity first:
        # "The connectivity test can't be skipped", so it runs in every invocation anyway, and when it
        # fails on a DC no further test is started there. PartitionParts: a verdict this test prints
        # in a partition block is about THIS server's replicas, so it counts for the DC that was
        # asked. EnterpriseParts: the test is one of dcdiag's enterprise tests, whose verdict is
        # printed under "Running enterprise tests on : <forest>" with the forest as its subject
        # (Learn's sample prints LocatorCheck and Intersite there), so in a "/s:<this DC>"
        # invocation without /e that verdict is the enterprise test run from THIS DC and counts for
        # it. Without the flag an enterprise verdict counted for no DC, which left FsmoCheck Not
        # Assessed on every DC and read a failed DNS summary as Pass (independent review, C11).
        # EventLog: the test reads a remote event log, and a log that "could not be queried"
        # is an instrument failure (KB 2512643), not a finding.
        PerDcTests         = @(
            @{ Test = 'Connectivity'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Verifies that the DSA and DNS are registered and reachable using LDAP and RPC.' }
            @{ Test = 'Replications'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $true; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'This test checks all AD replication connection objects for all naming contexts on specified DC(s)' }
            @{ Test = 'Advertising'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks whether each domain controller advertises itself in the roles that it should be capable of performing.' }
            @{ Test = 'Services'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'This test validates that various AD-dependent services are running, accessible, and set to specific start types.' }
            # Learn spells it NetLogons; dcdiag test names are matched without regard to case, and the
            # grid column keeps the spelling it has had since v1.0.
            @{ Test = 'Netlogons'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Validates that the user running DCDiag can connect to and read the SYSVOL and NETLOGON shares without any security errors.' }
            @{ Test = 'SysVolCheck'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'This test reads the DCs Netlogon SysVolReady registry key to validate that SYSVOL is ready.' }
            @{ Test = 'DFSREvent'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $true
               Learn = 'checking the DFSR event log warning and error entries from the past 24 hours. This test uses RPC and EventLog Remoting Protocol.' }
            @{ Test = 'FrsEvent'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $true
               Learn = 'Checks to see if there are errors in the File Replication Service (FRS) event log from the past 24 hours' }
            @{ Test = 'KccEvent'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $true
               Learn = 'This test queries the KCC on the DC for errors and warnings generated in the Directory Services event log during the last 15 minutes.' }
            # PORT-PLAN C6e. Learn describes RidManager as checks only, over LDAP and RPC; it does not
            # say in so many words that the test never requests a RID pool. Kept in the default list
            # on that description, read 2026-11-16.
            @{ Test = 'RidManager'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks whether the relative identifier (RID) master is accessible and if: It contains the proper information. Can be located and contacted through a DsBind. Has valid RID pool values. ... This test uses LDAP and RPC.'
               Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag'
               ReadDate = '2026-11-16' }
            @{ Test = 'KnowsOfRoleHolders'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'This test returns the DCs knowledge of the five Flexible Single Master Operation (FSMO) roles' }
            @{ Test = 'MachineAccount'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks whether the machine account has properly registered and that the services are advertised using LDAP and RPC over SMB' }
            @{ Test = 'ObjectsReplicated'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks that the Machine Account and Directory System Agent (DSA) objects have replicated.' }
            @{ Test = 'NCSecDesc'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks permissions on all the naming contexts (such as Schema, Configuration, etc.) on the source DC ... This test uses LDAP.' }
            @{ Test = 'VerifyReferences'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks that certain system references are intact for the FRS and replication infrastructure.' }
            @{ Test = 'VerifyReplicas'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $true; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks that all application directory partitions are fully instantiated on all replica servers. It verifies that the specified server hosts the application partitions specified by its crossref attributes' }
            # NOT in Learn's table: Learn mentions it only in a legacy Windows 2000 upgrade article.
            # Kept because the grid had it; PORT-PLAN C11c decides whether it stays. EnterpriseParts:
            # Microsoft publishes no FsmoCheck output; field captures print its verdict in the
            # enterprise block, like LocatorCheck and Intersite in Learn's sample, and a verdict in
            # the server block is counted too. To be confirmed against a real capture (C11b, C17).
            @{ Test = 'FsmoCheck'; InLearnTable = $false; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $true; EventLog = $false
               Learn = '' }
            @{ Test = 'CheckSecurityError'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Test is not run by default. Performs various security checks for errors related to the security components of the DC' }
            @{ Test = 'VerifyEnterpriseReferences'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Checks that specified system references are intact for the FRS and replication infrastructure across all objects in the enterprise on each domain controller.' }
            # Opt-in (DcdiagActiveTests): Learn says "reading and writing entries".
            @{ Test = 'SystemLog'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $false; EventLog = $true
               Learn = 'Validates the System Event Log''s health by reading and writing entries from the past 60 minutes for any errors and warnings.' }
            # Opt-in (DcdiagActiveTests): the dynamic update test registers a test record.
            # EnterpriseParts: Learn calls it "enterprise-wide DNS health checks"; its summary
            # verdict is counted with the server-block one, and "failed" wins.
            @{ Test = 'DNS'; InLearnTable = $true; PartitionParts = $false; EnterpriseParts = $true; EventLog = $false
               Learn = 'Tests enterprise-wide DNS health checks using DNS, RPC, and WMI protocols. Not ran by default and must be explicitly requested.' }
        )
        # Once per scoped domain, against a writable DC of that domain.
        DomainTests        = @(
            @{ Test = 'Topology'; InLearnTable = $true
               Learn = 'uses ... DsReplicaSyncAll with the flag DS_REPSYNCALL_DO_NOT_SYNC, meaning that it analyzes and validates replication topology without actually replicating changes.' }
        )
        # Once per forest: both read the crossRef objects in CN=Partitions,CN=Configuration, which every
        # DC holds, so any DC can answer; one of the forest root domain is asked first.
        ForestTests        = @(
            @{ Test = 'CheckSDRefDom'; InLearnTable = $true
               Learn = 'validates cross reference objects located in cn=partitions,cn=configuration,dc=<forest root domain> contain the correct domain names in their msDS-SDReferenceDomain attributes.' }
            @{ Test = 'CrossRefValidation'; InLearnTable = $true
               Learn = 'This test looks at the nCName, dnsRoot, nETBIOSName, and systemFlags attributes' }
        )
        # Once per site that holds a DC, with /a, only with -IncludeDcdiagActiveTests (it triggers a
        # replication; DcdiagActiveTests above).
        SiteTests          = @(
            @{ Test = 'Intersite'; InLearnTable = $true
               Learn = 'The /a or /e parameter must be used as not providing a site would allow the test to run but skips actual testing.' }
        )
        # Never run, and why.
        NotAdded           = @(
            # Report: the catalogue has a row for it (DCDIAG-009), so the section says it was not run.
            @{ Test = 'LocatorCheck'; Report = $true
               Why = 'not in the known-tests table ("DCDiag known tests") of the Microsoft Learn dcdiag reference; it appears only in that page''s sample output, with no description' }
            @{ Test = 'CutoffServers'; Why = 'Microsoft documents that it uses DsReplicaSyncAll, "which triggers replication on the DCs"' }
            @{ Test = 'DcPromo'; Why = 'a promotion pre-check that runs locally only and needs promotion arguments' }
            @{ Test = 'RegisterInDNS'; Why = 'a registration pre-check that runs locally only and needs /DnsDomain' }
            @{ Test = 'OutboundSecureChannels'; Why = 'needs /testdomain; the Trusts and DcSecureChannel sections test secure channels' }
        )
        # The en-US lines the parser reads, from the dcdiag reference's sample output (Learn publishes
        # en-US output only). A verdict may wrap, so "\s+" spans the line break before the test name.
        OutputMarkers      = @{
            ServerBlock     = '(?im)^[ \t]*Testing server:[ \t]*(?:[^\\\r\n]*\\)?(?<name>[^\s\\]+)'
            PartitionBlock  = '(?im)^[ \t]*Running partition tests on[ \t]*:[ \t]*(?<name>\S+)'
            EnterpriseBlock = '(?im)^[ \t]*Running enterprise tests on[ \t]*:[ \t]*(?<name>\S+)'
            Verdict         = '(?i)(?:(?<subject>\S+)[ \t]+)?(?<verdict>passed|failed)\s+test\s+(?<test>[A-Za-z0-9]+)\b'
            Recognised      = @('(?im)^[ \t]*Directory Server Diagnosis', '(?im)^[ \t]*Performing initial setup', '(?im)^[ \t]*Doing (initial required|primary) tests', '(?im)^[ \t]*Starting test:')
        }
        # A remote event log that could not be read. The sample lines are Microsoft's (KB 2512643),
        # which calls them expected errors caused by firewall rules.
        EventLogUnreadablePatterns = @(
            @{ Pattern = '(?i)\bcould not be queried\b'; Provisional = $false
               Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/identity/dcdiag-commands-running-errors'; ReadDate = '2026-11-16' }
        )
        # PROVISIONAL: Microsoft publishes no dcdiag text for a server it cannot reach (a Learn search
        # on 2026-11-16 found none). Used only to explain a test that printed NO verdict; never to
        # overrule one. To be confirmed against a real capture (PORT-PLAN C11b, with C17).
        UnreachablePatterns = @(
            @{ Pattern = '(?i)Ldap search capability attribute search failed'; Provisional = $true }
            @{ Pattern = '(?i)\bcould not be contacted\b'; Provisional = $true }
        )
        # ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED's system message is "Access is denied." Used only to explain a test that
        # printed NO verdict: a replication error 5 under a failed Replications verdict is a finding
        # about the DC (KB 3073945), not about the account running this tool.
        AccessDeniedPatterns = @(
            @{ Pattern = '(?i)\bAccess is denied\b'; Provisional = $false
               Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/debug/system-error-codes--0-499-'; ReadDate = '2026-11-16' }
        )
        # --- The DNS test's summary table, one row per test group (PORT-PLAN C12, closes H15) ---
        # Read from the output of the DNS test C11 already runs ("/DnsAll /s:<DC>", opt-in): no new
        # invocation, no new switch. The only Learn page that shows the table is KB 2102154 (below):
        # a header "Auth Basc Forw Del  Dyn  RReg Ext" with uneven spacing, "Domain: <fqdn>" lines,
        # one row per DC with its short name and one cell per column (PASS, FAIL, n/a), then
        # "Enterprise DNS infrastructure test results:" and the overall verdict. The sample was
        # taken with /v /e; whether a "/s:<DC>" run prints the same table, only that DC's row, the
        # "Domain:" lines, the WARN token and what n/a means are NOT published and are confirmed in
        # the first real capture (PORT-PLAN C12a, with C17). The header is found by its tokens, never
        # by column position; cells are split on whitespace and mapped to the columns by order; a
        # row whose cell count is not the header's is not graded. /x (XML) is not used: Microsoft
        # documents it only as "Redirects all output to xmllog.xml" and publishes no schema (H15).
        # This table cannot be set from a file: the DcDiag key of -ConfigOverridePath refuses it.
        DnsSummary         = @{
            SourceUrl             = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/replication-error-1722-rpc-server-unavailable'
            ReadDate              = '2026-11-16'
            LayoutSource          = 'one sample on Learn (KB 2102154, from the DNS test run with /v /e): header tokens and order, Domain lines, cell values PASS, FAIL and n/a; spacing, a separator line and the layout under /s: from field captures, to be confirmed in the first real capture (PORT-PLAN C12a)'
            # A header is a line whose whitespace-separated tokens are all column tokens below, none
            # twice, and at least this many. Microsoft publishes no such rule.
            HeaderMinTokens       = 4
            HeaderMinTokensSource = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value: more than half the seven columns, so a line of prose that happens to hold one or two of the words is never read as the header'
            # "Domain: contoso.com" in Learn's sample; the name is kept with the row.
            DomainLine            = '(?i)^Domain:\s*(?<name>\S+)'
            # The table ends at the enterprise block (Learn's sample), at a verdict line or at a block
            # header of the OutputMarkers above, whichever comes first.
            EndMarkers            = @('(?i)^Enterprise DNS infrastructure test results', '(?i)\b(passed|failed)\s+test\b')
            # A line of underscores, dashes or equals signs under the header: not in Learn's sample;
            # field captures show one. Skipped, never read as a row.
            SeparatorLine         = '^[_=\-\s]+$'
            # Column order and names: Learn KB 2102154 ("There are seven test groups for this command",
            # of which it lists six). What each test checks: the dcdiag reference, "DNS syntax",
            #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag
            # The Enterprise Admins minimum for the DNS tests:
            #   https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/active-directory-replication-event-id-2087
            #   Read: 2026-11-16 (all).
            # NaStatus: what n/a in this column grades to, when it is not the Cells default below.
            Categories            = @(
                @{ Token = 'Auth'; Name = 'Authentication'; InLearn = $true; Switch = ''
                   Learn = 'Authentication (Auth) is the first of the DNS test groups; Microsoft documents that membership in Enterprise Admins, or equivalent, is the minimum requirement to complete the DNS tests (KB event 2087 page)' }
                @{ Token = 'Basc'; Name = 'Basic'; InLearn = $true; Switch = '/DnsBasic'
                   Learn = 'Performs basic DNS tests, including network connectivity, DNS client configuration, service availability, and zone existence' }
                @{ Token = 'Forw'; Name = 'Forwarders/Root hints'; InLearn = $true; Switch = '/DnsForwarders'
                   Learn = 'Performs the /DnsBasic tests and also checks the configuration of forwarders' }
                @{ Token = 'Del'; Name = 'Delegations'; InLearn = $true; Switch = '/DnsDelegation'
                   Learn = 'Performs the /DnsBasic tests and also checks for proper delegations' }
                @{ Token = 'Dyn'; Name = 'Dynamic update'; InLearn = $true; Switch = '/DnsDynamicUpdate'
                   Learn = 'Performs /DnsBasic tests and also determines if dynamic update is enabled in the Active Directory zone' }
                @{ Token = 'RReg'; Name = 'Records registration'; InLearn = $true; Switch = '/DnsRecordRegistration'
                   Learn = 'Performs the /DnsBasic tests and also checks if the address (A), canonical name (CNAME) and well-known service (SRV) resource records are registered' }
                # Ext: Learn never names this column. That it is the external name resolution test is
                # an INFERENCE: /DnsAll "Performs all tests, except for the /DnsResolveExtName test",
                # and Learn's /DnsAll sample prints n/a under Ext on every DC. Under the /DnsAll this
                # tool runs, n/a here is "not requested", graded Info as catalogue DNSDIAG-007 says.
                @{ Token = 'Ext'; Name = 'External name resolution (inferred: Learn does not name this column)'; InLearn = $false; Switch = '/DnsResolveExtName'
                   Learn = 'Resolves <www.microsoft.com> (or /DnsInternetName); /DnsAll performs all tests except this one'
                   NaStatus = 'Info'
                   # Graded as catalogue DNSDIAG-007 says (PASS Pass, WARN Warning, FAIL Fail), with
                   # Microsoft's caveat (archived Windows Server 2003 SP1 dcdiag page, cc776854, read
                   # 2026-11-16). Under /DnsAll only n/a is expected here.
                   ResultNote = 'Microsoft notes that the external name resolution test cannot resolve external Internet names in an environment where a proxy server is being used (archived Windows Server 2003 SP1 dcdiag page), so this result depends on whether the domain controller can reach the internet.'
                   NaMeaning = 'not requested: /DnsAll performs all tests except /DnsResolveExtName (Microsoft, dcdiag reference), and /DnsAll is what this tool runs. Not applicable to this run; this is not a pass' }
            )
            # Cell values, compared without regard to case. PASS, FAIL and n/a are in Learn's sample.
            # WARN is not: two Learn pages say only, in prose, to note the DCs that report "Warn" or
            # "Fail" in the summary table (event 2087 page; Verify DNS functionality page). Any other
            # value is "cell value not recognised", Not Assessed, never Pass.
            # n/a: Microsoft does not publish what it means. Not Assessed under every column except
            # one with its own NaStatus (Ext), as catalogue DNSDIAG-001..006 say ("n/a -> Not
            # Assessed"); operator to confirm (PORT-PLAN C12b).
            Cells                 = @(
                @{ Token = 'PASS'; Status = 'Pass'; Provisional = $false }
                @{ Token = 'FAIL'; Status = 'Fail'; Provisional = $false }
                @{ Token = 'WARN'; Status = 'Warning'; Provisional = $true }
                @{ Token = 'n/a'; Status = 'Not Assessed'; Provisional = $false }
            )
        }
    }
    # --- DC system: OS lifecycle, and patch-level inventory (PORT-PLAN C1) ------------------
    # Volatile vendor facts, replaceable from a file via -DcSystemConfigPath.
    #
    # Dates are CALENDAR dates as Microsoft prints them on the two pages below. The product
    # lifecycle pages print the same instants as Pacific Time timestamps that fall on the next
    # calendar day ("1/13/2027 6:59:59 AM" for the date both pages below call 2027-01-12), so
    # the two conventions must not be mixed. The end date itself counts as still supported; the
    # day after it is past.
    #
    #   Extended support end, 2016 / 2019 / 2022 / 2025, and their builds (14393 / 17763 /
    #   20348 / 26100):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/get-started/windows-server-release-info
    #   Extended support end for 2012 and 2012 R2 (one row, "Windows Server 2012/R2"), and every
    #   Extended Security Updates (ESU) end date:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/lifecycle/faq/extended-security-updates
    #   Read: 2026-11-13 (both)
    #
    # EsuEnd is the Year 3 end date where an ESU programme is published, and $null where none
    # is: no ESU row exists for 2019, 2022 or 2025, so no date is invented for them. The ESU FAQ
    # contradicts itself for 2016 - its table says extended support ends January 12, 2027 and
    # one prose answer says January 12, 2026. The table agrees with the release-information
    # page and with the product lifecycle page, so the table's date is the one used.
    #
    # 2012 and 2012 R2 carry no Build: the release-information page has no section for them, so
    # they are matched by name. The 2012 pattern must not match 2012 R2.
    DcSystem                = @{
        # Both pages, because a finding cites this for every date it states and neither page
        # holds them all: the first has no 2012 rows, the second no 2019, 2022 or 2025 rows.
        SourceUrl   = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/get-started/windows-server-release-info and https://learn.microsoft.com/lifecycle/faq/extended-security-updates'
        ReadDate    = '2026-11-13'
        # Ports probed before a remote read: WinRM over HTTP, then the RPC endpoint mapper for
        # the DCOM fallback. The same two values the rest of this script already uses. NOT
        # replaceable from -DcSystemConfigPath (PORT-PLAN C3g): New-CimSession is opened without
        # -Port, so these are the ports it connects to, and a file could only have changed the probe.
        #   https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/cimcmdlets/new-cimsession (-Port: "Before
        #   using an alternate port, you must configure the WinRM listener on the remote computer")
        #   https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/cimcmdlets/new-cimsessionoption (-Protocol
        #   Dcom, Default or Wsman; no port option in either parameter set). Read: 2026-11-17.
        WsmanPort   = 5985
        RpcPort     = 135
        OsLifecycle = @(
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2025';    Build = 26100; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2025';             ExtendedEnd = '2034-11-14'; EsuEnd = $null }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2022';    Build = 20348; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2022';             ExtendedEnd = '2031-10-14'; EsuEnd = $null }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2019';    Build = 17763; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2019';             ExtendedEnd = '2029-01-09'; EsuEnd = $null }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2016';    Build = 14393; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2016';             ExtendedEnd = '2027-01-12'; EsuEnd = '2030-01-08' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2012 R2'; Build = $null; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2012\s*r2';        ExtendedEnd = '2023-10-10'; EsuEnd = '2026-10-13' }
            @{ Label = 'Windows Server 2012';    Build = $null; Pattern = '(?i)windows server\s*2012(?!\s*r2)';    ExtendedEnd = '2023-10-10'; EsuEnd = '2026-10-13' }
        )
    }
    # --- DC disk space: NTDS database, NTDS log and SYSVOL volumes (PORT-PLAN C2) ----------
    # THE FOUR THRESHOLDS ARE TOOL DEFAULTS, NOT MICROSOFT VALUES, and every finding says so.
    # Microsoft publishes no percentage and no absolute free-space threshold for the volumes
    # that hold the directory database, its logs or SYSVOL. The capacity planning page says that
    # space available "is irrelevant for most AD environments", and the three sizing figures it
    # gives are all relative to the DATABASE size, not to the volume:
    #   "To enable offline defrag, make sure the free space you have available is 110% of the
    #    NTDS.dit size."
    #   "If you have the storage for it, allocating enough free space to equal 300% of the DIT
    #    size is a safe way to accommodate growth and defragging."
    #   For a fixed VHD: "The disk should be at least 210% the size of the DIT ... This fixed
    #    VHD size includes 100% of the DIT size plus 110% free space."
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/performance-tuning/role/active-directory-server/capacity-planning-for-active-directory-domain-services
    #   Read: 2026-11-13
    # Each needs the size of the ntds.dit FILE, which this section does not read, so none is
    # evaluated. The finding names the 110 % one, the minimum of the three, as not measured
    # (PORT-PLAN C2a).
    #
    # How the defaults were chosen. 20 % / 10 % is the pair the AdAssessment26 catalogue lists
    # for this check as operator-set (ADA-DISK-001). The absolute floor is this tool's addition,
    # because a percentage alone misleads on a small volume - 25 % of an 8 GB volume is 2 GB.
    # The floor is kept low (5 GB / 2 GB) so that a dedicated database or log volume of 10 to
    # 20 GB, a common and correct design, is not flagged while its percentage is healthy. A
    # volume smaller than the floor can never pass it; set both floors to 0 to switch them off.
    # Either test is enough: the worse of the two results is reported, with which one fired.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "DcDisk": the four
    # thresholds. Paths, SourceUrl and ReadDate are not replaceable, and the two ports are refused
    # (PORT-PLAN C3g: the CIM session is opened on them, without -Port; see the DcSystem table).
    DcDisk                  = @{
        ThresholdSource  = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value'
        WarnBelowPercent = 20
        FailBelowPercent = 10
        # 1 GB = 1024^3 bytes. 0 = no floor.
        WarnBelowFreeGB  = 5
        FailBelowFreeGB  = 2
        # Ports probed before a remote read: WinRM over HTTP, then the RPC endpoint mapper for
        # the DCOM fallback. The same two values the DcSystem section uses. Not replaceable (C3g).
        WsmanPort        = 5985
        RpcPort          = 135
        # Vendor facts: the registry values that name the three locations. No LDAP attribute
        # publishes them; they exist only in the registry of each DC.
        #   "NTDS database files: The registry key HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\CurrentControlSet\
        #    Services\NTDS\Parameters\DSA Database File specifies the location." and
        #   "AD DS transaction log files: ... \NTDS\Parameters\Database Log Files Path specifies
        #    the location.":
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-endpoint/microsoft-defender-antivirus-exclusions-windows-server
        #   "... the Netlogon service's SysVol registry key: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\
        #    CurrentControlSet\Services\NetLogon\Parameters\SysVol". After a migration to DFSR
        #    the value points at the SYSVOL_DFSR copy, so it is used as read:
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/vss/backing-up-and-restoring-an-frs-replicated-sysvol-folder
        #   Read: 2026-11-13 (both)
        # The first page prints the SysVol value under ControlSet001; the second prints it under
        # CurrentControlSet, which is what is read here. The registry TYPE of the three values
        # (REG_SZ or REG_EXPAND_SZ) is not published, and neither is the meaning of an absent
        # value: an absent value is reported as not found, never replaced by a default path.
        # "DSA Database File" is the path of a FILE; the other two are folders.
        SourceUrl        = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-endpoint/microsoft-defender-antivirus-exclusions-windows-server and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/vss/backing-up-and-restoring-an-frs-replicated-sysvol-folder'
        ReadDate         = '2026-11-13'
        Paths            = @(
            @{ Role = 'NTDS database'; Kind = 'Database'; Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters';     ValueName = 'DSA Database File' }
            @{ Role = 'NTDS logs';     Kind = 'Logs';     Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters';     ValueName = 'Database Log Files Path' }
            @{ Role = 'SYSVOL';        Kind = 'Sysvol';   Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\Netlogon\Parameters'; ValueName = 'SysVol' }
        )
    }
    # --- DC hardening: LDAP signing and channel binding, SMB signing, LAN Manager level (PORT-PLAN C3) ---
    # Six registry values per DC, read with StdRegProv GetDWORDValue, each graded against the
    # value Microsoft's security baselines expect on a domain controller. A row shows the policy
    # CONFIGURED in the registry of the DC, not what a client and the DC agree on the wire.
    #
    # Every URL below was read on 2026-11-13, except where its own line gives another date. What
    # each row holds:
    #   Key, ValueName   where the value is. Not replaceable from a file.
    #   KeyNote          said in every row of the setting, where the registry path itself is not
    #                    a published fact (LDAPServerIntegrity, below). Not replaceable.
    #   Meanings         the numbers Microsoft publishes a meaning for. A number that is not
    #                    listed is reported Not Assessed, never graded.
    #   ExpectedMinimum  the lowest number that passes. In all six settings a higher documented
    #                    number is stricter, so it passes too. Replaceable from a file, but only
    #                    with one of ExpectedChoices.
    #   OtherStatus      what a value below the expectation is reported as: Warning (the rule the
    #                    AdAssessment26 catalogue gives for these rows) or Fail. Replaceable.
    #                    A TOOL DEFAULT, NOT A MICROSOFT VALUE: Microsoft publishes the value it
    #                    expects, and this tool chooses what to call a DC that misses it. Every
    #                    row graded with it says so.
    #   Defaults         what Microsoft documents for a value that is NOT SET, by OS build. The
    #                    only boundary it publishes is "Windows Server 2022 or earlier" against
    #                    "Windows Server 2025 or later", so the rows are keyed on build 20348 and
    #                    below, and 26100 and above; a build between the two, or one that was not
    #                    read, has no documented default and is reported Not Assessed. Known =
    #                    $false is a row that says why no default is known for those builds.
    #                    Value is the number of the default where Microsoft publishes it; where
    #                    it names the default only as a policy choice, Value is $null and Meets
    #                    says whether that choice meets the expectation.
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled so wherever a finding relies on it:
    #   - The registry path of LDAPServerIntegrity for AD DS. Learn gives the path only for
    #     AD LDS (Services\<InstanceName>\Parameters); NTDS\Parameters is where it publishes the
    #     two sibling values (LdapEnforceChannelBinding, LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity), and it
    #     names LDAPServerIntegrity=2 as the domain controller setting in the ADV190023 article.
    #   - What LDAPServerIntegrity = 1 means, and which number the policy choice "None" stores.
    #     Learn publishes 0 ("Disables signing (default)", for AD LDS) and 2 ("Requires signing").
    #   - A value table for LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity. The Windows Server 2025 baseline expects
    #     1 and the policy has the choices Enabled / Disabled / Not Configured; 1 = Enabled and
    #     0 = Disabled is inferred from that.
    #   - What an absent RequireSecuritySignature means on a domain controller, on any build.
    #   - The default LmCompatibilityLevel, and the meaning of its levels, on Windows Server 2025.
    #   - The default LdapEnforceChannelBinding on a Windows Server 2025 DC that was upgraded.
    # EnableSecuritySignature is deliberately not read: "The EnableSecuritySignature registry
    # setting for SMB2 and later clients and servers is ignored" (smb-signing-overview).
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "DcHardening": per setting
    # id, ExpectedMinimum and OtherStatus. Nothing else. WsmanPort and RpcPort are NOT replaceable
    # for this section: the session Get-AdfaDcHardeningRaw opens always goes to the default WinRM
    # port, or to the RPC endpoint mapper for DCOM, so a file that changed the port would change
    # what is probed and not what is connected to. A file that names either is refused.
    DcHardening             = @{
        SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025'
        ReadDate  = '2026-11-13'
        # Ports probed before a remote read: WinRM over HTTP, then the RPC endpoint mapper for
        # the DCOM fallback. The same two values the DcSystem and DcDisk sections use. They are
        # the ports the CIM session itself uses by default, and they cannot be set from a file.
        WsmanPort = 5985
        RpcPort   = 135
        # --- The SMB server configuration: the SMBv1 row and the second instrument of SMB server
        # signing (PORT-PLAN C3f). Provenance only: the cmdlet is called by its literal name in
        # Get-AdfaDcHardeningRaw, where a test reads it from the parsed source, and nothing here
        # can be changed from a file (a 'SmbServer' key in an override is refused).
        # Every URL below was read on 2026-11-17.
        #   Get-SmbServerConfiguration: "Retrieves the SMB server configuration." Syntax
        #   "[-CimSession <CimSession[]>] [-ThrottleLimit <Int32>] [-AsJob] [-WhatIf] [-Confirm]";
        #   -CimSession "Runs the cmdlet in a remote session or on a remote computer. Enter a
        #   computer name or a session object, such as the output of a New-CimSession"; output
        #   "The MSFT_SmbServerConfiguration object represents the configuration of the SMB server."
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/smbshare/get-smbserverconfiguration
        #   Detection of SMBv1 on the server: "Get-SmbServerConfiguration | Select EnableSMB1Protocol"
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/troubleshoot/detect-enable-and-disable-smbv1-v2-v3
        #   EnableSMB1Protocol "Controls whether SMB1 protocol is enabled." and
        #   RequireSecuritySignature "Controls whether security signature is required.", both
        #   boolean (archived class page):
        #     https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/desktop/smb/msft-smbserverconfiguration
        # Not published, and so not claimed: whether the module runs under Windows PowerShell 5.1
        # and PowerShell 7 (MEASURED on one Windows 11 workstation, not a DC, both: yes), the
        # rights a remote run needs, and the error codes it raises (PORT-PLAN C17a).
        SmbServer = @{
            Instrument = 'Get-SmbServerConfiguration'
            Module     = 'SmbShare'
            Properties = @('EnableSMB1Protocol', 'RequireSecuritySignature')
            SourceUrl  = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/smbshare/get-smbserverconfiguration ; https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/troubleshoot/detect-enable-and-disable-smbv1-v2-v3'
            ReadDate   = '2026-11-17'
        }
        Settings  = @(
            @{
                Id = 'LdapServerIntegrity'; Catalogue = 'ADA-LDAP-001'; Item = 'LDAP server signing'
                Policy = 'Domain controller: LDAP server signing requirements'
                # Path inferred for AD DS, see above. KeyNote puts that in every row of the setting.
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters'; ValueName = 'LDAPServerIntegrity'
                KeyNote = 'The registry path of this value is inferred, not published by Microsoft for AD DS: Microsoft Learn gives the path of LDAPServerIntegrity for AD LDS only, and NTDS\Parameters, read here, is where it publishes the two related values LdapEnforceChannelBinding and LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity.'
                #   "The LDAPServerIntegrity value accepts the following values: 0: Disables
                #    signing (default). 2: Requires signing." (for AD LDS)
                #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/manage-ldap-signing-group-policy
                #   "The LDAPServerIntegrity=2 setting ... enforces the use of signing by the client."
                #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/ldap-session-security-settings-requirements-adv190023
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'signing disabled - published for AD LDS only'
                    '1' = 'meaning not published on Microsoft Learn; it is not 2, the value that requires signing'
                    '2' = 'Require signing'
                }
                ExpectedMinimum = 2; ExpectedChoices = @(2); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                #   "We recommend that you set Domain controller: LDAP server signing requirements
                #    to Require signature."
                ExpectedSource = 'Microsoft recommends Require signing: https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/domain-controller-ldap-server-signing-requirements (read 2026-11-13)'
                AppliesFromBuild = $null
                # On Windows Server 2025 and later the enforcement value decides with this one:
                #   "The Domain controller: LDAP server signing requirements enforcement policy
                #    takes precedence over the Domain controller: LDAP server signing
                #    requirements policy."
                OverriddenBy = 'LdapServerEnforceIntegrity'; OverriddenByValueName = 'LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity'; OverriddenFromBuild = 26100
                OverrideOnValue = 1; OverrideOffValue = 0
                Defaults = @(
                    #   "LDAP server signing: optional by default; domain controllers accept both
                    #    signed and unsigned LDAP binds." and policy page "DC Effective Default
                    #    Settings | None". The number that "None" stores is not published.
                    @{ MinBuild = $null; MaxBuild = 20348; Known = $true; Value = $null; Meets = $false
                       Label = 'None: the domain controller accepts both signed and unsigned LDAP binds'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-signing (read 2026-11-13)' }
                    #   "No server policy set: if no LDAP server signing policy exists, Windows
                    #    Server 2025 requires LDAP signing by default." Used only where the
                    #    enforcement value is not set either; the verdict function decides.
                    @{ MinBuild = 26100; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $true; Value = $null; Meets = $true
                       Label = 'LDAP signing required, where no LDAP server signing policy exists'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-signing (read 2026-11-13)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/manage-ldap-signing-group-policy'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
            @{
                Id = 'LdapServerEnforceIntegrity'; Catalogue = 'ADA-LDAP-003'; Item = 'LDAP server signing enforcement'
                Policy = 'Domain controller: LDAP server signing requirements enforcement'
                #   "Registry Key: HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters
                #    Registry Value: LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity Registry Value Type: REG_DWORD ...
                #    Domain Controller = Equals(1)"
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters'; ValueName = 'LDAPServerEnforceIntegrity'
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'Disabled - inferred from the baseline row, which expects 1; Microsoft publishes no value table'
                    '1' = 'Enabled'
                }
                ExpectedMinimum = 1; ExpectedChoices = @(1); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                ExpectedSource = 'Windows Server 2025 security baseline, Domain Controller = Equals(1): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025 (read 2026-11-13)'
                # The policy is documented for Windows Server 2025 only; whether an earlier
                # release honours the value is not published.
                AppliesFromBuild = 26100; AppliesFromLabel = 'Windows Server 2025'
                Governs = 'LdapServerIntegrity'; GovernsValueName = 'LDAPServerIntegrity'; GovernedMinimum = 2
                Defaults = @(
                    #   "Default: Not Configured, which has the same effect as Enabled (default
                    #    for new deployments)." Against it: "Existing policies preserved: if you
                    #    have LDAP server-side or client-side signing policies in place, the
                    #    upgrade maintains your current settings" - so the verdict function does
                    #    not apply this default where a legacy signing value below 2 is present.
                    @{ MinBuild = 26100; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $true; Value = $null; Meets = $true
                       Label = 'Not Configured, which has the same effect as Enabled'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/manage-ldap-signing-group-policy (read 2026-11-13)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/manage-ldap-signing-group-policy'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
            @{
                Id = 'LdapEnforceChannelBinding'; Catalogue = 'ADA-LDAP-002'; Item = 'LDAP channel binding'
                Policy = 'Domain controller: LDAP server channel binding token requirements'
                #   "Registry Key: HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters
                #    Registry Value: LdapEnforceChannelBinding Registry Value Type: REG_DWORD ...
                #    Domain Controller = Equals(2)" (Windows Server 2025 baseline)
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters'; ValueName = 'LdapEnforceChannelBinding'
                #   "0 | Never | The server doesn't perform channel binding validation."
                #   "1 | When supported | Clients that support CBT must provide it. Clients that
                #    don't support CBT are still allowed. The server logs auditing events."
                #   "2 | Always | All applicable TLS and SASL clients must provide CBT."
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'Never: the server performs no channel binding validation'
                    '1' = 'When supported: clients that support channel binding must provide it, others are still allowed'
                    '2' = 'Always: clients that provide no valid channel binding token are rejected'
                }
                # 1 is offered as a choice for the audit phase Microsoft describes before Always.
                ExpectedMinimum = 2; ExpectedChoices = @(1, 2); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                # No baseline row for this value was found for Windows Server 2022 and earlier;
                # on those releases the expectation rests on the second page ("For the strongest
                # security posture, use SASL authentication with Kerberos over TLS and enforce
                # channel binding").
                ExpectedSource = 'Windows Server 2025 security baseline, Domain Controller = Equals(2): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025 ; and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-channel-binding (read 2026-11-13)'
                AppliesFromBuild = $null
                Defaults = @(
                    #   "Windows Server 2022 or earlier: Channel binding policy: Set to Never."
                    @{ MinBuild = $null; MaxBuild = 20348; Known = $true; Value = 0; Meets = $null
                       Label = 'Never'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-channel-binding (read 2026-11-13)' }
                    #   "For new Active Directory deployments on Windows Server 2025 or later:
                    #    Channel binding policy: Defaults to When supported." Nothing is said for
                    #    a DC that was upgraded, and the registry does not show which a DC is.
                    @{ MinBuild = 26100; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $false; Value = $null; Meets = $null
                       Reason = 'Microsoft states the default "When supported" only for new Active Directory deployments on Windows Server 2025 or later, and the registry does not show whether this DC was a new deployment or an upgrade'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-channel-binding (read 2026-11-13)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/ldap-channel-binding'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
            @{
                Id = 'SmbServerSigning'; Catalogue = 'ADA-SMB-001'; Item = 'SMB server signing'
                Policy = 'Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)'
                #   "Registry key: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\LanManServer\
                #    Parameters Registry value: RequireSecuritySignature Data Type: REG_DWORD
                #    Data: 0 (disable), 1 (enable)"
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LanManServer\Parameters'; ValueName = 'RequireSecuritySignature'
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'SMB signing not required of clients'
                    '1' = 'SMB signing required of clients'
                }
                ExpectedMinimum = 1; ExpectedChoices = @(1); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                ExpectedSource = 'Windows Server 2025 security baseline, Domain Controller = Equals(1): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025 ; and "By default, domain controllers require SMB signing of anyone connecting to them": https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing-overview (read 2026-11-13)'
                AppliesFromBuild = $null
                # The SMB server's own configuration, read in the same session with the cmdlet
                # Microsoft documents for exactly this check (PORT-PLAN C3f, see SmbServer below):
                #   "If the returned information is True, then SMB signing is enabled, otherwise,
                #    if the returned information is False, then SMB signing is disabled."
                #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing
                #     Read: 2026-11-17.
                SecondInstrument = 'Get-SmbServerConfiguration RequireSecuritySignature'
                Defaults = @(
                    @{ MinBuild = $null; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $false; Value = $null; Meets = $null
                       Reason = 'Microsoft does not publish what an absent value means on a domain controller. Its policy page lists "Default Domain Controller Policy: Enabled" and "DC Effective Default Settings: Enabled" beside "Stand-Alone Server Default Settings: Disabled", so on a DC the requirement normally arrives through Group Policy, which sets the value; and its two pages on Windows Server 2025 disagree on whether inbound signing is required by default'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/microsoft-network-server-digitally-sign-communications-always ; https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing ; https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security-hardening (read 2026-11-13)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing-overview'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
            @{
                Id = 'SmbClientSigning'; Catalogue = 'ADA-SMB-002'; Item = 'SMB client signing'
                Policy = 'Microsoft network client: Digitally sign communications (always)'
                #   "Registry key: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\
                #    LanManWorkstation\Parameters Registry value: RequireSecuritySignature
                #    Data Type: REG_DWORD Data: 0 (disable), 1 (enable)"
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LanManWorkstation\Parameters'; ValueName = 'RequireSecuritySignature'
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'SMB signing not required on outbound connections'
                    '1' = 'SMB signing required on outbound connections'
                }
                ExpectedMinimum = 1; ExpectedChoices = @(1); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                ExpectedSource = 'Windows Server 2025 security baseline, Domain Controller = Equals(1): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025 (read 2026-11-13)'
                AppliesFromBuild = $null
                Defaults = @(
                    #   Policy page: "DC Effective Default Settings | Disabled".
                    @{ MinBuild = $null; MaxBuild = 20348; Known = $true; Value = 0; Meets = $null
                       Label = 'Disabled (DC Effective Default Settings: Disabled)'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/microsoft-network-client-digitally-sign-communications-always (read 2026-11-13)' }
                    #   "Windows Server 2025 requires outbound SMB signing only." (smb-signing) and
                    #   "all outbound and inbound SMB connections are now required to be signed by
                    #    default" (smb-security-hardening): the two pages agree on outbound.
                    @{ MinBuild = 26100; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $true; Value = 1; Meets = $null
                       Label = 'required: Windows Server 2025 requires SMB signing on outbound connections by default'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing ; https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security-hardening (read 2026-11-13)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing-overview'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
            @{
                Id = 'LmCompatibilityLevel'; Catalogue = 'ADA-NTLM-001'; Item = 'LAN Manager authentication level'
                Policy = 'Network security: LAN Manager authentication level'
                #   "HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\LmCompatibilityLevel"
                Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa'; ValueName = 'LmCompatibilityLevel'
                # The meanings are the security policy page's. The Policy CSP page words levels
                # 2 to 5 differently and contradicts itself, so it is not used.
                Meanings = @{
                    '0' = 'Send LM & NTLM responses; domain controllers accept LM, NTLM and NTLMv2'
                    '1' = 'Send LM & NTLM, NTLMv2 session security where the server supports it; domain controllers accept LM, NTLM and NTLMv2'
                    '2' = 'Send NTLM response only; domain controllers accept LM, NTLM and NTLMv2'
                    '3' = 'Send NTLMv2 response only; domain controllers accept LM, NTLM and NTLMv2'
                    '4' = 'Send NTLMv2 response only; domain controllers refuse LM and accept NTLM and NTLMv2'
                    '5' = 'Send NTLMv2 response only; domain controllers refuse LM and NTLM and accept only NTLMv2'
                }
                # 3 is the countermeasure the security policy page names ("Send NTLMv2 responses
                # only"); 5 is what both security baselines and the NTLMv1 audit article give for
                # a domain controller. 5 is the default here; 3 and 4 are offered as choices.
                ExpectedMinimum = 5; ExpectedChoices = @(3, 4, 5); OtherStatus = 'Warning'
                ExpectedSource = 'Windows Server 2025 security baseline, Domain Controller = Equals(5): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/guest-configuration-baseline-windows-server-2025 ; and "To configure a DC to use only NTLMv2 ... Value: 5": https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/audit-domain-controller-ntlmv1 (read 2026-11-13)'
                AppliesFromBuild = $null
                Defaults = @(
                    #   "In Windows Server 2008 R2 and later, this setting is configured to Send
                    #    NTLMv2 responses only." and "DC Effective Default Settings | Send NTLMv2
                    #    response only". Windows Server 2008 R2 is every build above 7100, and
                    #    the release before it, Windows Server 2008 with SP2, is build 6002:
                    #    "Windows Server 2008 R2 | 601 | greater than 7100" (VersionNT, WindowsBuild)
                    #      https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/msi/operating-system-property-values
                    #      Read: 2026-11-15.
                    @{ MinBuild = 7101; MaxBuild = 20348; Known = $true; Value = 3; Meets = $null
                       Label = 'Send NTLMv2 response only (level 3)'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/network-security-lan-manager-authentication-level (read 2026-11-13)' }
                    #   Windows Server 2025, features no longer in development, row "NTLM": "NTLMv1 is
                    #    removed. LANMAN and NTLMv2 are no longer under active feature development
                    #    and are deprecated." The same page lists NTLMv1 under "Features removed"
                    #    for Windows Server 2025. Read: 2026-11-15.
                    @{ MinBuild = 26100; MaxBuild = $null; Known = $false; Value = $null; Meets = $null
                       Reason = 'NTLMv1 is removed in Windows Server 2025, and no Microsoft page read restates the default, or the meaning of the levels, for that release'
                       Source = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/get-started/removed-deprecated-features-windows-server (read 2026-11-15)' }
                )
                SourceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/network-security-lan-manager-authentication-level'; ReadDate = '2026-11-13'
            }
        )
    }
    # --- Windows LAPS: schema, DSRM password backup per DC, computers with no expiry time (PORT-PLAN C4) ---
    # DIRECTORY READS ONLY, and never a password. The section asks for attribute DEFINITIONS in
    # the schema partition, for msDS-Behavior-Version on each domain head, and for a fixed list
    # of non-secret attributes on computer objects. The seven names in NeverRead are never put
    # in a property list and never used in a search filter on a computer object; a test fails
    # if any of them appears in the script outside this table.
    #
    # Every URL below was read on 2026-11-15.
    #   The seven Windows LAPS attribute names, and that msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime is "a
    #   64-bit integer that specifies the currently scheduled password expiration time in UTC"
    #   with SearchFlags 0, while the other six carry SearchFlags 904 (fRODCFilteredAttribute,
    #   fNEVERVALUEAUDIT, fCONFIDENTIAL, fPRESERVEONDELETE). "The Update-LapsADSchema cmdlet adds
    #   the schema attributes to the directory and to the mayContain list on the computer schema
    #   class." msLAPS-CurrentPasswordVersion "is only available when using the Windows Server
    #   2025 forest schema ... it's not installed by running the Update-LapsADSchema cmdlet",
    #   so it is reported and never required. The legacy names, from the mapping table:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-technical-reference
    #   DSRM: "If your domain functional level (DFL) is earlier than 2016 ... You can't configure
    #   domain controllers to manage their local DSRM account", and "any Windows Server 2016 and
    #   earlier domain controllers that you run don't support Windows LAPS":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-scenarios-windows-server-active-directory
    #   msDS-Behavior-Version 7 = DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2016, 10 = DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2025; 8 and 9 are not
    #   defined. CAVEAT: this page is MS-ADTS 6.1.4.4, the FOREST functional level on the
    #   crossRefContainer object. KnownDomainLevels applies its table to the DOMAIN head; the
    #   MS-ADTS page for the domain level was searched for and not found in session (re-checked
    #   2026-11-16), so that the domain table holds the same values is an assumption (PORT-PLAN
    #   C4f). A level that is not in KnownDomainLevels is reported Not Assessed, never graded:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/564dc969-6db3-49b3-891a-f2f8d0a68a7f
    #   Windows Server 2019 = OS build 17763:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/get-started/windows-server-release-info
    #   The OS builds that can run Windows LAPS (CapableBuilds), from the "Applicable OS" row of
    #   every policy: 10.0.17763.4244, 10.0.19041.2784, 10.0.20348.1663, 10.0.22000.1754,
    #   10.0.22621.1480, 10.0.25145 "and later". operatingSystemVersion in the directory carries
    #   the build and not the revision, so "capable" here means the release, not the patch level:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/client-management/mdm/laps-csp
    #   userAccountControl: ACCOUNTDISABLE 0x2, SERVER_TRUST_ACCOUNT 0x2000 (a domain
    #   controller), PARTIAL_SECRETS_ACCOUNT 0x04000000 (a read-only domain controller):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/useraccountcontrol-manipulate-account-properties
    #   A group managed service account is a "Subclass of Computer":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-msds-groupmanagedserviceaccount
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled so wherever a finding relies on it:
    #   - A LAPS-specific statement of who can read the two expiry attributes by default. What
    #     IS published, in general terms: "Default security in Active Directory is set so that
    #     authenticated users have read access to all attributes" (KB 922836, which applies to
    #     Windows Server 2003; re-read 2026-11-16):
    #       https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/mark-attribute-as-confidential
    #     and that the Windows one is not confidential (SearchFlags 0, technical reference
    #     above). Permissions can still differ per organizational unit, and the legacy
    #     attribute's flags are not published. An attribute this account has no right to read
    #     looks exactly like one that is not set, so a row says "no LAPS expiry time visible to
    #     this account".
    #   - That DSRM password backup writes msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime to the computer object
    #     of the DC. The Get-LapsADPassword example shows an ExpirationTimestamp for a DC whose
    #     Source is the DSRM one; no page states it in prose. Every DSRM row calls it an inference.
    #   - Whether DSRM password backup is supported on a read-only domain controller.
    #   - The syntax and searchFlags of ms-Mcs-AdmPwdExpirationTime.
    #   - A marker for cluster and other virtual computer objects. They are left out by having
    #     no operatingSystem value, which is a tool heuristic.
    #   - Any share of computers without LAPS that should warn or fail. The four values below
    #     marked "tool default" are this tool's, and every row that uses one says so.
    #
    # How the defaults were chosen. WarnAbovePercent 0 is the rule the AdAssessment26 catalogue
    # gives (ADA-LAPS-002): any enabled non-DC computer without a LAPS expiry time is a Warning.
    # FailAbovePercent 100 can never be exceeded, so the row never fails unless a file lowers it:
    # this read cannot rule out a device that backs its password up to Microsoft Entra ID, or an
    # attribute it has no right to read, and a Fail would claim more than was measured.
    # SampleLimit 25 bounds the names listed in one row. ExpiredGraceDays 7 is how long after its
    # expiry time a DC's value still counts as current: a managed device rotates at the first
    # policy cycle after expiry, and a DC that was off for a few days is not a finding.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "Laps": WarnAbovePercent,
    # FailAbovePercent, SampleLimit, ExpiredGraceDays. Nothing else - in particular not an
    # attribute name and not NeverRead, or a file could put a secret attribute in a property list.
    Laps                    = @{
        SourceUrl          = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-technical-reference'
        ReadDate           = '2026-11-15'
        ThresholdSource    = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value'
        # Tool defaults, all four.
        WarnAbovePercent   = 0
        FailAbovePercent   = 100
        SampleLimit        = 25
        ExpiredGraceDays   = 7
        # Vendor values.
        MinDomainLevel     = 7
        KnownDomainLevels  = @(0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10)
        MinDsrmDcBuild     = 17763
        AccountDisableFlag = 2
        ServerTrustFlag    = 8192
        PartialSecretsFlag = 67108864
        WindowsExpiry      = 'msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime'
        LegacyExpiry       = 'ms-Mcs-AdmPwdExpirationTime'
        # An attribute every schema defines. It is asked for in the same schema query, so that
        # an answer without the LAPS attributes can be told from an answer that is not an answer.
        SchemaControl      = 'operatingSystemVersion'
        # Set: WindowsLaps = added by Update-LapsADSchema; WindowsServer2025 = arrives with that
        # forest schema; LegacyLaps = added by the legacy product's own schema update.
        SchemaAttributes   = @(
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime';       Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $false }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-Password';                     Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-EncryptedPassword';            Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-EncryptedPasswordHistory';     Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-EncryptedDSRMPassword';        Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-EncryptedDSRMPasswordHistory'; Set = 'WindowsLaps';       Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'msLAPS-CurrentPasswordVersion';       Set = 'WindowsServer2025'; Secret = $true }
            @{ Name = 'ms-Mcs-AdmPwdExpirationTime';         Set = 'LegacyLaps';        Secret = $false }
            @{ Name = 'ms-Mcs-AdmPwd';                       Set = 'LegacyLaps';        Secret = $true }
        )
        # Never in a property list, never in a filter on a computer object.
        NeverRead          = @('msLAPS-Password', 'msLAPS-EncryptedPassword', 'msLAPS-EncryptedPasswordHistory',
            'msLAPS-EncryptedDSRMPassword', 'msLAPS-EncryptedDSRMPasswordHistory', 'msLAPS-CurrentPasswordVersion', 'ms-Mcs-AdmPwd')
        # What is asked of every computer object, besides the expiry attributes the schema holds.
        ComputerProperties = @('operatingSystem', 'operatingSystemVersion', 'userAccountControl', 'lastLogonTimestamp', 'dNSHostName', 'objectClass')
        # MaxBuild $null = no upper bound. 17763 is Windows 10 version 1809 and Windows Server
        # 2019; 19041 and later covers Windows 10 version 2004 and later, Windows Server 2022,
        # Windows 11 and Windows Server 2025. Builds 18362 and 18363 are not listed by Microsoft.
        CapableBuilds      = @(
            @{ MinBuild = 17763; MaxBuild = 17763 }
            @{ MinBuild = 19041; MaxBuild = $null }
        )
        ExcludedClasses    = @('msDS-GroupManagedServiceAccount', 'msDS-ManagedServiceAccount')
    }
    # --- Declared property lists: no wildcard read, no secret attribute (PORT-PLAN C4b) ---
    # Before v1.33.0 five reads asked for every attribute ("-Properties *"): the AllUsers_ and
    # AllComputers_ exports (Get-ADUser / Get-ADComputer), the trust health rows (Get-ADTrust), the
    # site links (Get-ADReplicationSiteLink) and the connection objects (Get-ADReplicationConnection).
    # Each now asks for the list below, which is exactly what its consumers read (the export CSV
    # columns are the list itself). Nothing here can be set from a file: a list from a file could name
    # a secret attribute. The caps are Inventory.MaxObjects (a tool default, overridable there).
    #
    # Every URL below was read on 2026-11-17.
    #   "-Properties": "To display all of the attributes that are set on the object, specify *
    #   (asterisk)"; "For properties that are not default or extended properties, you must specify the
    #   LDAP display name of the attribute"; -ResultSetSize caps a Get-ADObject/Get-ADUser search. The
    #   default property sets are NOT published (Learn says to run Get-Member), so every column a
    #   consumer reads is named here, even one that is probably returned by default:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-aduser
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-adcomputer
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-adtrust
    #   Why: searchFlags 0x80 "Mark attribute as confidential"; "only Administrators can read
    #   confidential attributes" (so a Domain Admin running "-Properties *" receives them); a Windows
    #   Server 2025 DC logs Directory Service event 3079 for a search of confidential attributes over
    #   an unencrypted LDAP session and silently omits them ("User error: None"):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-searchflags
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/mark-attribute-as-confidential
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/confidential-attributes-unexpected-behavior-using-windows-server-2025-dc
    #   The export populations: users by the Inventory count filter; computers by objectClass=computer,
    #   which, as Get-ADComputer -Filter * did, includes managed service accounts - a group managed
    #   service account is a "Subclass of Computer":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-msds-groupmanagedserviceaccount
    #   Site links: Name, Cost, ReplicationFrequencyInMinutes, SitesIncluded and
    #   InterSiteTransportProtocol are the names the cmdlet pages use (filters, examples, parameters):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-adreplicationsitelink
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/new-adreplicationsitelink
    #   Connections: the property names the old code selected (FromServer, ToServer, Enabled,
    #   AutoGenerated) are NOT published, so the connection is read by LDAP display names of the
    #   NTDS-Connection class, each mandatory there: fromServer ("The distinguished name of the
    #   replication source server"), enabledConnection and options ("A bitfield, where the meaning of
    #   the bits varies from objectClass to objectClass" - kept raw, not decoded); its possible
    #   superior is NTDS-DSA. Searched for and not found on Learn: the bit of options that marks a
    #   KCC-generated connection, so AutoGenerated and ToServer are no longer columns (PORT-PLAN C4b3):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-ntdsconnection
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-fromserver
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-options
    #   Trusts: the ADTrust property names the trust health rows read (Direction, TrustType,
    #   SIDFilteringQuarantined, ...) are NOT published on Learn (the output type is only named); they
    #   are the names the code has read since v1.0, now asked for by name. Measured on a real DC: no
    #   (PORT-PLAN C4b2, with C17).
    #
    # SecretNames: never in a property list, whatever the object kind. Composed at use with
    # Laps.NeverRead (the seven LAPS names, kept in one place). A name list, not the searchFlags bit:
    # the gMSA password blob and the password hashes carry searchFlags 0. Read 2026-11-17:
    #   msDS-ManagedPassword ("returns a blob that contains the clear-text previous and current
    #   password", Search-Flags 0): https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msds-managedpassword
    #   unicodePwd, dBCSPwd, ntPwdHistory, lmPwdHistory, supplementalCredentials ("Kerberos Keys,
    #   WDigest"): https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/security/kerberos/passwords-technical-overview
    #     and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-dbcspwd
    #   msTPM-OwnerInformation (Search-Flags 0x298, confidential):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-mstpm-ownerinformation
    #   msFVE-RecoveryPassword (Search-Flags 0x298; on a child object, listed for defence in depth):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msfve-recoverypassword
    #   trustAuthIncoming, trustAuthOutgoing ("Authentication information for the ... portion of a
    #   trust") and initialAuthOutgoing, on Trusted-Domain:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-trustauthincoming
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-trustauthoutgoing
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-initialauthoutgoing
    #   currentValue, priorValue (secret objects, Search-Flags 0): adschema, read with a-dbcspwd.
    # ADJACENT, NOT READ THIS SESSION (listed by analogy, refusing more is harmless): initialAuthIncoming,
    # msFVE-KeyPackage, userPassword, unixUserPassword.
    DirectoryRead           = @{
        SourceUrl                = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-aduser and https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-adtrust and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-searchflags and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/confidential-attributes-unexpected-behavior-using-windows-server-2025-dc and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-ntdsconnection'
        ReadDate                 = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource          = 'the caps are Inventory.MaxObjects, a tool default, not a Microsoft value'
        UserExportFilter         = '(&(objectCategory=person)(objectClass=user))'
        ComputerExportFilter     = '(objectClass=computer)'
        # The AllUsers_<domain>.csv columns, in this order, then Enabled (from ACCOUNTDISABLE) and ListComplete.
        UserExportProperties     = @('sAMAccountName', 'userPrincipalName', 'name', 'displayName', 'givenName', 'sn', 'mail', 'description',
            'distinguishedName', 'objectGUID', 'objectSid', 'userAccountControl', 'primaryGroupID', 'adminCount', 'servicePrincipalName',
            'msDS-SupportedEncryptionTypes', 'pwdLastSet', 'accountExpires', 'lastLogonTimestamp', 'whenCreated', 'whenChanged')
        # The AllComputers_<domain>.csv columns, in this order, then Enabled and ListComplete.
        ComputerExportProperties = @('sAMAccountName', 'name', 'dNSHostName', 'operatingSystem', 'operatingSystemVersion', 'operatingSystemServicePack',
            'description', 'distinguishedName', 'objectGUID', 'objectSid', 'userAccountControl', 'primaryGroupID', 'servicePrincipalName',
            'msDS-SupportedEncryptionTypes', 'pwdLastSet', 'lastLogonTimestamp', 'whenCreated', 'whenChanged')
        # What Get-AdfaTrustHealth and Get-AdfaTrustSecurityWarning read, and nothing else.
        TrustHealthProperties    = @('Name', 'Source', 'Target', 'Direction', 'TrustType', 'IntraForest', 'ForestTransitive', 'SelectiveAuthentication',
            'SIDFilteringQuarantined', 'SIDFilteringForestAware', 'TGTDelegation', 'UsesRC4Encryption', 'Created', 'Modified')
        SiteLinkProperties       = @('Name', 'Cost', 'ReplicationFrequencyInMinutes', 'InterSiteTransportProtocol', 'SitesIncluded')
        ConnectionProperties     = @('name', 'distinguishedName', 'fromServer', 'enabledConnection', 'options')
        SecretNames              = @('unicodePwd', 'dBCSPwd', 'ntPwdHistory', 'lmPwdHistory', 'supplementalCredentials', 'msDS-ManagedPassword',
            'msTPM-OwnerInformation', 'msFVE-RecoveryPassword', 'msFVE-KeyPackage', 'trustAuthIncoming', 'trustAuthOutgoing',
            'initialAuthIncoming', 'initialAuthOutgoing', 'currentValue', 'priorValue', 'userPassword', 'unixUserPassword')
    }
    # --- DC certificates: the certificate each DC presents on LDAPS, and those published for it (PORT-PLAN C5) ---
    # Two instruments. The LDAPS handshake (opt-in, -IncludeLdapsHandshake) reads the certificate a
    # DC actually hands its clients on port 636; the directory read (always) reads the certificates
    # a CA published on the DC's computer object. The DC's certificate stores are NOT read
    # (PORT-PLAN C5a). Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # Why the handshake and not the store, and why it is opt-in:
    #   "If there is a certificate in the NT Directory Services (NTDS) store, DC use the certificate
    #    in the NTDS store instead." and "if a Windows Server 2008 or a later version domain
    #    controller finds multiple certificates in its store, it will random chose one of these
    #    certificates." "When connecting to ports 636 or 3269, SSL/TLS is negotiated before any LDAP
    #    traffic is exchanged." (KB 321051)
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/enable-ldap-over-ssl-3rd-certification-authority
    #   "Active Directory preferentially checks the NTDS store first." Requirements: Server
    #   Authentication EKU 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1; the DC's AD FQDN in the subject CN or a SAN DNS entry.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/configure-ldap-signing-certificates
    #   "The default value for Schannel event logging is 0x00000001 in Windows, which means that
    #    error messages are logged." No Microsoft page read states that a TLS handshake changes
    #    nothing on the server, so the handshake runs only when the operator asks for it.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/developer/webapps/iis/health-diagnostic-performance/enable-schannel-event-logging
    #
    # The directory read, and what an absent value means:
    #   userCertificate (X509-Cert) is multi-valued, in the global catalog, and holds "the
    #   DER-encoded X.509v3 certificates issued to the user".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-usercertificate
    #   Default templates published to AD DS: "Domain Controller" Yes (version 1); "Domain Controller
    #   Authentication" No; "Kerberos Authentication" No. So no value is NOT evidence of no certificate.
    #   The same table also lists "Directory E-mail Replication" (subject type Computer) as Yes
    #   (version 2), and several user templates as Yes: a published value is therefore not
    #   necessarily a domain controller certificate, and its EKUs are reported for that reason.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/certificate-template-concepts
    #   A userCertificate value is only ever read as a single DER certificate: the X509Certificate2
    #   byte-array constructor also accepts a PKCS7 blob ("the signer certificate is used to create
    #   the object") and a PKCS12 (PFX) blob, whose private key it imports. The content type is
    #   checked first with X509Certificate2.GetCertContentType, and anything that is not "Cert" is
    #   refused before any constructor runs.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.cryptography.x509certificates.x509certificate2.-ctor
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.cryptography.x509certificates.x509certificate2.getcertcontenttype
    #
    # EKU names, each read on a Learn page:
    #   1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1 Server Authentication and 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2 Client Authentication -
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/requirements-domain-controller
    #   1.3.6.1.5.2.3.5 KDC Authentication -
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/security/identity-protection/hello-for-business/deploy/on-premises-cert-trust
    #   1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.2 Smart Card Logon -
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/certificates-and-public-key-infrastructure-pki/enabling-smart-card-logon-third-party-certification-authorities
    # They are reported, never graded: Microsoft documents that the Domain Controller and Domain
    # Controller Authentication templates do not carry KDC Authentication, and does not require
    # Smart Card Logon on a DC certificate.
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled so wherever a finding relies on it:
    #   - Any warning window before expiry for a DC, LDAPS or KDC certificate. WarnWithinDays 30
    #     is the operator-set window of the AdAssessment26 catalogue (ADA-CERT-001, S24: "Warning at
    #     most 30 days left"); it leaves several weeks after the six-week renewal period of
    #     Microsoft's autoenrollment example ("a certificate valid for one year ... If the
    #     certificate has a renewal period of six weeks, it will be renewed during the 46th week"),
    #     by which time a certificate that autoenrollment manages should have been renewed:
    #       https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/certificates-and-public-key-infrastructure-pki/approval-required-certificate-renewals-autoenrollment
    #     FailWithinDays 0 means only an expired certificate
    #     fails, which is the only Fail Microsoft's guidance supports ("The domain controller
    #     certificate has expired" is a listed cause of failure). Both are TOOL DEFAULTS.
    #   - A timeout for the handshake. HandshakeTimeoutMs 5000 is a tool default, and bounds the
    #     WHOLE handshake: a deadline closes the connection when it runs out, so a peer that keeps
    #     sending a byte at a time cannot hold the per-DC loop (the socket's own ReceiveTimeout
    #     restarts on every read, so it alone bounds nothing).
    #   - A timeout for the handshake's own TCP connection. ConnectTimeoutMs 5000 is a tool
    #     default. It is above the shared port probe's bound on purpose: Windows reported a refused
    #     loopback connection only after about 2 s (measured by this tool's tests on Windows
    #     PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7, 2026-11-16 - Microsoft documents no such figure), so a
    #     bound of 1200 ms reported a refusal as a time-out.
    #   - What a DC without a usable certificate does on port 636. A refused connection and a
    #     failed handshake are each reported as observed, never as "no certificate".
    #   - That a certificate not yet valid should be a Warning: this tool's rule.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "DcCertificates":
    # WarnWithinDays, FailWithinDays, HandshakeTimeoutMs, ConnectTimeoutMs. Nothing else - not the
    # port (another port, 3269 among them, would change what a row describes) and not the EKU names.
    DcCertificates          = @{
        SourceUrl          = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/enable-ldap-over-ssl-3rd-certification-authority and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/configure-ldap-signing-certificates and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/certificate-template-concepts'
        ReadDate           = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource    = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value'
        # Tool defaults, all four.
        WarnWithinDays     = 30
        FailWithinDays     = 0
        HandshakeTimeoutMs = 5000
        ConnectTimeoutMs   = 5000
        # Vendor value: LDAP over TLS.
        LdapsPort          = 636
        EkuNames           = @{
            '1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1'      = 'Server Authentication'
            '1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2'      = 'Client Authentication'
            '1.3.6.1.5.2.3.5'        = 'KDC Authentication'
            '1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.2' = 'Smart Card Logon'
        }
    }
    # --- RID pool: allocation enabled, global RID space issued, per-DC pool (PORT-PLAN C6) ---
    # Directory reads only: CN=RID Manager$,CN=System,<domain> of every scoped domain, and the RID
    # Set object of every DC. Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # The global RID space, per domain:
    #   "Each domain has an object CN=RID Manager$,CN=System,DC=<domain_name>. This object has an
    #    attribute named rIDAvailablePool." "The upper part defines the number of security
    #    principals that can be allocated for each domain (0x3FFFFFFF or just over 1 billion). The
    #    lower part is the number of RIDs that have been allocated in the domain." Sample value
    #    4611686014132422708: low part 2100 "(beginning of the next RID pool to be allocated)",
    #    upper part 1073741823 - used as a test vector.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-raise-rid-pool
    #   rIDAvailablePool, rIDPreviousAllocationPool, rIDAllocationPool and rIDUsedPool are Interval
    #   values: "identical to the LargeInteger syntax, except the high and low values are unsigned
    #   integers." https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/s-interval
    #
    # The per-DC pool:
    #   RID-Set (lDAPDisplayName rIDSet): "There is one of these per Domain Controller. It holds the
    #   RID pools that were allocated by the DC." Its four RID attributes are mandatory.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-ridset
    #   rIDSetReferences: on class Computer, multi-valued (Is-Single-Valued False).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-ridsetreferences
    #   rIDPreviousAllocationPool: "the pool of relative identifiers (RIDs) that a domain controller
    #   allocates from ... The start value is in the lower four bytes and the end value is in the
    #   upper four bytes." https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-ridpreviousallocationpool
    #   rIDAllocationPool: "A pool that was prefetched for use by the RID manager when the
    #   RID-Previous-Allocation-Pool has been used up." Its page does not restate the halves; they
    #   are read as for the current pool, AN INFERENCE, said in every row that shows them.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-ridallocationpool
    #   rIDNextRID: "The next free Relative Identifier in the current pool."
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-ridnextrid
    #   rIDUsedPool: "The RID Pools that have been used by a DC." Its halves are not described: read,
    #   not interpreted. https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-ridusedpool
    #
    # From Managing RID Issuance:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/managing-rid-issuance
    #   - Event 16658: the RID master "computes the ten (10) percent used mark in the global pool
    #     and logs an event when reached"; first "when allocating the pool containing the
    #     107,374,182nd RID" (30-bit), "starting at 214,748,365" (31-bit); "This event is not
    #     expected". -> WarnAtPercentUsed 10, a Microsoft value.
    #   - The RID ceiling: "hard-coded at ten percent remaining of the available RID space. That is,
    #     the ceiling activates when the RID master allocates a pool that includes the RID
    #     corresponding to ninety (90) percent of the global RID space", trigger points 966,367,640
    #     (30-bit) and 1,932,735,282 (31-bit); the RID master writes event 16657 and "sets ...
    #     msDS-RIDPoolAllocationEnabled ... to FALSE", which "prevents further RID block issuance to
    #     all domain controllers". -> FailAtPercentUsed 90, a Microsoft value; and FALSE is a Fail.
    #   - "On the next RID allocation performed by the RID master, the attribute will return to its
    #     default NOT SET value." -> not set is not blocked. But an attribute this account is
    #     denied read on is left out of the answer the same way, and the ACL is not evaluated, so
    #     an allocation attribute that is not returned is Not Assessed, never Pass (PORT-PLAN C6d).
    #   - The size of the space: "limited to 2^30 (or 1,073,741,823)" by default; after the unlock
    #     "2,147,483,648" in one sentence and, as dcdiag shows it, "2,147,483,647" in another. The
    #     upper part is read, never assumed; 2,147,483,647 is matched, and that the attribute holds
    #     it after the unlock is AN INFERENCE (Learn shows only dcdiag output). SidCompatibilityVersion
    #     "isn't readable using ADSIEdit, LDP, or other tools", so the unlock is never claimed as read.
    #   - "Previous Windows Server operating systems had a RID pool leak when missing
    #     rIDSetReferences attribute" (KB 2618669).
    #   - Default security descriptors of RID-Manager and RID-Set grant Authenticated Users read
    #     (A;;RPLCLORC;;;AU): https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-ridmanager
    #
    # The comparison is on integers: Warning when issued * 100 >= size * 10, Fail when issued * 100
    # >= size * 90. On the 31-bit space the Warning point is 214,748,365, Learn's figure; on the
    # 30-bit space it is 107,374,183, one past the RID Learn names, and the Fail points are
    # 966,367,641 and 1,932,735,283, one past Learn's trigger RIDs. The low part is where the NEXT
    # pool begins (the last pool's end + 1), so the allocation of the pool that CONTAINS Learn's RID
    # (re-read 2026-11-16: the ceiling "activates when the RID master allocates a pool that includes
    # the RID corresponding to ninety (90) percent") leaves a low part of at least one past it, which
    # is graded on that same allocation; a pool that ends below it is not, and Microsoft has not
    # triggered either. The integer rule therefore lands on the same allocation as Microsoft's.
    # PercentUsed, the figure shown, is TRUNCATED (never rounded) to four decimals, so a count graded
    # below a point never shows that point (107,374,182 of 1,073,741,823 shows 9.9999, not 10).
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and said where a row relies on it: the schema page of
    # msDS-RIDPoolAllocationEnabled (404 on 2026-11-16) and what its absence from an older schema
    # means (Not Assessed); whether a read-only DC has a RID Set; whether the end of a pool is
    # inclusive ("remaining" is end - next + 1, a tool derivation); the exception the module throws
    # for an object that does not exist. dcdiag /test:RidManager is not run by this section; the
    # DC Diagnostics section runs it, on Learn's description of it as checks only (PORT-PLAN C6e,
    # closed by C11 on 2026-11-16).
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "RidPool": WarnAtPercentUsed and
    # FailAtPercentUsed. Nothing else - not the ceilings and not any attribute or object name.
    RidPool                 = @{
        SourceUrl              = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/managing-rid-issuance and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-raise-rid-pool'
        ReadDate               = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource        = 'Microsoft: event 16658 at the first 10 % of the global RID space used, and the hard-coded RID ceiling at 90 % used (event 16657), Managing RID Issuance'
        WarnAtPercentUsed      = 10
        FailAtPercentUsed      = 90
        KnownCeilings          = @(
            @{ Value = [int64]1073741823; Label = '30-bit (default)'; Note = '1,073,741,823, the default size Microsoft documents (2^30)' }
            @{ Value = [int64]2147483647; Label = '31-bit (unlocked; inferred)'; Note = '2,147,483,647, the size dcdiag shows after the 31st bit is unlocked; that this attribute holds it after the unlock is inferred from Microsoft''s dcdiag example, and the unlock itself (SidCompatibilityVersion) cannot be read' }
        )
        RidManagerRdn          = 'CN=RID Manager$,CN=System'
        AvailablePoolAttribute = 'rIDAvailablePool'
        AllocationAttribute    = 'msDS-RIDPoolAllocationEnabled'
        ReferenceAttribute     = 'rIDSetReferences'
        RidSetClass            = 'rIDSet'
        # In this order: the current pool, the prefetched next pool, the next RID, the used pools.
        RidSetAttributes       = @('rIDPreviousAllocationPool', 'rIDAllocationPool', 'rIDNextRID', 'rIDUsedPool')
    }
    # --- SID history: principals with sIDHistory, same-domain and privileged historic SIDs, and
    #     the domains those SIDs came from (PORT-PLAN C7; rows of the SecurityPosture section) ---
    # Directory reads only, per scoped domain: one (sIDHistory=*) search, the trustedDomain objects
    # under CN=System, and Get-ADDomain for the SID of every domain of the forest. sIDHistory is
    # never written. Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # The attribute:
    #   sIDHistory: "Contains previous SIDs used for the object if the object was moved from another
    #   domain." Syntax String(Sid), Is-Single-Valued False (multi-valued), indexed, in the global
    #   catalog, Search-Flags 0x00000001 (not confidential), "Classes used in: Security-Principal".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-sidhistory
    #   String(Sid) is an octet string; its .NET type is System.Byte[].
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/s-string-sid
    #   The user class and the group class carry Security-Principal as an auxiliary class (c-user,
    #   c-group); computer derives from user, and the managed service account classes from computer
    #   - an INFERENCE from the class hierarchy, not re-read for each class.
    #   "This property can hold multiple values." "any one of the SIDs in the access token
    #   (including one of the SIDs in the SIDHistory property), can allow or deny the user access."
    #   "All values up to, but not including, the last value in the series collectively identify a
    #   domain ... The last value in the series, the RID". Builtin SIDs use domain identifier 32.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-identifiers
    #   SID layout (MS-DTYP 2.4.2.2): Revision MUST be 0x01; SubAuthorityCount at most 15; a 6-byte
    #   IdentifierAuthority; SubAuthority "unsigned 32-bit integers". The page leaves the byte order
    #   of a sub-authority to the protocol: little-endian is the layout the fallback parser uses, and
    #   it is cross-checked against .NET SecurityIdentifier on every Windows run - measured, not read.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-dtyp/f992ad60-0fe4-4b87-9fed-beb478836861
    #
    # Who can read it: sIDHistory and objectSid are members of the General-Information property set,
    # which the user class's default security descriptor grants Authenticated Users read access to
    # ((OA;;RP;59ba2f42-79a2-11d0-9020-00c04fc2d3cf;;AU)); the group and trustedDomain classes grant
    # Authenticated Users (A;;RPLCLORC;;;AU). These are SCHEMA DEFAULTS for new objects: Learn has no
    # sentence that a deployed domain keeps them, and "a search operation can succeed, but the search
    # results don't include objects or properties to which the caller doesn't have access". An empty
    # answer is therefore "none visible to this account", never "none".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/r-general-information
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-user
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-group
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-trusteddomain
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/access-control-and-read-operations
    #   trustedDomain securityIdentifier: String(Sid), single-valued, the SID of the trusted domain.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-securityidentifier
    #
    # The risk, as Microsoft states it:
    #   Defender for Identity, "Unsecure SID History attributes": "a nonsensitive account in a domain
    #   can contain the Enterprise Admin SID in its SID History from another domain in the Active
    #   Directory forest"; "If you have a forest trust without SID Filtering enabled (also called
    #   Quarantine), it's possible to inject a SID from another forest"; the remediation removes the
    #   value with Set-ADUser -Remove @{SIDHistory=...}. NO SEVERITY IS PUBLISHED.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-for-identity/security-posture-assessments/accounts
    #   DsAddSidHistory: "The source and destination domains must not be in the same forest ... This
    #   inter-forest constraint ensures that duplicate SIDs, whether appearing as primary SIDs or
    #   sIDHistory values, are not created in the same forest"; it refuses "Accounts with Well Known
    #   SIDs"; and "Adding SIDs to a security principal's sIDHistory is a security-sensitive
    #   operation that effectively grants to the destination principal access to all resources
    #   accessible to the source principal".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/using-dsaddsidhistory
    #   "the ultimate solution to token bloat is to reduce the number of SIDs associated with user
    #   accounts, whether by rationalizing group memberships, eliminating SID history, or a
    #   combination of both" - no grade is given.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/planning-for-compromise
    #
    # Privileged SIDs (the well-known SID table of the Security identifiers page above, and
    # https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/secauthz/well-known-sids for the 0x220.. aliases and
    # 0x20E/0x20F). Domain-relative RIDs are matched only on an account-domain SID
    # (S-1-5-21-x-y-z-RID); the fixed SIDs only on the whole SID. Learn writes Key Admins as
    # "S-1-5-root domain-526" and Enterprise Key Admins as "S-1-5-domain-527", the other way round
    # from their names (the Active Directory security groups page gives <domain> for both); the match
    # is on the RID alone, so this does not change the check. Protected Users (525), Read-only Domain
    # Controllers (521), Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers (498) and Replicators (S-1-5-32-552)
    # are NOT in the list: the brief for this row did not name them and the AdAssessment26 reference
    # list differs (it has 525, not 500/502/516) - PORT-PLAN C7b asks the operator.
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled where a row relies on it:
    #   - Any grade for SID history that is present, not same-domain and not privileged. PresentStatus
    #     Warning is a TOOL DEFAULT, the operator-approved rule of the AdAssessment26 catalogue
    #     (ADA-OBJ-009, "Warning when count > 0"); a file may set Info instead.
    #   - That a same-domain historic SID indicates abuse. The Fail rests on the DsAddSidHistory
    #     constraint and on moves always issuing a new SID from ANOTHER domain - an inference.
    #   - Which privileged list Microsoft would use: it gives only the Enterprise Admins example.
    #   - How many names one row should list. NameListCap 25 is a tool default, as Laps.SampleLimit.
    #   - What the ActiveDirectory module returns for sIDHistory, objectSid and securityIdentifier
    #     (byte[], SecurityIdentifier, or a collection of either). The parser accepts all of them.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "SidHistory": PresentStatus
    # (Warning or Info) and NameListCap (0 to 1000). Nothing else - not the privileged list, the
    # filters or any property list, so a file cannot make the tool ask for every attribute.
    SidHistory              = @{
        SourceUrl            = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-sidhistory and https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-for-identity/security-posture-assessments/accounts and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/using-dsaddsidhistory'
        ReadDate             = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource      = 'tool default (AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-OBJ-009, operator-approved), not a Microsoft value'
        # Tool defaults, both.
        PresentStatus        = 'Warning'
        NameListCap          = 25
        # What is asked, and of what. Fixed: a file cannot change them.
        SearchFilter         = '(sIDHistory=*)'
        TrustFilter          = '(objectClass=trustedDomain)'
        TrustContainerRdn    = 'CN=System'
        ObjectProperties     = @('sIDHistory', 'objectSid', 'objectClass', 'sAMAccountName', 'distinguishedName', 'userAccountControl')
        TrustProperties      = @('securityIdentifier', 'trustPartner', 'flatName')
        # ACCOUNTDISABLE, read from userAccountControl of users and computers only.
        # https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/useraccountcontrol-manipulate-account-properties
        AccountDisableFlag   = 2
        # Class buckets for the counts. The managed service account classes count as computers (an
        # inference from the class hierarchy); anything not listed counts as other.
        UserClasses          = @('user', 'inetOrgPerson')
        ComputerClasses      = @('computer', 'msDS-GroupManagedServiceAccount', 'msDS-ManagedServiceAccount')
        GroupClasses         = @('group')
        # Vendor values (Learn, read 2026-11-16).
        PrivilegedDomainRids = @(
            @{ Rid = 500; Name = 'Administrator' }
            @{ Rid = 502; Name = 'KRBTGT' }
            @{ Rid = 512; Name = 'Domain Admins' }
            @{ Rid = 516; Name = 'Domain Controllers' }
            @{ Rid = 517; Name = 'Cert Publishers' }
            @{ Rid = 518; Name = 'Schema Admins' }
            @{ Rid = 519; Name = 'Enterprise Admins' }
            @{ Rid = 520; Name = 'Group Policy Creator Owners' }
            @{ Rid = 526; Name = 'Key Admins' }
            @{ Rid = 527; Name = 'Enterprise Key Admins' }
        )
        PrivilegedSids       = @(
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-544'; Name = 'Administrators' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-548'; Name = 'Account Operators' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-549'; Name = 'Server Operators' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-550'; Name = 'Print Operators' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-551'; Name = 'Backup Operators' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-9'; Name = 'Enterprise Domain Controllers' }
        )
    }
    # --- Group Policy version consistency: the version of each GPO in the directory against the
    #     version in its GPT.INI in SYSVOL, both read from the domain's PDC emulator (PORT-PLAN C8;
    #     rows of the Gpo section, report section "GPO Inventory") ---
    # Instrument: a directory read of the groupPolicyContainer objects under CN=Policies,CN=System
    # and a read-only file read of each GPO's GPT.INI over the PDC's SYSVOL share. The GroupPolicy
    # module is NOT used for this check: Learn documents Get-GPO's formatted output ("AD Version: n,
    # SysVol Version: n") but not the Microsoft.GroupPolicy.Gpo members behind it, nor which DC's
    # SYSVOL it reads. Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # Vendor facts (MS-GPOL, Microsoft's Group Policy protocol specification, on Learn):
    #   "user GPO version: ... a 16-bit integer encoded in the upper 16 bits of a GPO version";
    #   "machine Group Policy Object (GPO) version: ... encoded in the lower 16 bits"; the GPO
    #   container version is "stored in the Active Directory portion of the GPO" and the file
    #   system version "in the file system portion".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/2f362c13-9a2d-469b-8c4b-7b4045258995
    #   versionNumber: "A GPO container version. It is a 32-bit integer which consists of 16 bits of
    #   user GPO version and 16 bits of machine GPO version"; "<gpo path>\gpt.ini ... corresponds to
    #   the file system path retrieved for the GPO in the gPCFileSysPath attribute"; gpt.ini "MUST be
    #   encoded in ANSI", "MUST have the required section, "General"" (otherwise "the file is
    #   considered corrupt"), and its key "Version" "MUST be a 32-bit integer"; flags 0 enabled,
    #   bit 0 ignore for user mode, bit 1 ignore for computer mode, 3 disabled for both; cn is "a
    #   curly braced GUID string"; displayName "a human-readable directory string" (free text, so
    #   it never reaches Item or Detail).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/b0e5c9e8-e858-4a7a-a94a-4a3d0a9d87a2
    #   On every edit "both the GPO container version and the GPO file system version" of the
    #   edited half MUST be updated - the basis for expecting them equal.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/70fd86b1-926a-4dcf-9ce7-6f9d2149c20d
    #   The GPO path "will always be a Universal Naming Convention (UNC) path of the form:
    #   "\\<dns domain name>\sysvol\<dns domain name>\policies\<gpo guid>"" (the glossary above);
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/2f362c13-9a2d-469b-8c4b-7b4045258995
    #   and "the GUID in the GPO DN and the GPO path MUST be the same" (3.1.1 Server Abstract Data
    #   Model, which says only that a GPO path MUST be available, not its form).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/34f68012-77c5-4487-837f-84a23829273b
    #   versionNumber has syntax 2.5.5.9 (Enumeration, VT_I4, System.Int32): a user half of 0x8000
    #   or more therefore reads as a negative Int32 and is masked to 32 bits before it is split -
    #   an INFERENCE from the type, not a Learn statement.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-ada3/5e517dde-c710-45fa-b694-2704e17b5612
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/s-enumeration
    #   groupPolicyContainer's default security descriptor grants Authenticated Users RPLCLORC; an
    #   administrator can remove it per GPO, and such a GPO is then invisible to the runner.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-grouppolicycontainer
    #   Get-GPO contacts the PDC emulator when -Server is not given, and the Group Policy Object
    #   Editor uses the PDC Operations Master unless configured otherwise - why both reads here are
    #   pinned to the PDC.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/grouppolicy/get-gpo
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-grouppolicy
    #   AD and SYSVOL are "two independent mechanisms"; DFSR SYSVOL "Within sites, replication
    #   occurs every 15 minutes" - why a mismatch can be in flight.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-processing
    #   A SYSVOL folder whose GUID "is not listed in Active Directory" is an orphaned GPO; a container
    #   with no folder "on any domain controller in the domain" can be deleted from the directory.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/group-policy/rebuild-sysvol-tree-and-content-in-a-domain
    #   SMB on TCP 445 for the SYSVOL share (the port probed before any file read).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements
    #   Error codes 2 file not found, 3 path not found, 5 access denied, 51 and 53 network path not
    #   found, 64 network name no longer available, 65 network access denied, 1231/1232 network
    #   location cannot be reached; an IOException's code is its HResult with the upper 16 bits
    #   stripped; ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND maps to FileNotFoundException and ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND to
    #   DirectoryNotFoundException; SecurityException is thrown "for applications running in
    #   limited trust ... on .NET Framework only" - not for a refused file, so it is not read as
    #   access denied.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/debug/system-error-codes--0-499-
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/debug/system-error-codes--1000-1299-
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/io/handling-io-errors
    #   DriveNotFoundException: "a drive or share that is not available"; it carries
    #   COR_E_DIRECTORYNOTFOUND and "can be thrown when a Win32 ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND" occurs - so it
    #   is read as not found, like error 3.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.io.drivenotfoundexception
    #   GPT.INI is opened with new FileStream(path, FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read, FileShare.ReadWrite
    #   | FileShare.Delete): read access only, and every other open - for reading, writing or delete -
    #   left free while it is held. FileShare.Read alone (what File.OpenRead uses) would make a GPMC
    #   save or a DFSR install of that GPT.INI on the PDC fail while the tool held it - of
    #   FileShare.Write: "If this flag is not specified, any request to open the file for writing
    #   (by this process or another process) will fail until the file is closed".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.io.filestream.-ctor
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.io.fileshare
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled where a row relies on it:
    #   - Any grade for a version mismatch, or a time after which one stops being transient.
    #     MismatchStatus Warning is a TOOL DEFAULT, the operator-approved rule of the AdAssessment26
    #     catalogue (ADA-GPO-001); a file may set Fail.
    #   - Any grade for a SYSVOL folder with no container. OrphanFolderStatus Warning is a TOOL
    #     DEFAULT; a file may set Info.
    #   - The default ACL of the SYSVOL share and folders: access denied is reported as such, and
    #     no default is claimed.
    #   - GptIniMaxBytes (a GPT.INI larger than this is not read) and SmbProbeTimeoutMs are tool
    #     defaults.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "Gpo": MismatchStatus (Warning
    # or Fail) and OrphanFolderStatus (Warning or Info). Nothing else - not the filter, the property
    # list, the share, the folder or the file name.
    Gpo                     = @{
        SourceUrl            = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/2f362c13-9a2d-469b-8c4b-7b4045258995 and https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-gpol/b0e5c9e8-e858-4a7a-a94a-4a3d0a9d87a2 and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-processing'
        ReadDate             = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource      = 'tool default (AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-GPO-001, operator-approved 2026-09-24), not a Microsoft value'
        # Tool defaults, both.
        MismatchStatus       = 'Warning'
        OrphanFolderStatus   = 'Warning'
        # What is asked, and of what. Fixed: a file cannot change them.
        ContainerFilter      = '(objectClass=groupPolicyContainer)'
        PoliciesContainerRdn = 'CN=Policies,CN=System'
        ContainerProperties  = @('versionNumber', 'gPCFileSysPath', 'displayName', 'flags')
        SysvolShare          = 'SYSVOL'
        PoliciesFolder       = 'Policies'
        GptIniFileName       = 'GPT.INI'
        GptIniSection        = 'General'
        GptIniKey            = 'Version'
        # MS-GPOL: every GPO common name is "a curly braced GUID string".
        GuidPattern          = '^\{[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{12}\}$'
        SmbPort              = 445
        # Tool defaults.
        SmbProbeTimeoutMs    = 1200
        GptIniMaxBytes       = 65536
        # Tool rule, not a Microsoft value: when the Policies folder could not be listed, GPT.INI
        # files are still read one by one only after a listing refused as access denied (a folder
        # can refuse a listing and still let its files be read). After any other class -
        # unreachable, timed out, not found, or an error not interpreted - no file is read: each
        # read would go to the same path that just failed, with no timeout of its own. Fixed: a
        # file cannot change it.
        ReadFilesAfterListingClass = @('AccessDenied')
        # MS-GPOL flags values (Learn, read 2026-11-16).
        FlagLabels           = @(
            @{ Value = 0; Label = 'enabled' }
            @{ Value = 1; Label = 'user settings disabled' }
            @{ Value = 2; Label = 'computer settings disabled' }
            @{ Value = 3; Label = 'all settings disabled' }
        )
    }
    # --- Dangerous rights in the directory (PORT-PLAN C9; section Acl, report section "Dangerous ACLs
    #     (DCSync)"; AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-ACL-001, -002 and -003) ---
    # Instrument: per scoped domain, Get-ADObject -Identity <DN> -Server <domain> asking for
    # nTSecurityDescriptor only, for the domain head and CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System; and one one-level
    # search of CN=Policies,CN=System for every groupPolicyContainer, asking for nTSecurityDescriptor
    # only. The descriptor is turned into its self-relative binary form and read with
    # System.Security.AccessControl.RawSecurityDescriptor in memory: the owner SID, whether a DACL is
    # present at all, and every ACE of the DACL in its order. Nothing is written: no Set-Acl, no
    # Set-ADObject, no Persist, no CommitChanges. Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # Vendor facts:
    #   The three replication control access rights and their rights GUIDs, each applying to
    #   Domain-DNS, Configuration and DMD:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/r-ds-replication-get-changes          1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/r-ds-replication-get-changes-all      1131f6ad-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/r-ds-replication-get-changes-in-filtered-set  89e95b76-444d-4c62-991a-0facbeda640c
    #   The Domain-DNS class's default security descriptor (Windows Server 2012 schema, the last one
    #   that page lists) grants, as ACEs of their own: Get-Changes to Enterprise Domain Controllers (ED)
    #   and Administrators (BA); Get-Changes-All to Domain Controllers (DD) and BA; In-Filtered-Set to
    #   ED and BA; and write-all-properties, all extended rights, WriteDacl and WriteOwner to Domain
    #   Admins (DA), BA, SYSTEM (SY) and Enterprise Admins (EA) - "(A;;RPWPCRLCLOCCRCWDWOSW;;;DA)",
    #   "(A;CI;RPWPCRLCLOCCRCWDWOSDSW;;;BA)", "(A;;RPWPCRLCLOCCDCRCWDWOSDDTSW;;;SY)",
    #   "(A;CI;RPWPCRLCLOCCDCRCWDWOSDDTSW;;;EA)":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-domaindns
    #   Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers of the forest root domain hold Replicating Directory
    #   Changes on each NC head (added by adprep /rodcprep); a grant of Replicating Directory Changes
    #   All to them is the misconfiguration Microsoft tells you to change back:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/replication-error-8453
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/rodc-replicates-passwords-grant-incorrect-permissions
    #   THE TWO LEARN SOURCES DISAGREE about Enterprise Domain Controllers (S-1-5-9): the RODC article
    #   says "By default, the Enterprise Domain Controllers group that contains only writeable DCs has
    #   the permission Replicating Directory Changes All on the domain partition"; the c-domaindns
    #   default descriptor (2012 schema) does not grant it to ED. ED is allowed Get-Changes-All on the
    #   domain head on the strength of the RODC article (a group of writable DCs only), in its own row
    #   below, so that this disagreement stays visible; a real forest settles it (PORT-PLAN C9a).
    #   AdminSDHolder is CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System,<domain DN>; "By default, Enterprise Admins can make
    #   changes to any domain's AdminSDHolder object, as can the domain's Domain Admins and
    #   Administrators groups"; Domain Admins owns it; SDProp applies its ACL to the protected accounts
    #   and groups:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/appendix-c--protected-accounts-and-groups-in-active-directory
    #   The groupPolicyContainer default descriptor grants DA, EA and CREATOR OWNER (CO)
    #   "RPWPCCDCLCLOLORCWOWDSDDTSW" and SY "RPWPCCDCLCLORCWOWDSDDTSW" - write of all properties,
    #   WriteDacl and WriteOwner, but NO control access (CR), so it is not full control - read to
    #   Authenticated Users and Enterprise Domain Controllers, and Apply-Group-Policy (a specific
    #   extended right) to Authenticated Users. Only those three rights are allowed to DA, EA, CO and SY
    #   on a GPO as Microsoft's; all extended rights and the replication rights on a GPO are allowed to
    #   the same four as a labelled TOOL DEFAULT (they are not in Learn's descriptor):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-grouppolicycontainer
    #   Class GUIDs (schemaIDGUID) and the chains: domainDNS 19195a5b-6da0-11d0-afd3-00c04fd930c9,
    #   subclass of domain 19195a5a-6da0-11d0-afd3-00c04fd930c9, subclass of top
    #   bf967ab7-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2; groupPolicyContainer f30e3bc2-9ff0-11d1-b603-0000f80367c1,
    #   subclass of container bf967a8b-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2, subclass of top:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-domaindns
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-domain
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-grouppolicycontainer
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-container
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-top
    #   AdminSDHolder's class is not stated on Learn; Defender for Identity's posture page calls it the
    #   "CN=AdminSDHolder container", so container and top are its chain here (tool assumption):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-for-identity/security-posture-assessments/accounts
    #   An object ACE whose GUID is the node of the object itself grants that right on every node below
    #   it: OBJECT_TYPE_LIST "Level zero indicates the object itself"; MS-DTYP 2.5.3.2 removes an object
    #   allow ACE's mask from the node with the ACE's GUID and "each node ns such that ns is a descendent
    #   of n", and an object ACE whose GUID is not in the list grants nothing; "the rights granted or
    #   denied at a higher level apply also to the lower levels":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winnt/ns-winnt-object_type_list
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-dtyp/4f1bbcbb-814a-4c70-a11e-2a5b8779a6f9
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/secauthz/aces-to-control-access-to-an-object-s-properties
    #   WHICH class GUID Active Directory puts at level zero is in MS-ADTS, which could not be read on
    #   Learn in this session. TOOL RULE: the GUID of the object's class and of every class above it in
    #   the chain is read as the object itself, so a grant scoped to any of them is graded as unscoped;
    #   this can over-report (if only the structural class is at level zero) and never under-reports.
    #   An object ACE scoped to any other GUID grants only that node: WriteDacl, WriteOwner and full
    #   control in it are never object-wide rights, and only a replication right's own GUID is graded.
    #   The well-known SIDs and RIDs (S-1-5-9, S-1-5-18, S-1-5-32-544, S-1-3-0, -512, -516, -519, -498):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-identifiers
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-groups
    #   Access mask bits: ADS_RIGHT_WRITE_DAC 0x40000, ADS_RIGHT_WRITE_OWNER 0x80000,
    #   ADS_RIGHT_DS_WRITE_PROP 0x20 ("If ObjectType does not contain a GUID, the ACE controls the right
    #   to write all of the object properties"), ADS_RIGHT_DS_CONTROL_ACCESS 0x100 ("... the right to
    #   perform all extended right operations"), ADS_RIGHT_GENERIC_ALL 0x10000000,
    #   ADS_RIGHT_GENERIC_WRITE 0x40000000:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/iads/ne-iads-ads_rights_enum
    #   What the two generic bits mean for a directory object, as ActiveDirectoryRights values:
    #   GenericAll 983551 (0xF01FF) and GenericWrite 131112 (ReadControl, WriteProperty, Self):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices.activedirectoryrights
    #   An inherit-only ACE "does not control access to the object to which it is attached":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winnt/ns-winnt-ace_header
    #   InheritedObjectType alone does NOT stop an ACE taking effect on the container: "If this is not
    #   done [INHERIT_ONLY], the ACE will also become effective on the container":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/setting-rights-to-specific-types-of-objects
    #   "An object's owner implicitly has WRITE_DAC access to the object":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/secauthz/owner-of-a-new-object
    #   Owner and DACL need READ_CONTROL only; the SACL needs SE_SECURITY_NAME. Not sending the SD
    #   flags control equals asking for owner, group, DACL and SACL:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/reading-an-objectampaposs-security-descriptor
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/3888c2b7-35b9-45b7-afeb-b772aa932dd0
    #   "a search operation can succeed, but the search results don't include objects or properties
    #   to which the caller doesn't have access" - why an absent nTSecurityDescriptor is not measured:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/access-control-and-read-operations
    #   ControlFlags.DiscretionaryAclPresent (4) "Specifies that the DACL is not null"; AceFlags
    #   InheritOnly 8 and Inherited 16; ObjectAceFlags ObjectAceTypePresent 1 and
    #   InheritedObjectAceTypePresent 2; RawSecurityDescriptor(byte[], int):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.accesscontrol.controlflags
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.accesscontrol.aceflags
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.accesscontrol.objectaceflags
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.accesscontrol.objectace
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.accesscontrol.rawsecuritydescriptor.-ctor
    #   SecurityIdentifier.Translate throws IdentityNotMappedException when a SID cannot be translated
    #   (names are resolved for display only, in a column the verdict never reads):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.security.principal.securityidentifier.translate
    #
    #   "A null DACL grants full access to any user that requests it" (and MS-DTYP above: "No DACL in
    #   the security descriptor implies that there is no policy in place to govern access to the
    #   object; any access check will succeed"):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/secauthz/null-dacls-and-empty-dacls
    #
    # MEASURED IN THIS SESSION (2026-11-16, Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7.6, one Windows 11
    # workstation, descriptors built from SDDL - not read from a directory): ActiveDirectorySecurity's
    # GetAccessRules returns ONE synthetic "Everyone, full control" rule for a descriptor that carries
    # no DACL. That is why the DACL is read from the binary form, where ControlFlags says whether it is
    # present, and never through GetAccessRules. Also measured: SDDL "D:NO_ACCESS_CONTROL" (a NULL
    # DACL) gives ControlFlags with DiscretionaryAclPresent SET and DiscretionaryAcl null, so a null
    # DiscretionaryAcl is not the same as a clear present flag: the converter tells the two apart
    # (DaclAbsent when the flag is clear, NullDacl when it is set and no ACL came with it).
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled where a row relies on it:
    #   - Which .NET type Get-ADObject returns for nTSecurityDescriptor, and which descriptor parts the
    #     ActiveDirectory module asks for. An ActiveDirectorySecurity and a byte array are both read;
    #     anything else is Not Assessed. Confirmed only by a first real run (PORT-PLAN C9a).
    #   - The full default DACL of the domain head as it is on a running forest (Windows Server 2016
    #     and later schema updates are not on the adschema pages), and of AdminSDHolder. SYSTEM on
    #     AdminSDHolder and Administrators on a GPO are TOOL DEFAULTS (AdAssessment26 decision D14);
    #     all extended rights and the replication rights on a GPO, for DA, EA, CO and SY, are a TOOL
    #     DEFAULT of C9 (Learn's groupPolicyContainer descriptor grants them no CR).
    #   - Any grade. FlaggedStatus Warning is a TOOL DEFAULT, the operator-approved catalogue rule
    #     ("Warning when a non-allowlisted principal holds GenericAll, GenericWrite, WriteDacl,
    #     WriteOwner or a replication right"); a file may set Fail. The allowlist is PROVISIONAL until
    #     it is captured from a clean lab forest (catalogue P44/P45), and every row says so.
    #   - Group membership is not expanded: a principal that holds a right through a group is reported
    #     as the group's SID, and a deny ACE is honoured only when it names the same SID.
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "Acl": FlaggedStatus (Warning or
    # Fail) and AdditionalAllowed (SIDs a file adds to the allowlist, each for named objects and named
    # rights, with a reason). Nothing else - not the masks, the GUIDs, the built-in allowlist, the
    # filter or the property list.
    Acl                     = @{
        SourceUrl            = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-domaindns and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-grouppolicycontainer and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/appendix-c--protected-accounts-and-groups-in-active-directory and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/replication-error-8453'
        ReadDate             = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource      = 'tool default (AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-ACL-001..003, operator-approved 2026-09-24), not a Microsoft value'
        AllowlistNote        = 'allowlist provisional: Microsoft''s published default holders plus labelled tool defaults (AdAssessment26 decision D14, and extended rights on GPOs), matched by SID only and never by name, until it is captured from a clean lab forest'
        # Tool default.
        FlaggedStatus        = 'Warning'
        # What is asked, and of what. Fixed: a file cannot change them.
        Properties           = @('nTSecurityDescriptor')
        AdminSdHolderRdn     = 'CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System'
        PoliciesContainerRdn = 'CN=Policies,CN=System'
        GpoFilter            = '(objectClass=groupPolicyContainer)'
        # Access mask bits (ads_rights_enum, ActiveDirectoryRights; Learn, read 2026-11-16).
        Masks                = @{
            WriteDacl      = 0x40000
            WriteOwner     = 0x80000
            WriteProperty  = 0x20
            ExtendedRight  = 0x100
            GenericAllBit  = 0x10000000
            GenericWriteBit = 0x40000000
            # ActiveDirectoryRights.GenericAll and .GenericWrite: what the two generic bits stand for.
            GenericAll     = 983551
            GenericWrite   = 131112
        }
        ReplicationRights    = @(
            @{ Name = 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes'; Guid = '1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2' }
            @{ Name = 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All'; Guid = '1131f6ad-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2' }
            @{ Name = 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'; Guid = '89e95b76-444d-4c62-991a-0facbeda640c' }
        )
        # Every right this section grades, by the names rows and the allowlist use.
        RightNames           = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties', 'AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set')
        ObjectKinds          = @('DomainRoot', 'AdminSDHolder', 'Gpo')
        # The class GUIDs read as "the object itself" for each kind: its class and every class above it
        # (Learn adschema pages, read 2026-11-16; AdminSDHolder's class a tool assumption; the whole chain
        # a TOOL RULE - see the comment above). An object ACE scoped to one of them is graded as unscoped.
        ClassGuids           = @{
            DomainRoot    = @('19195a5b-6da0-11d0-afd3-00c04fd930c9', '19195a5a-6da0-11d0-afd3-00c04fd930c9', 'bf967ab7-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2')
            AdminSDHolder = @('bf967a8b-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2', 'bf967ab7-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2')
            Gpo           = @('f30e3bc2-9ff0-11d1-b603-0000f80367c1', 'bf967a8b-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2', 'bf967ab7-0de6-11d0-a285-00aa003049e2')
        }
        # Which rights each row grades. The owner is graded as WriteDacl, which it holds implicitly.
        Rows                 = @{
            DCSync            = @{ Rights = @('AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Owner = $false; CatalogueId = 'ADA-ACL-001'; Severity = 'High' }
            DomainRootControl = @{ Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties'); Owner = $true; CatalogueId = 'ADA-ACL-001'; Severity = 'High' }
            AdminSDHolder     = @{ Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties', 'AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Owner = $true; CatalogueId = 'ADA-ACL-002'; Severity = 'High' }
            Gpo               = @{ Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties', 'AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Owner = $true; CatalogueId = 'ADA-ACL-003'; Severity = 'Medium' }
        }
        # The allowlist, by SID only. Sid is a fixed SID; Rid with Scope Domain is <scoped domain SID>-RID,
        # with Scope ForestRoot <forest root domain SID>-RID. Rights '*' is every right in RightNames.
        # Name is for the reader of this table only: no decision reads it.
        # On a GPO, Microsoft's default descriptor grants DA, EA, CO and SY write of all properties,
        # WriteDacl and WriteOwner and no control access, so those are the Learn rows; all extended rights
        # and the replication rights on a GPO are a separate, labelled tool default row for each.
        Allowlist            = @(
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-18'; Name = 'SYSTEM'; Objects = @('DomainRoot'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-18'; Name = 'SYSTEM'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties'); Origin = 'Learn: c-grouppolicycontainer' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-18'; Name = 'SYSTEM'; Objects = @('AdminSDHolder'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'tool default (D14), not a Microsoft value' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-18'; Name = 'SYSTEM'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Origin = 'tool default (C9), not a Microsoft value' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-544'; Name = 'Administrators'; Objects = @('DomainRoot', 'AdminSDHolder'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns, AdminSDHolder appendix C' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-32-544'; Name = 'Administrators'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'tool default (D14), not a Microsoft value' }
            @{ Rid = 512; Scope = 'Domain'; Name = 'Domain Admins'; Objects = @('DomainRoot', 'AdminSDHolder'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns, AdminSDHolder appendix C' }
            @{ Rid = 512; Scope = 'Domain'; Name = 'Domain Admins'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties'); Origin = 'Learn: c-grouppolicycontainer' }
            @{ Rid = 512; Scope = 'Domain'; Name = 'Domain Admins'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Origin = 'tool default (C9), not a Microsoft value' }
            @{ Rid = 519; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Name = 'Enterprise Admins'; Objects = @('DomainRoot', 'AdminSDHolder'); Rights = @('*'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns, AdminSDHolder appendix C' }
            @{ Rid = 519; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Name = 'Enterprise Admins'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties'); Origin = 'Learn: c-grouppolicycontainer' }
            @{ Rid = 519; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Name = 'Enterprise Admins'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Origin = 'tool default (C9), not a Microsoft value' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-9'; Name = 'Enterprise Domain Controllers'; Objects = @('DomainRoot'); Rights = @('DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-5-9'; Name = 'Enterprise Domain Controllers'; Objects = @('DomainRoot'); Rights = @('DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All'); Origin = 'Learn: rodc-replicates-passwords-grant-incorrect-permissions (c-domaindns does not grant it; the two pages disagree)' }
            @{ Rid = 516; Scope = 'Domain'; Name = 'Domain Controllers'; Objects = @('DomainRoot'); Rights = @('DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All'); Origin = 'Learn: c-domaindns' }
            @{ Rid = 498; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Name = 'Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers'; Objects = @('DomainRoot'); Rights = @('DS-Replication-Get-Changes'); Origin = 'Learn: replication-error-8453, rodc-replicates-passwords-grant-incorrect-permissions' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-3-0'; Name = 'CREATOR OWNER'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('WriteDacl', 'WriteOwner', 'WriteAllProperties'); Origin = 'Learn: c-grouppolicycontainer' }
            @{ Sid = 'S-1-3-0'; Name = 'CREATOR OWNER'; Objects = @('Gpo'); Rights = @('AllExtendedRights', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All', 'DS-Replication-Get-Changes-In-Filtered-Set'); Origin = 'tool default (C9), not a Microsoft value' }
        )
        # Added by an override file only; empty in the built-in table.
        AdditionalAllowed    = @()
        # SIDs a file may never add: Everyone, Anonymous Logon, Authenticated Users, Users, Guests, and
        # Domain Users, Domain Guests and Domain Computers of any domain (RIDs 513, 514, 515). Tool rule.
        RefusedOverrideSids  = @('S-1-1-0', 'S-1-5-7', 'S-1-5-11', 'S-1-5-32-545', 'S-1-5-32-546')
        RefusedOverrideRids  = @(513, 514, 515)
    }
    # --- Privileged groups and delegation (PORT-PLAN C10; sections PrivilegedAccounts, PrivilegedHygiene
    #     and Kerberos, report sections "Privileged Accounts", "Privileged Hygiene" and "Kerberos
    #     Exposure"; AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-PRIV-001..002, ADA-OBJ-001..004, ADA-OBJ-006,
    #     ADA-INV-005 and ADA-INV-017) ---
    # Instrument: Get-ADDomain for every domain of the forest (its DN and SID, so a member DN is read
    # from the domain that holds it); per scoped domain, a Get-ADObject search by objectSid for each
    # group of the table below, Get-ADObject -Identity for every member DN (nested groups followed,
    # security and distribution alike), a (primaryGroupID=<RID>) search for every group with an
    # account-domain SID, one (adminCount=1) search, one delegation search and the two Kerberos roast
    # reads (Get-ADUser, unchanged); once per run, the nTDSDSA and server objects (serverReference)
    # under CN=Sites of the configuration partition, and dSHeuristics. Nothing is written: no Set-AD*,
    # no Add-ADGroupMember, no Set-ADAccountControl, and no rootDSE modify (RunProtectAdminGroupsTask,
    # which Appendix C documents, is a write and is never sent). Every URL below was read on 2026-11-16.
    #
    # Vendor facts:
    #   The protected accounts and groups: Account Operators, Administrator, Administrators, Backup
    #   Operators, Domain Admins, Domain Controllers, Enterprise Admins, Enterprise Key Admins, Key
    #   Admins, Krbtgt, Print Operators, Read-only Domain Controllers, Replicator, Schema Admins, Server
    #   Operators; SDProp runs every 60 minutes by default on the PDC emulator:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/appendix-c--protected-accounts-and-groups-in-active-directory
    #   The security-groups reference gives each SID and RID used below, the forest-root-only scope of
    #   Schema Admins and Enterprise Admins, Protected Users (RID 525, "Protected by AdminSDHolder? No"),
    #   and marks Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers (<root>-498) as protected - which Appendix C
    #   does not list. THE TWO PAGES DISAGREE: -498 is treated as protected, which can only keep an
    #   object from being called an orphan, never make one. The security-identifiers page gives Key
    #   Admins as a root-domain SID and the security-groups page as <domain>: -526 is looked up by RID
    #   in every scoped domain, and one that is absent is "not present", not a failure. Enterprise Key
    #   Admins (-527): the security-groups page gives <domain>-527, but the domain-wide updates page
    #   (Operation 85) names "rootdomain\Enterprise Key Admins" and says that in nonroot domains the
    #   update "resulted in a bogus domain-relative ACE with a nonresolvable -527 SID" (removed again by
    #   Operation 87), so -527 is read from the forest root only (Scope ForestRoot), like -518 and -519,
    #   and counts as a protected source for every child domain. The same page: "The Enterprise Key
    #   Admins and Key Admins groups are only created after a Windows Server 2016 Domain Controller is
    #   promoted and takes over the PDC Emulator FSMO role" - why both are Always $false.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-groups
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-identifiers
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/deploy/domain-wide-updates
    #   "When an account is flagged as a protected account, the value of the adminCount attribute on
    #   the object is set to 1"; the domain's Administrator (RID 500) is a protected account (Appendix
    #   C). So an (adminCount=1) search that does not return the domain's RID-500 Administrator did not
    #   return its declared population, and nothing is passed on it (the same page as below).
    #   "When an account is removed from a protected group, it is no longer considered a protected
    #   account, but its adminCount attribute remains set to 1 if it is not manually changed"; the object
    #   "still does not inherit permissions"; protection follows "direct or transitive membership ...
    #   regardless of whether the membership is derived from security or distribution groups"; Schema
    #   Admins "is intended to be populated only temporarily and occasionally". Learn says "formerly
    #   protected" and never "orphan": that word is this tool's label, an INFERENCE.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/reducing-the-active-directory-attack-surface
    #   Enterprise Admins "should contain no users on a day-to-day basis, with the possible exception of
    #   the root domain's Administrator account":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/appendix-e--securing-enterprise-admins-groups-in-active-directory
    #   userAccountControl: ACCOUNTDISABLE 2, SERVER_TRUST_ACCOUNT 8192, TRUSTED_FOR_DELEGATION 524288,
    #   NOT_DELEGATED 1048576, TRUSTED_TO_AUTH_FOR_DELEGATION 16777216, PARTIAL_SECRETS_ACCOUNT 67108864:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-useraccountcontrol
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/useraccountcontrol-manipulate-account-properties
    #   A writable DC carries SERVER_TRUST_ACCOUNT and TRUSTED_FOR_DELEGATION; an RODC is typically
    #   0x5001000, which holds TRUSTED_TO_AUTH_FOR_DELEGATION - why every registered DC is excluded from
    #   both bit rows, and why the one delegation search, which asks for either bit, is expected to
    #   return every DC registered for the domain (a TOOL RULE built on those defaults: a registered DC
    #   the search did not return makes a "none" answer Not Assessed, never Pass):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/replication-error-8453
    #   msDS-AllowedToDelegateTo holds the SPNs of constrained delegation; "use any authentication
    #   protocol" adds TRUSTED_TO_AUTH_FOR_DELEGATION; msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity is a
    #   String(NT-Sec-Desc) holding the principals allowed (RBCD); the KDC always allows protocol
    #   transition for RBCD:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msds-allowedtodelegateto
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msds-allowedtoactonbehalfofotheridentity
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/configure-kerberos-delegation-group-managed-service-accounts
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-constrained-delegation-overview
    #   "Account is sensitive and cannot be delegated" is a best practice for all sensitive accounts; a
    #   Protected Users member "might not have this setting configured"; Protected Users members cannot be
    #   delegated, and service and computer accounts must never be added. That delegation restriction is
    #   DC-side: "If the domain functional level is Windows Server 2012 R2, members of the group can no
    #   longer ... Be delegated by using unconstrained or constrained delegation", and "To provide domain
    #   controller-side restrictions for Protected Users ... the domain functional level must be Windows
    #   Server 2012 R2" (how-to-configure-protected-accounts); the functional-levels page lists it under
    #   the 2012 R2 DFL. So Protected Users membership counts as delegation protection only when Get-ADDomain
    #   reported a DomainMode in ProtectedUsersDelegationModes; a lower mode makes the member delegable,
    #   and a mode that was not read or is not in either list leaves it undecided. The DomainMode values
    #   are those Set-ADDomainMode accepts:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/active-directory-functional-levels
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/set-addomainmode
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-default-user-accounts
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/how-to-configure-protected-accounts
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/security/credentials-protection-and-management/protected-users-security-group
    #   Defender for Identity: "Ensure privileged accounts are not delegated", "Unsecure Kerberos
    #   delegation" (non-domain-controller entities), "Remove a Service Principal Name" (shown for
    #   sensitive accounts); default primary group IDs 516 (DCs) and 521 (RODCs); no severity:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-for-identity/security-posture-assessments/accounts
    #   serverReference, on a server object, "contains the distinguished name of the domain controller in
    #   the domain naming context"; nTDSDSARO (an RODC's) is a subclass of nTDSDSA, so (objectClass=nTDSDSA)
    #   is taken to match it (an inference from the class page):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-serverreference
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-ntdsdsaro
    #   dSHeuristics, on CN=Directory Service,CN=Windows NT,CN=Services,<configuration NC>, does not exist
    #   by default and every unset character is "0"; character 16 is dwAdminSDExMask, the groups taken
    #   out of AdminSDHolder protection:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/e5899be4-862e-496f-9a38-33950617d2c5
    #   A SID in a filter is its encoded byte string, one \XX per byte; a bitwise rule takes a decimal:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/how-to-specify-comparison-values
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adsi/search-filter-syntax
    #   A DC returns no more values of one attribute than MaxValRange without range retrieval:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/domain-controller-returns-500-values-ldap-response
    #   Get-ADObject takes -ResultSetSize (Int32) in its LdapFilter set; "ResultSetSize controls the
    #   maximum number of returned objects" - how a primary-group search is held to what is left of
    #   MaxExpandedObjects:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-adobject
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/about/about_activedirectory_filter
    #
    # NOT PUBLISHED ON MICROSOFT LEARN, and labelled where a row relies on it:
    #   - Any grade or severity for these findings. Every *Status below is a TOOL DEFAULT: the
    #     operator-approved AdAssessment26 catalogue rules, and for the delegable-account row a C10
    #     addition. Severity is the catalogue's proposed value.
    #   - Whether Get-ADObject -Properties member does range retrieval past MaxValRange. A member list of
    #     exactly 1500 or 5000 values (MemberRangeLimits, a tool rule) makes that group Not Assessed.
    #   - What the module returns for a requested attribute that has no value, and the .NET type of
    #     msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity. Both are read defensively.
    #   - Which bit of dwAdminSDExMask removes which operator group (Learn points to KB 817433, off
    #     Learn). When the character is not 0, or dSHeuristics was not read, an adminCount=1 object
    #     protected only through Account, Server, Print or Backup Operators is not judged.
    #   - NameListCap and MaxExpandedObjects (a cap on the objects read per run, member reads and
    #     primary-group search results together) are tool defaults.
    #   - That a domain's naming context is named by the trailing DC= components of a DN: used to tell
    #     which domain a registered DC belongs to (an inference, as in Resolve-AdfaPrivDnDomain).
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "Privileged": the eight *Status
    # keys (Warning or Fail), EnterpriseAdminsExemptRootAdministrator (true or false), NameListCap (0 to
    # 1000) and MaxExpandedObjects (1 to 100000). Nothing else - not the groups, the bits, the filters,
    # the property lists or the range limits.
    Privileged              = @{
        SourceUrl            = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/appendix-c--protected-accounts-and-groups-in-active-directory and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-security-groups and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-useraccountcontrol and https://learn.microsoft.com/defender-for-identity/security-posture-assessments/accounts'
        ReadDate             = '2026-11-16'
        ThresholdSource      = 'tool default (AdAssessment26 catalogue ADA-PRIV-001..002, ADA-OBJ-001..004 and ADA-OBJ-006, operator-approved 2026-09-24; the delegable-account rule is a C10 addition), not a Microsoft value'
        # Tool defaults, all eight.
        SchemaAdminsStatus       = 'Warning'
        EnterpriseAdminsStatus   = 'Warning'
        OrphanStatus             = 'Warning'
        PrivilegedSpnStatus      = 'Warning'
        DelegableStatus          = 'Warning'
        UnconstrainedStatus      = 'Warning'
        ProtocolTransitionStatus = 'Warning'
        RbcdStatus               = 'Warning'
        # The catalogue counts the forest root's Administrator; Learn names it as a possible exception.
        EnterpriseAdminsExemptRootAdministrator = $false
        # Tool defaults.
        NameListCap          = 25
        MaxExpandedObjects   = 5000
        # Tool rule: a member list of exactly one of these sizes may be cut at MaxValRange.
        MemberRangeLimits    = @(1500, 5000)
        # What is asked, and of what. Fixed: a file cannot change them.
        ObjectProperties     = @('objectClass', 'objectSid', 'sAMAccountName', 'userAccountControl', 'adminCount', 'servicePrincipalName', 'primaryGroupID', 'member')
        DelegationProperties = @('objectClass', 'objectSid', 'sAMAccountName', 'userAccountControl', 'primaryGroupID', 'msDS-AllowedToDelegateTo', 'msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity')
        AdminCountFilter     = '(adminCount=1)'
        SitesRdn             = 'CN=Sites'
        NtdsDsaFilter        = '(objectClass=nTDSDSA)'
        DsaProperties        = @('objectClass')
        ServerFilter         = '(objectClass=server)'
        ServerProperties     = @('serverReference')
        DirectoryServiceRdn  = 'CN=Directory Service,CN=Windows NT,CN=Services'
        HeuristicsProperties = @('dSHeuristics')
        AdminSdExMaskPosition = 16
        # userAccountControl bits (Learn, read 2026-11-16).
        Uac                  = @{ AccountDisable = 2; ServerTrust = 8192; TrustedForDelegation = 524288; NotDelegated = 1048576; TrustedToAuthForDelegation = 16777216; PartialSecrets = 67108864 }
        # Defender for Identity's default primary group IDs of DCs and RODCs. Reported, never used to exclude.
        DcPrimaryGroupIds    = @(516, 521)
        UserClasses          = @('user', 'inetOrgPerson')
        ComputerClasses      = @('computer', 'msDS-GroupManagedServiceAccount', 'msDS-ManagedServiceAccount')
        GroupClasses         = @('group')
        ForeignClasses       = @('foreignSecurityPrincipal')
        ProtectedUsersRid    = 525
        # Get-ADDomain DomainMode values (Set-ADDomainMode's accepted values, Learn, read 2026-11-16):
        # at or above Windows Server 2012 R2 the DC-side Protected Users restrictions, delegation among
        # them, are in force; below it they are not. Any other value (UnknownDomain, unread, a later
        # release) leaves a Protected Users member undecided. A new release is a config edit.
        ProtectedUsersDelegationModes = @('Windows2012R2Domain', 'Windows2016Domain', 'Windows2025Domain')
        BelowProtectedUsersModes      = @('Windows2000Domain', 'Windows2003InterimDomain', 'Windows2003Domain', 'Windows2008Domain', 'Windows2008R2Domain', 'Windows2012Domain')
        # Learn's protected accounts, protected as themselves: Administrator and krbtgt.
        ProtectedAccountRids = @(500, 502)
        KrbtgtRid            = 502
        RootAdministratorRid = 500
        # Account, Server, Print and Backup Operators: the groups dwAdminSDExMask can take out of protection.
        OperatorGroupSids    = @('S-1-5-32-548', 'S-1-5-32-549', 'S-1-5-32-550', 'S-1-5-32-551')
        # The groups, by SID only. Builtin: a fixed SID in every domain. Domain: <domain SID>-RID in every
        # scoped domain. ForestRoot: <forest root SID>-RID, read in the root domain only. Protected: on
        # Appendix C (or -498, see above). Always: Learn documents it in every such domain; a group that
        # is not Always and not found is "not present". Name labels a row; no decision reads it.
        Groups               = @(
            @{ Key = 'Administrators'; Name = 'Administrators'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-544'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'DomainAdmins'; Name = 'Domain Admins'; Rid = 512; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'EnterpriseAdmins'; Name = 'Enterprise Admins'; Rid = 519; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'SchemaAdmins'; Name = 'Schema Admins'; Rid = 518; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'AccountOperators'; Name = 'Account Operators'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-548'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'ServerOperators'; Name = 'Server Operators'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-549'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'PrintOperators'; Name = 'Print Operators'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-550'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'BackupOperators'; Name = 'Backup Operators'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-551'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'Replicator'; Name = 'Replicator'; Sid = 'S-1-5-32-552'; Scope = 'Builtin'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'DomainControllers'; Name = 'Domain Controllers'; Rid = 516; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $true; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'ReadOnlyDomainControllers'; Name = 'Read-only Domain Controllers'; Rid = 521; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $true; Always = $false }
            @{ Key = 'EnterpriseReadOnlyDomainControllers'; Name = 'Enterprise Read-only Domain Controllers'; Rid = 498; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Protected = $true; Always = $false }
            @{ Key = 'KeyAdmins'; Name = 'Key Admins'; Rid = 526; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $true; Always = $false }
            @{ Key = 'EnterpriseKeyAdmins'; Name = 'Enterprise Key Admins'; Rid = 527; Scope = 'ForestRoot'; Protected = $true; Always = $false }
            @{ Key = 'CertPublishers'; Name = 'Cert Publishers'; Rid = 517; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $false; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'GroupPolicyCreatorOwners'; Name = 'Group Policy Creator Owners'; Rid = 520; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $false; Always = $true }
            @{ Key = 'ProtectedUsers'; Name = 'Protected Users'; Rid = 525; Scope = 'Domain'; Protected = $false; Always = $false }
        )
        # Catalogue ids and their PROPOSED severities (AdAssessment26 FindingCatalog.psd1), not Microsoft
        # values. The delegable-account row has no catalogue id.
        Catalogue            = @{
            SchemaAdmins       = @{ Id = 'ADA-PRIV-001'; Severity = 'Medium (proposed)' }
            EnterpriseAdmins   = @{ Id = 'ADA-PRIV-002'; Severity = 'Medium (proposed)' }
            Inventory          = @{ Id = 'ADA-INV-005'; Severity = 'Informational' }
            Orphan             = @{ Id = 'ADA-OBJ-001'; Severity = 'Low (proposed)' }
            Unconstrained      = @{ Id = 'ADA-OBJ-002'; Severity = 'High (proposed)' }
            ProtocolTransition = @{ Id = 'ADA-OBJ-003'; Severity = 'Medium (proposed)' }
            Rbcd               = @{ Id = 'ADA-OBJ-004'; Severity = 'Low (proposed)' }
            PrivilegedSpn      = @{ Id = 'ADA-OBJ-006'; Severity = 'High (proposed)' }
            Constrained        = @{ Id = 'ADA-INV-017'; Severity = 'Informational' }
            Delegable          = @{ Id = ''; Severity = 'tool default (C10 addition)' }
        }
    }
    # Directory Service events that block or mask recovery in a mixed-restore forest.
    DsEventsOfInterest      = @(
        @{ Id = 1988; Severity = 'Fail';    Meaning = 'Lingering object detected - replication BLOCKED by strict consistency.' }
        @{ Id = 2042; Severity = 'Fail';    Meaning = 'Replication stopped - tombstone lifetime exceeded; will not resume without intervention.' }
        @{ Id = 2095; Severity = 'Fail';    Meaning = 'USN rollback detected - the directory is silently diverging.' }
        @{ Id = 2103; Severity = 'Fail';    Meaning = 'AD DS database restored using an unsupported restore procedure.' }
        # VM-revert markers. 2170 is the safe path - the hypervisor supplied a new VM-Generation
        # ID, AD noticed, and reset the invocation ID and RID pool itself - but it still means a
        # snapshot or import was applied to a DC, which is not a supported way to restore one.
        # https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/virtual-dc/virtualized-domain-controller-troubleshooting
        # Read: 2026-09-21.
        @{ Id = 2170; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'VM Generation ID change detected - a snapshot, import or live migration was applied to this DC. AD recovered itself (new invocation ID, RID pool invalidated, SYSVOL resynced), but restoring a DC from a VM snapshot is not a supported procedure.' }
        @{ Id = 2181; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'A transaction was aborted because the VM was reverted to a previous state (snapshot, import or live migration).' }
        @{ Id = 2087; Severity = 'Fail';    Meaning = 'DNS lookup failure resolving a source DC GUID - direct cause of RPC 1722 replication errors.' }
        @{ Id = 2088; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'DNS lookup failed but a fallback succeeded - DNS is broken and replication is masking it.' }
        @{ Id = 1311; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'KCC could not build a replication topology.' }
        @{ Id = 1865; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'KCC could not reach one or more sites.' }
        @{ Id = 1925; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'Failed to establish a replication link.' }
        @{ Id = 1084; Severity = 'Warning'; Meaning = 'Replication failed for a specific object.' }
    )
    # NTDS-DSA options bit 0 = this DSA is a Global Catalog.
    NtdsDsaOptionIsGc       = 1
    # --- Windows Time hierarchy ------------------------------------------------------------
    # Every pattern and type name below is the vendor's own published string, kept here as data
    # so a wording change is a config edit. Sources and read dates:
    #   Type values (NoSync | NTP | NT5DS | AllSync) and the w32tm /query syntax:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings
    #   "VM IC Time Synchronization Provider" as a published Source string, and the guidance that
    #   a virtualised DC taking time from its host can leave lingering objects and stop
    #   replication (KB 976924):
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/windows-time-service-event-ids-24-29-38
    #   The forest root PDC emulator must not synchronise from the domain hierarchy (W32Time
    #   logs event ID 12 for exactly this), and should use an external authoritative source:
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/services-hub/microsoft-engage-center/health/remediation-steps-ad/configure-the-root-pdc-with-an-authoritative-time-source-and-avoid-widespread-time-skew
    #   The one place the vendor carves out the PDC from "disable host time sync":
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-server-2016-improvements#best-practices-for-accurate-timekeeping
    # Read: 2026-09-22.
    Time                    = @{
        ReadDate            = '2026-09-22'
        SourceUrl           = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings'
        HypervisorUrl       = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/windows-time-service-event-ids-24-29-38'
        RootPdcUrl          = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/services-hub/microsoft-engage-center/health/remediation-steps-ad/configure-the-root-pdc-with-an-authoritative-time-source-and-avoid-widespread-time-skew'
        # No upstream at all: the machine is running on its own oscillator.
        LocalClockPattern   = '(?i)local cmos clock|free[- ]running'
        # The hypervisor integration-service provider. Matching the provider name and the
        # registry provider key covers both the /source and the /dumpreg spellings.
        HypervisorPattern   = '(?i)vm ic time synchronization provider|vmictimeprovider'
        # "NT5DS: The time service synchronizes with the domain hierarchy" - invalid on the
        # forest root PDC emulator, which sits at the top of that hierarchy.
        DomainHierarchyType = 'NT5DS'
        # The client types that name an explicit upstream peer list.
        ExternalTypes       = @('NTP', 'AllSync')
    }
    # --- Clock offset ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 300 seconds is Microsoft's, not ours: it is the default maximum Kerberos time skew, and it
    # is published in two independent places, both read 2026-09-22:
    #   "the time skew calculated between the servers to verify it's less than 300 seconds
    #    (5 minutes) for Kerberos" - dcdiag CheckSecurityError
    #    https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag
    #   MaxAllowedPhaseOffset, default 300 for domain members
    #    https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings
    # The WARNING threshold below it is OURS, and the findings say so: 300s is the cliff where
    # authentication stops, not a target to sit near. A DC drifting past a minute is already
    # failing to keep time and will reach the cliff unattended.
    ClockSkew               = @{
        ReadDate            = '2026-09-22'
        KerberosUrl         = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag'
        StripchartUrl       = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings'
        # Vendor value. Do not lower this thinking it is a tightening - it is the documented
        # Kerberos limit and a finding quotes it as such.
        KerberosMaxSeconds  = 300
        # Ours.
        WarnSeconds         = 60
        # Samples per DC. More is steadier but every sample costs a poll interval; the default
        # /period is 2 seconds, so 3 samples is a few seconds per DC.
        Samples             = 3
        # PORT-PLAN C14 (TIME-001), read 2026-11-17: each DC's offset is also graded against the
        # forest root PDC emulator, the reference Microsoft names: "the PDC operations master at the
        # root of the forest becomes the authoritative time server for the organization" (KB 884776).
        # Kerberos needs only the relative difference between KDC and target ("It cares only that the
        # relative time difference between the KDC and target domain controller is within the maximum
        # time skew", replication error 5 article). 300 s: the Kerberos SkewTime default "5 (minutes)"
        # and the Kerberos policy default of five minutes. The offset against the PDC is the difference
        # of two w32tm /stripchart readings taken from the host running the assessment, and its error
        # bound half the sum of the two round trips: both are TOOL REASONING, not a Microsoft method.
        PdcReadDate         = '2026-11-17'
        SkewTimeUrl         = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/kerberos-protocol-registry-kdc-configuration-keys'
        RelativeSkewUrl     = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/replications-fail-with-error-5'
        RootPdcReferenceUrl = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/configure-w32ime-against-huge-time-offset'
        ThresholdSource     = 'built-in: 300 s is the Kerberos default maximum tolerance Microsoft documents; the 60 s warning level and 3 samples are tool defaults, not Microsoft values'
        # -ConfigOverridePath, key "ClockSkew": WarnSeconds (1 to 299) and Samples (1 to 20) only. The
        # Kerberos maximum is a vendor value and cannot be set from a file.
        OverrideLabel       = 'ClockSkew'
        OverrideExample     = '{ "WarnSeconds": 30 }'
        OverrideRefused     = 'KerberosMaxSeconds, the sources and the read dates cannot be set from a file.'
        OverrideSpec        = [ordered]@{
            WarnSeconds = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 299; Unit = 'seconds, below the 300 s Kerberos maximum' }
            Samples     = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 20; Unit = 'stripchart samples per DC' }
        }
    }
    # --- DC reachability classes (PORT-PLAN C14, REACH-001/002; section PortMatrix) ----------------
    # Every DC is classified ONCE per run, before the first per-DC section, and the answer is kept in
    # $script:DcReachCache and seeded into the shared port cache, so a later section reads it rather
    # than probing again. Name resolution first (Dns.BeginGetHostAddresses, bounded), then TCP to each
    # resolved ADDRESS on LDAP 389 until one answers, then WinRM 5985 and RPC 135 at that address. A
    # probe takes an address only, so "does not resolve" is never merged with "silent". No ICMP, one
    # attempt per port, no retry.
    #   SocketError names (read 2026-11-17): HostNotFound 11001, TryAgain 11002, NoRecovery 11003,
    #   NoData 11004 (name resolution); TimedOut 10060; ConnectionRefused 10061; NetworkUnreachable
    #   10051, HostDown 10064, HostUnreachable 10065.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.net.sockets.socketerror
    #   DC ports: LDAP 389, RPC endpoint mapper 135, dynamic RPC 49152-65535, SMB 445, GC 3268.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts
    #   WinRM default HTTP port 5985; "WinRM listeners can be configured on any arbitrary port", so a
    #   silent 5985 is "default management ports silent", not proof the DC cannot be managed.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/winrm/installation-and-configuration-for-windows-remote-management
    # NOT PUBLISHED: any classification of a DC as unreachable or not manageable. The classes, the
    # ports probed and the time-out are tool design (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rules REACH-001 and
    # REACH-002). ProbeTimeoutMs 3000 is a TOOL DEFAULT, not a Microsoft value. Access denied is not a
    # reachability class: the read that meets it reports it.
    # -ConfigOverridePath, key "Reach": ProbeTimeoutMs (100 to 60000), UnreachableStatus (Fail |
    # Warning) and NotManageableStatus (Warning | Info). The ports and the error names are refused.
    Reach                   = @{
        SourceUrl              = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.net.sockets.socketerror and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/winrm/installation-and-configuration-for-windows-remote-management'
        ReadDate               = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource        = 'tool defaults, not Microsoft values (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rules REACH-001 and REACH-002); Microsoft publishes no reachability classes'
        LdapPort               = 389
        WinRmPort              = 5985
        RpcPort                = 135
        ProbeTimeoutMs         = 3000
        UnreachableStatus      = 'Fail'
        NotManageableStatus    = 'Warning'
        UnresolvedSocketErrors = @('HostNotFound', 'TryAgain', 'NoRecovery', 'NoData')
        TimedOutSocketErrors   = @('TimedOut')
        RefusedSocketErrors    = @('ConnectionRefused')
        NetworkSocketErrors    = @('HostUnreachable', 'NetworkUnreachable', 'HostDown', 'NetworkDown')
        OverrideLabel          = 'Reach'
        OverrideExample        = '{ "ProbeTimeoutMs": 5000 }'
        OverrideRefused        = 'The ports, the socket error names and the sources cannot be set from a file.'
        OverrideSpec           = [ordered]@{
            ProbeTimeoutMs      = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 100; Max = 60000; Unit = 'milliseconds per probe' }
            UnreachableStatus   = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Fail', 'Warning') }
            NotManageableStatus = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Info') }
        }
    }
    # --- Subnets and clients without a site (PORT-PLAN C14, SITE-001; section Topology) ------------
    # Read 2026-11-17:
    #   siteObject, "The distinguished name for the site to which this subnet belongs", single-valued,
    #   on class subnet. The page does NOT say that an absent value means "no site": that is REASONING
    #   from "The siteObject property of the subnet object defines the name of the site that contains
    #   the client" (directory-service-functions), and every row says so.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-siteobject
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/directory-service-functions
    #   Netlogon event 5807: clients whose addresses map to no site "have been logged on this computer in
    #   the following log file '%SystemRoot%\debug\netlogon.log' and, potentially, in the log file
    #   '%SystemRoot%\debug\netlogon.bak'"; "search for lines that contain text 'NO_CLIENT_SITE:'. The
    #   first word after this string is the client name and the second word is the client IP address";
    #   the size is bounded by LogFileMaxSize, default 20000000 bytes (KB 2668820).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/dc-delayed-response-to-ldap-kerberos-request
    #   "Netlogon-related activity is logged to %windir%\debug\netlogon.log" (debug logging article).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-client/windows-security/enable-debug-logging-netlogon-service
    # NOT PUBLISHED: the full line format and its time stamp, whether the lines are written without
    # debug logging, how long they stay, and the right needed to read the file remotely. The log is read
    # over \\<dc>\ADMIN$ (administrative share), read-only, its last LogTailBytes bytes only; netlogon.bak
    # is not read. A log with no entry is Info, never Pass.
    # TOOL DEFAULTS, not Microsoft values: UnassignedStatus Warning (catalogue SITE-001), NoClientSiteStatus
    # Warning, LogTailBytes 4194304, NameListCap 20.
    # -ConfigOverridePath, key "SiteCoverage": the two statuses (Warning | Info), LogTailBytes (65536 to
    # 20000000) and NameListCap (0 to 1000). The path, the marker and the port are refused.
    SiteCoverage            = @{
        SourceUrl          = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-siteobject and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/ad/directory-service-functions and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/dc-delayed-response-to-ldap-kerberos-request'
        ReadDate           = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource    = 'tool defaults, not Microsoft values (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rule SITE-001); Microsoft publishes no grade for a subnet or a client without a site'
        UnassignedStatus   = 'Warning'
        NoClientSiteStatus = 'Warning'
        NetlogonLogPath    = '\\{0}\ADMIN$\debug\netlogon.log'
        NoClientSiteMarker = 'NO_CLIENT_SITE:'
        LogTailBytes       = 4194304
        NameListCap        = 20
        SmbPort            = 445
        OverrideLabel      = 'SiteCoverage'
        OverrideExample    = '{ "UnassignedStatus": "Info" }'
        OverrideRefused    = 'The log path, the marker, the port and the sources cannot be set from a file.'
        OverrideSpec       = [ordered]@{
            UnassignedStatus   = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Info') }
            NoClientSiteStatus = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Info') }
            LogTailBytes       = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 65536; Max = 20000000; Unit = 'bytes read from the end of netlogon.log' }
            NameListCap        = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 0; Max = 1000; Unit = 'names listed per row' }
        }
    }
    # --- Stale enabled objects (PORT-PLAN C14, STALE-001/002; section StaleObjects) -----------------
    # Read 2026-11-17:
    #   lastLogonTimestamp: "If the value is older [ current_time - msDS-LogonTimeSyncInterval ], the
    #   value is updated. The initial update after the raise of the domain functional level is calculated
    #   as 14 days minus random percentage of 5 days."
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-lastlogontimestamp
    #   "updated only when the current value in Active Directory is 9 to 14 days older than the time of
    #   logon" (KB 2679653).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/mem/configmgr/discovery/lastlogontimestamp-not-accurate
    #   msDS-LogonTimeSyncInterval, on the domain head; the schema page gives no default. "The default
    #   value is 14 and is set in code"; 0 disables the feature (AskDS archive on Learn).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msds-logontimesyncinterval
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/archive/blogs/askds/the-lastlogontimestamp-attribute-what-it-was-designed-for-and-how-it-works
    #   pwdLastSet: "If this value is set to 0 and the User-Account-Control attribute does not contain the
    #   UF_DONT_EXPIRE_PASSWD flag, then the user must set the password at the next logon." It is never
    #   called "never set" here.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-pwdlastset
    #   DONT_EXPIRE_PASSWORD (UF_DONT_EXPIRE_PASSWD) is 0x10000 (65536) in userAccountControl (KB 305144).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/useraccountcontrol-manipulate-account-properties
    #   The random part SHORTENS the wait ("9 to 14 days" under the default 14), so the value trails the
    #   real last logon by at most the interval, never the interval plus 5 days (corrected after review,
    #   2026-11-17): a -StaleDays not longer than the interval is Not Assessed.
    #   Get-ADUser / Get-ADComputer -LDAPFilter with -ResultSetSize (Int32).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/activedirectory/get-aduser
    # NOT PUBLISHED: an AD DS stale threshold. -StaleDays (90) is a TOOL DEFAULT; Defender for Identity
    # calls a user stale after 90 days for its own rule. PasswordAgeDays 365, MaxObjects 100000 and
    # NameListCap 20 are tool defaults too. Disabled accounts are not counted here (C15 inventory).
    # -ConfigOverridePath, key "Stale": StaleStatus and PasswordAgeStatus (Warning | Info), PasswordAgeDays
    # (30 to 3650), MaxObjects (1 to 1000000), NameListCap (0 to 1000). StaleDays stays the parameter;
    # the sync interval default, the filters and the property lists are refused.
    Stale                   = @{
        SourceUrl               = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-lastlogontimestamp and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/mem/configmgr/discovery/lastlogontimestamp-not-accurate and https://learn.microsoft.com/archive/blogs/askds/the-lastlogontimestamp-attribute-what-it-was-designed-for-and-how-it-works and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-pwdlastset'
        ReadDate                = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource         = 'tool defaults, not Microsoft values (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rules STALE-001 and STALE-002); Microsoft publishes no stale threshold for AD DS'
        SyncIntervalDefaultDays = 14
        SyncRandomisationDays   = 5
        StaleStatus             = 'Warning'
        PasswordAgeDays         = 365
        PasswordAgeStatus       = 'Warning'
        MaxObjects              = 100000
        NameListCap             = 20
        OverrideLabel           = 'Stale'
        OverrideExample         = '{ "StaleStatus": "Info" }'
        OverrideRefused         = 'StaleDays is the -StaleDays parameter; the sync interval default, the filters and the property lists cannot be set from a file.'
        OverrideSpec            = [ordered]@{
            StaleStatus       = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Info') }
            PasswordAgeDays   = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 30; Max = 3650; Unit = 'days' }
            PasswordAgeStatus = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Info') }
            MaxObjects        = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 1000000; Unit = 'objects per read' }
            NameListCap       = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 0; Max = 1000; Unit = 'names listed per row' }
        }
    }
    # --- SYSVOL replication engine (PORT-PLAN C14, SYSVOL-004; section Sysvol) ---------------------
    # Per DC, from the directory of its own domain: CN=SYSVOL Subscription under the DC's
    # DFSR-LocalSettings means DFSR (KB 2218556); the NTFRS subscriber "cn=domain system volume (SYSVOL
    # share),cn=NTFRS Subscriptions,CN=%DCNAME%,..." means FRS (missing SYSVOL and Netlogon shares
    # article); both means a migration in progress; neither, or a read that failed, is not known.
    # msDFSR-Flags on CN=DFSR-GlobalSettings,CN=System is quoted as corroboration only: Start 0, Prepared
    # 16, Redirected 32, Eliminated 48 (dd639789, PROVISIONAL: the research brief's read of 2026-11-17).
    # Its absence is never read as FRS: Learn does not say what it means, and a domain born at Windows
    # Server 2008 functional level uses DFSR without migration.
    #   "Starting in Windows Server 2019, promoting new domain controllers requires the DFS Replication
    #   (DFSR) to replicate the contents in the SYSVOL share" (KB 4493934).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/sysvol-dfsr-migration-fails-after-in-place-upgrade
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-missing-sysvol-and-netlogon-shares
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2008-r2-and-2008/dd639789(v=ws.10)
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msdfsr-flags
    # NOT PUBLISHED: the exception the ActiveDirectory module throws for an object that does not exist;
    # NotFoundTypePattern is the module's own type name, unmeasured against a real directory (C17).
    # TOOL DEFAULT: FrsStatus Warning (catalogue SYSVOL-004 / D20).
    # -ConfigOverridePath, key "SysvolEngine": FrsStatus (Warning | Fail) only.
    SysvolEngine            = @{
        SourceUrl           = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/sysvol-dfsr-migration-fails-after-in-place-upgrade and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-missing-sysvol-and-netlogon-shares and https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2008-r2-and-2008/dd639789(v=ws.10)'
        ReadDate            = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource     = 'tool default, not a Microsoft value (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rule SYSVOL-004)'
        FrsStatus           = 'Warning'
        DfsrSubscriptionDn  = 'CN=SYSVOL Subscription,CN=Domain System Volume,CN=DFSR-LocalSettings,{0}'
        FrsSubscriberDn     = 'CN=Domain System Volume (SYSVOL share),CN=NTFRS Subscriptions,{0}'
        GlobalSettingsDn    = 'CN=DFSR-GlobalSettings,CN=System,{0}'
        GlobalStateNames    = @{ '0' = 'Start'; '16' = 'Prepared'; '32' = 'Redirected'; '48' = 'Eliminated' }
        NotFoundTypePattern = '(?i)ADIdentityNotFoundException$'
        OverrideLabel       = 'SysvolEngine'
        OverrideExample     = '{ "FrsStatus": "Fail" }'
        OverrideRefused     = 'The DN templates, the state names and the patterns cannot be set from a file.'
        OverrideSpec        = [ordered]@{
            FrsStatus = @{ Kind = 'Status'; Allowed = @('Warning', 'Fail') }
        }
    }
    # --- Inventory depth (PORT-PLAN C15; catalogue INV-001, INV-006..INV-012, INV-015) -------------
    # Built into the existing sections Forest, Trusts, Identity, StaleObjects (which reads the Stale
    # table), Gpo, DsEvents and TimeHierarchy. Every row is Info when collected and Not Assessed with
    # its cause when not: the catalogue grades none of these rows (Kind Inventory, decision D7), so no
    # row here is Pass, Warning or Fail. Every URL below was read on 2026-11-17.
    #
    # Functional levels (INV-001): msDS-Behavior-Version on CN=Partitions,CN=Configuration (forest) and
    #   on the domain NC head (domain). 0 DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2000, 1 WIN2003_WITH_MIXED_DOMAINS, 2 WIN2003,
    #   3 WIN2008, 4 WIN2008R2, 5 WIN2012, 6 WIN2012R2, 7 WIN2016, 10 WIN2025; no 8 or 9 is defined.
    #   "The absence of the msDS-Behavior-Version attribute on a crossRefContainer object is equivalent
    #   to the msDS-Behavior-Version attribute on that object having the value zero" (and the same for
    #   a domain NC root object).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/564dc969-6db3-49b3-891a-f2f8d0a68a7f
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/d7422d35-448a-451a-8846-6a7def0044df
    # FSMO holders (INV-001): fSMORoleOwner on the domain head (PDC emulator), CN=RID Manager$,CN=System
    #   (RID master), CN=Infrastructure (infrastructure master), CN=Partitions,CN=Configuration (domain
    #   naming master) and CN=Schema,CN=Configuration (schema master); "written with the Distinguished
    #   Name (DN) of the NTDS Settings object" of the holder. A value naming a deleted NTDS Settings
    #   object ("CN=NTDS Settings DEL:") is documented as invalid.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/fsmo-transfer-seizure-process
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/error-seize-rid-master-role-with-ntdsutil
    # Runner DC (INV-001): the dnsHostName rootDSE returns to this run - the DC that answered.
    # Trusts (INV-006): the trustedDomain objects under CN=System of each scoped domain, listed
    #   attributes only. trustAttributes bits from MS-ADTS 6.1.6.7.9; a bit not in the table is kept as
    #   "unknown 0x..", never dropped. trustType 1..5 (MS-ADTS 6.1.6.7.15; 5 AAD only with the listed
    #   updates). trustDirection is a bitmap: 1 inbound, 2 outbound, 3 both, 0 "the trust is said to be
    #   disabled" (MS-LSAD). TGT delegation is derived from the MS-KILE rule: enabled by WITHIN_FOREST or
    #   CROSS_ORGANIZATION_ENABLE_TGT_DELEGATION, disabled by CROSS_ORGANIZATION_NO_TGT_DELEGATION or
    #   QUARANTINED_DOMAIN (and by a source ticket without ok-as-delegate, which a trust object cannot
    #   show). NOT PUBLISHED: that CROSS_ORGANIZATION (0x10) is "selective authentication"; the raw flag
    #   name is reported.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/e9a2d23c-c31e-4a6f-88a0-6646fdb51a3c
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/36565693-b5e4-4f37-b0a8-c1b12138e18e
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-lsad/f28f42b7-173c-4cda-9809-3fe4a5213ab3
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-kile/bac4dc69-352d-416c-a9f4-730b81ababb3
    # UPN and DNS suffixes (INV-007): uPNSuffixes on CN=Partitions (multi-valued; what an absent value
    #   means is not published, so it is reported as "attribute absent" after a read that succeeded);
    #   msDS-AllowedDNSSuffixes on the domain head; disjoint is "a primary DNS suffix that does not match
    #   the DNS name of the Active Directory domain", compared here on each DC's dNSHostName - inventory
    #   only: "Disjoint namespace can be used with caution in some scenarios".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-upnsuffixes
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-msds-alloweddnssuffixes
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/disjoint-namespace
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/event-ids-5788-5789
    # Object counts and groups (INV-008, INV-010): ACCOUNTDISABLE 0x2; groupType 0x2 global, 0x4 domain
    #   local, 0x8 universal, 0x80000000 security ("If this flag is not set, then the group is a
    #   distribution group"). MaxValRange, the most values one read of an attribute returns before
    #   range retrieval is needed, defaults to 1500: a group returning that many member values or more
    #   is "not counted exactly", never a low count. Members by primaryGroupID are not in member.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/useraccountcontrol-manipulate-account-properties
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-grouptype
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/view-set-ldap-policy-using-ntdsutil
    # GPOs (INV-011): gPLink "[<GPC DN>;<options>]" with 0x1 disabled and 0x2 enforced, on sites, domains
    #   and OUs; gPCWQLFilter "a string that contains a GUID for the filter and a WMI namespace path";
    #   WMI filters are msWMI-Som objects. The GPO flags labels are the Gpo table's (MS-GPOL, read
    #   2026-11-16). NOT PUBLISHED: the gPOptions value meaning block inheritance (reported raw), the
    #   container that holds msWMI-Som (found by class under CN=System) and the exact gPCWQLFilter
    #   format (a GUID is taken from it by pattern - a tool pattern, not a Microsoft value).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-scope
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-gpcwqlfilter
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/c-mswmi-som
    # Events (INV-012): Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable LogName, Level and StartTime with -MaxEvents
    #   always passed ("The default is to return all the events"); levels 1 Critical, 2 Error,
    #   3 Warning. The DNS Server log exists "only on DNS servers". NOT PUBLISHED: the error raised for
    #   no matching event and for a log that does not exist (reference N57); EventNoEventsPattern is
    #   the text this script's DsEvents section already reads plus an error id, EventNoLogPattern a
    #   tool pattern - both tool patterns, not Microsoft values, unmeasured against a real DC (C17). Anything else is Not Assessed, never zero events. The
    #   window is -EventLookbackDays. NOT PUBLISHED, measured by the C15 review (Windows PowerShell 5.1,
    #   a local Security log, 2026-11-17): -FilterHashtable answers an account that may not read the
    #   log with NoMatchingEventsFound, so "no events" is confirmed by one -LogName -MaxEvents 1 read,
    #   which raised UnauthorizedAccessException; unmeasured remotely against a DC (PORT-PLAN C15d).
    # GPO links across domains: "A GPO is a per-domain object that can be linked anywhere in the
    #   forest" (read 2026-11-17), so links are matched across the scoped domains and "unlinked" needs
    #   every forest domain read.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-management-console
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent?view=powershell-5.1
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/samples/creating-get-winevent-queries-with-filterhashtable
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/application-management/move-event-viewer-log-files
    # W32Time (INV-015): Type and NtpServer under SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\Parameters,
    #   AnnounceFlags (a DWORD) under ...\W32Time\Config; read with StdRegProv GetStringValue and
    #   GetDWORDValue. "There's no default value for this registry entry [NtpServer] on domain
    #   members", so NtpServer not set is the documented default. "If you use Group Policy to set the
    #   NtpServer value ... W32Time doesn't use the NtpServer registry value": the policy area is not
    #   read in this build (its exact subkeys are not stated on the page), so a row states the service
    #   registry values, which may not be the effective configuration. Not graded (reference N21).
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/configure-authoritative-time-server
    #
    # TOOL DEFAULTS, not Microsoft values: MaxObjects 100000 per read, NameListCap 20, EventMaxEvents
    # 2000 per log and DC, EventGroupCap 50 event ID groups per log and DC (the reference SPEC's
    # -EventGroupCap). -ConfigOverridePath, key "Inventory": MaxObjects (1 to 1000000), NameListCap
    # (0 to 1000), EventMaxEvents (1 to 100000), EventGroupCap (1 to 1000). Everything else - the logs,
    # the levels, the filters, the flag tables, the labels, the registry keys and the patterns - is
    # refused.
    Inventory               = @{
        SourceUrl              = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/564dc969-6db3-49b3-891a-f2f8d0a68a7f and https://learn.microsoft.com/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-adts/e9a2d23c-c31e-4a6f-88a0-6646fdb51a3c and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-grouptype and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-scope and https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent?view=powershell-5.1 and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings'
        ReadDate               = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource        = 'tool defaults, not Microsoft values (caps only; inventory rows are not graded - AdAssessment26 catalogue Kind Inventory, decision D7)'
        MaxObjects             = 100000
        NameListCap            = 20
        EventMaxEvents         = 2000
        EventGroupCap          = 50
        # Learn (view-set-ldap-policy-using-ntdsutil): MaxValRange default 1500.
        MemberCountCeiling     = 1500
        BehaviorVersionLabels  = @{ '0' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2000'; '1' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2003_WITH_MIXED_DOMAINS'; '2' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2003'; '3' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2008'; '4' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2008R2'; '5' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2012'; '6' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2012R2'; '7' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2016'; '10' = 'DS_BEHAVIOR_WIN2025' }
        TrustAttributeFlags    = @(
            @{ Bit = 0x1; Name = 'NON_TRANSITIVE' }
            @{ Bit = 0x2; Name = 'UPLEVEL_ONLY' }
            @{ Bit = 0x4; Name = 'QUARANTINED_DOMAIN' }
            @{ Bit = 0x8; Name = 'FOREST_TRANSITIVE' }
            @{ Bit = 0x10; Name = 'CROSS_ORGANIZATION' }
            @{ Bit = 0x20; Name = 'WITHIN_FOREST' }
            @{ Bit = 0x40; Name = 'TREAT_AS_EXTERNAL' }
            @{ Bit = 0x80; Name = 'USES_RC4_ENCRYPTION' }
            @{ Bit = 0x100; Name = 'TRUST_USES_AES_KEYS' }
            @{ Bit = 0x200; Name = 'CROSS_ORGANIZATION_NO_TGT_DELEGATION' }
            @{ Bit = 0x400; Name = 'PIM_TRUST' }
            @{ Bit = 0x800; Name = 'CROSS_ORGANIZATION_ENABLE_TGT_DELEGATION' }
            @{ Bit = 0x1000; Name = 'DISABLE_AUTH_TARGET_VALIDATION' }
        )
        TrustDirectionLabels   = @{ '0' = 'Disabled'; '1' = 'Inbound'; '2' = 'Outbound'; '3' = 'Bidirectional' }
        TrustTypeLabels        = @{ '1' = 'DOWNLEVEL'; '2' = 'UPLEVEL'; '3' = 'MIT'; '4' = 'DCE'; '5' = 'AAD' }
        TrustProperties        = @('cn', 'trustPartner', 'flatName', 'trustAttributes', 'trustDirection', 'trustType')
        AccountDisableBit      = 2
        GroupScopeBits         = [ordered]@{ Global = 0x2; DomainLocal = 0x4; Universal = 0x8 }
        GroupSecurityBit       = 2147483648
        CountFilters           = [ordered]@{
            Users     = '(&(objectCategory=person)(objectClass=user))'
            Computers = '(&(objectCategory=computer)(!(objectClass=msDS-GroupManagedServiceAccount))(!(objectClass=msDS-ManagedServiceAccount)))'
            Groups    = '(objectClass=group)'
            Contacts  = '(objectClass=contact)'
            OUs       = '(objectClass=organizationalUnit)'
            gMSA      = '(objectClass=msDS-GroupManagedServiceAccount)'
            sMSA      = '(objectClass=msDS-ManagedServiceAccount)'
        }
        GpoContainerProperties = @('displayName', 'flags', 'gPCWQLFilter', 'versionNumber')
        SomProperties          = @('gPLink', 'gPOptions', 'objectClass')
        WmiFilterProperties    = @('msWMI-Name')
        # Tool pattern, not published: the first curly-braced GUID in gPCWQLFilter, and the GUID of a
        # GPO's container in a gPLink entry.
        GuidPattern            = '\{[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{12}\}'
        EventLogs              = @('System', 'Directory Service', 'DFS Replication', 'DNS Server', 'File Replication Service')
        EventLevels            = @(1, 2, 3)
        EventLevelNames        = @{ '1' = 'Critical'; '2' = 'Error'; '3' = 'Warning' }
        EventNoEventsPattern   = '(?i)No events were found|NoMatchingEventsFound'
        EventNoLogPattern      = '(?i)There is not an event log|NoMatchingLogsFound'
        W32TimeValues          = @(
            @{ Id = 'Type'; Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\Parameters'; ValueName = 'Type'; Kind = 'String' }
            @{ Id = 'NtpServer'; Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\Parameters'; ValueName = 'NtpServer'; Kind = 'String' }
            @{ Id = 'AnnounceFlags'; Key = 'SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\Config'; ValueName = 'AnnounceFlags'; Kind = 'Dword' }
        )
        WsmanPort              = 5985
        RpcPort                = 135
        OverrideLabel          = 'Inventory'
        OverrideExample        = '{ "MaxObjects": 50000 }'
        OverrideRefused        = 'The logs, the levels, the filters, the property lists, the flag tables and labels, the registry keys, the patterns and the ports cannot be set from a file; the event window is -EventLookbackDays.'
        OverrideSpec           = [ordered]@{
            MaxObjects     = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 1000000; Unit = 'objects per read' }
            NameListCap    = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 0; Max = 1000; Unit = 'names listed per row' }
            EventMaxEvents = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 100000; Unit = 'events per log and DC' }
            EventGroupCap  = @{ Kind = 'Int'; Min = 1; Max = 1000; Unit = 'event ID groups per log and DC' }
        }
    }
    # --- DNS: zone dynamic update, aging and scavenging, DC locator SRV records, DC A records,
    #     records that name no DC, zone and record inventory, DC DNS client settings (PORT-PLAN C13;
    #     sections Dns, DnsAdConsistency, DomainControllers and the opt-in DnsRecordExport) ---
    # Reads only: Get-DnsServerZone, Get-DnsServerScavenging, Get-DnsServerZoneAging,
    # Get-DnsServerForwarder and Get-DnsServerResourceRecord with -ComputerName (the DnsServer module
    # of the host running the assessment), Resolve-DnsName -DnsOnly -Server <dc> as the second
    # instrument, and Win32_NetworkAdapterConfiguration through the shared CIM seam. No Set-, Add-,
    # Remove- or Start-DnsServer cmdlet, no CIM method, no ipconfig /registerdns, no nltest /dsregdns.
    # Every URL below was read on 2026-11-17.
    #
    # Dynamic update (DNS-001):
    #   "Secure only is recommended for AD-integrated zones." The three options are None, Nonsecure and
    #   secure, Secure only; "The last option is available only for AD-integrated DNS zones."
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-client/networking/troubleshooting-dns-dynamic-update-issues
    #   "After a zone becomes Active Directory-integrated, DNS servers running Windows Server default to
    #   allowing only secure dynamic updates"; without secure dynamic update "any computer on the network
    #   can modify the existing A record".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update
    #   The strings None, Secure, NonsecureAndSecure are the accepted values of -DynamicUpdate, and
    #   ReplicationScope takes Forest, Domain, Legacy, Custom, on Set-DnsServerPrimaryZone.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/set-dnsserverprimaryzone
    #   NOT PUBLISHED: the OUTPUT property names of Get-DnsServerZone (ZoneName, ZoneType, IsAutoCreated,
    #   IsDsIntegrated, IsReverseLookupZone, DynamicUpdate, ReplicationScope, SecureSecondaries) - Learn
    #   gives "Outputs: CimInstance" only. They are read by name and a property not returned is Not
    #   Assessed, never Pass. MicrosoftDNS_Zone.AllowUpdate's numeric values are not published either,
    #   so the WMI class is not used.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/get-dnsserverzone
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/dns/microsoftdns-zone
    # Aging and scavenging (DNS-002):
    #   "Aging and scavenging are two independent but complementary mechanisms. You must enable both";
    #   scavenging "skips any zone that has aging disabled"; "Enable server-level scavenging on only one
    #   DNS server per zone"; defaults 7 days.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/dns/aging-scavenging
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/dns/configure-aging-scavenging
    #   "ScavengingInterval determines whether the scavenging feature for the DNS server is enabled ...
    #   The default setting is 0, which disables scavenging"; ScavengingState is only the default for
    #   NEW zones, so it is not what this tool grades.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/set-dnsserverscavenging
    #   Zone aging: Aging, alias AgingEnabled; "relevant only for DNS primary zones".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/set-dnsserverzoneaging
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/get-dnsserverzoneaging
    #   NOT PUBLISHED: the output property names of Get-DnsServerScavenging and Get-DnsServerZoneAging
    #   (ScavengingInterval, AgingEnabled, NoRefreshInterval, RefreshInterval, ScavengeServers). Read by
    #   name; provisional until the first real run (PORT-PLAN C13a).
    # DC locator records (DNS-003, DNS-005): the Netlogon mnemonic table - Dc SRV
    #   _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.<DnsDomainName>, Kdc SRV _kerberos._tcp.dc._msdcs.<DnsDomainName>, DsaCname
    #   CNAME <DsaGuid>._msdcs.<DnsForestName>; "Read-only DCs default to registering DNS records
    #   specific to their site"; DnsAvoidRegisterRecords can suppress records legitimately.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/problems-with-dc-ad-integrated-dns-zones
    #   The graded set is the reference repository's decision 61 (Dc and Kdc); the DsaCname record is
    #   graded per DNS server by the existing DsaCname section and is not graded twice. A record that
    #   names no DC: Microsoft publishes no grade, only "Remove references to stale domain controllers".
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-dns-event-id-4013
    # Record reads: Get-DnsServerResourceRecord returns HostName, RecordType, RecordClass, TimeToLive,
    #   Timestamp, RecordData; -RRType takes A, CName, Srv, ...; -ZoneName with no -Name gets every record.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/get-dnsserverresourcerecord
    #   NOT PUBLISHED: the property names inside RecordData. The target is read from DomainName (SRV),
    #   IPv4Address (A) and HostNameAlias (CNAME) - the PARAMETER names of Add-DnsServerResourceRecord,
    #   provisional as output names.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/add-dnsserverresourcerecord
    #   NOT PUBLISHED: the error a record that does not exist raises. NoRecordPattern reads only the two
    #   Win32 codes for "no such record": DNS_ERROR_RECORD_DOES_NOT_EXIST 9701 "DNS record does not
    #   exist." and DNS_ERROR_NAME_DOES_NOT_EXIST 9714 "DNS name does not exist.", by number or by that
    #   exact text, in the message or the error id. Anything else - a bare "was not found" or "does not
    #   exist" (WIN32 53 "The network path was not found.", 1355 "The specified domain either does not
    #   exist ..."), a CIM NotFound code (it does not say what was not found) - is a failed read, never
    #   an absence.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/debug/system-error-codes--9000-11999-
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/microsoft.management.infrastructure.nativeerrorcode
    #   Zones whose records the server read is allowed to judge: "A stub zone only contains information
    #   about the authoritative name servers for the zone"; a primary zone is "the primary source", a
    #   secondary zone "a read-only copy of a primary zone". A name in a stub zone, a conditional
    #   forwarder zone ('Forwarder', provisional), or below a delegation (an NS record below the apex of
    #   the zone read) is not held by that server's zone, so that server read is Unread, never Absent.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/dns/zone-types
    #   ReplicationScope: "Domain. The domain directory partition. Forest. The ForestDnsZone directory
    #   partition. Legacy. A legacy directory partition. Custom." Only a Domain-scope zone is known to be
    #   hosted by the DCs of one domain; a Forest or Custom zone is graded once over every DNS server of
    #   the run; any other scope (Legacy, none, not returned) leaves the hosting set unknown, so "no
    #   hosting server scavenges" is not asserted for it.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dnsserver/add-dnsserverprimaryzone
    # DC DNS client (INV-016): Win32_NetworkAdapterConfiguration, IPEnabled, IPAddress (IPv4 and IPv6),
    #   DNSServerSearchOrder "Array of server IP addresses to be used in querying for DNS servers". The
    #   class has methods that change the adapter; none is called.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/cimwin32prov/win32-networkadapterconfiguration
    #   "Configuring domain controllers to point to a single DNS server's IP address, including the
    #   127.0.0.1 loopback address, represents a single point of failure. This setting is tolerable in
    #   a forest with only one domain controller" (KB 4013 article above). "Don't configure the DNS
    #   client settings on the domain controllers to point to DNS servers of your Internet Service
    #   Provider (ISP)"; a DC may use itself as preferred server once replication is verified.
    #     https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/best-practices-for-dns-client-settings
    #   NOT PUBLISHED: "loopback must not be first". Reported as information only.
    #
    # TOOL DEFAULTS, not Microsoft values (Microsoft publishes no grade for any of them; the defaults are
    # the AdAssessment26 catalogue's rules ADA-DNS-001..005): NonsecureStatus Fail, ScavengingOffStatus
    # Warning, AgingOffStatus Warning, ARecordStatus Warning, StaleRecordStatus Warning,
    # OneInstrumentStatus Warning (a missing record seen by only one of the two instruments),
    # SingleServerStatus Warning, RecordExportCap 50000 per zone (the reference's -DnsRecordCap).
    #
    # Replaceable from a file via -ConfigOverridePath, under the key "Dns": the seven statuses, each
    # within its own allowed set, and RecordExportCap (1 to 1000000). Nothing else - not the record
    # names, the patterns, the zone types, the ports or the sources.
    Dns                     = @{
        SourceUrl              = 'https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-client/networking/troubleshooting-dns-dynamic-update-issues and https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/networking/dns/aging-scavenging and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/problems-with-dc-ad-integrated-dns-zones and https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-dns-event-id-4013'
        ReadDate               = '2026-11-17'
        ThresholdSource        = 'tool defaults, not Microsoft values (the AdAssessment26 catalogue rules ADA-DNS-001..005); Microsoft publishes no grade for these settings'
        DynamicUpdateValues    = @('None', 'Secure', 'NonsecureAndSecure')
        NonsecureDynamicUpdate = 'NonsecureAndSecure'
        # Zone types whose dynamic update and aging are graded. Aging "is relevant only for DNS primary
        # zones"; an AD-integrated zone reports ZoneType Primary (provisional, not published).
        GradedZoneTypes        = @('Primary')
        NonsecureStatus        = 'Fail'
        ScavengingOffStatus    = 'Warning'
        AgingOffStatus         = 'Warning'
        ARecordStatus          = 'Warning'
        StaleRecordStatus      = 'Warning'
        OneInstrumentStatus    = 'Warning'
        SingleServerStatus     = 'Warning'
        RecordExportCap        = 50000
        LocatorRecords         = @(
            @{ Mnemonic = 'Dc'; Name = '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.{0}' }
            @{ Mnemonic = 'Kdc'; Name = '_kerberos._tcp.dc._msdcs.{0}' }
        )
        WsmanPort              = 5985
        RpcPort                = 135
        # A record that does not exist: only the Win32 codes 9701 and 9714 or their exact text (see
        # above). Anything else is a failed read.
        NoRecordPattern        = '(?i)\bWIN32 (9701|9714)\b|\bDNS (record|name) does not exist\b'
        # Zone types whose records a server read may judge (see above); any other type, or none
        # returned, makes that server read Unread for a name in the zone.
        RecordZoneTypes        = @('Primary', 'Secondary')
        # The replication scopes whose hosting servers this tool can name: Domain (the DCs of one
        # domain that run DNS) and the forest-wide ones, pooled over every DNS server of the run.
        DomainScopes           = @('Domain')
        ForestScopes           = @('Forest', 'Custom')
        TimeoutPattern         = '(?i)timed out|timeout'
        # The DNS CIM provider is not there (reference decision 60): InvalidNamespace (3), InvalidClass (5).
        NoProviderErrorCodes   = @('InvalidNamespace', 'InvalidClass')
        LoopbackAddresses      = @('127.0.0.1', '::1')
    }
    # The forest-wide locator zone. Held as data because it is used to build a zone name, and a
    # typo there would produce a confident false negative.
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/integrating-ad-ds-into-an-existing-dns-infrastructure
    #   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-configure-dns
    # Read: 2026-09-22.
    MsdcsZoneLabel          = '_msdcs'
}

# Status vocabulary (fail-closed).
$script:Status = @{
    Pass        = 'Pass'
    Warning     = 'Warning'
    Fail        = 'Fail'
    NotAssessed = 'Not Assessed'
    Info        = 'Info'
}

# ===========================================================================
